-- The site is an editorial blog now, not an agency directory. This changes only the sentences,
-- links and tables in the guides that still described the directory (its listings, profiles,
-- "request proposals", agency price tables taken from it), links a few named sources to their
-- official pages, and replaces the author bio. Each guide is skipped if it was edited by hand
-- since (its original sentence is gone). Report: the commit that adds this file.

begin;

-- youtube-ads-agency-cost
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A YouTube ads agency usually costs $1,000 to $5,000 a month for a small or mid-sized business. That is the agency's fee only. The money you spend on the ads themselves is a separate bill paid to Google."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In our October 2026 review of publicly listed agency prices, most YouTube ads agencies started between $1,000 and $3,000. Agencies that bill as a share of your budget usually take 10% to 20% of monthly ad spend, and some YouTube specialists quote 15% to 25%."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bigger brands that want strategy, ad production and testing in one package often pay $5,000 to $12,000 or more a month on top of ad spend."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your own number depends on three things: how the agency charges, how much you plan to spend on ads, and whether you need them to make the videos. Each one is covered below, followed by three sample budgets you can copy."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"You pay two bills: the agency and Google"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Every YouTube ads budget splits into two parts. The first is the agency's fee for strategy, setup, targeting, testing and reporting. The second is your ad spend, which goes straight to Google Ads, usually charged to your own card or invoice."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is where most quotes get confusing. One agency says \"$1,500 a month\" and means the fee only. Another says \"$5,000 a month\" and means fee plus ad spend combined. Before you compare two quotes, ask each agency to split them."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A few agencies buy the media for you and bill you one total. That can work, but ask to see the Google Ads invoices. Otherwise you can't tell how much of your money reached YouTube."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The four ways YouTube ads agencies charge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most agencies use one of four pricing models. Flat monthly retainers are by far the most common. In a "},{"type":"text","text":"Credo survey","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.getcredo.com/guide/digital-marketing-industry-pricing-survey/ppc-pricing/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" of 146 firms that run paid ads, about 83% billed a flat retainer and about 13% billed only a percentage of spend."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Model"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How it works"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical range"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best for"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Flat monthly retainer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Same fee every month, whatever you spend"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $5,000 a month for most small and mid-sized accounts"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Steady budgets, businesses that want a predictable bill"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Percentage of ad spend"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fee is a share of what you spend that month"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10% to 20% of spend, often with a minimum of about $500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accounts spending $10,000+ a month that plan to scale"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hybrid"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A base fee plus a smaller percentage"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Example: $1,000 base plus 5% of spend"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Budgets that change a lot month to month"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per project or per video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One price for a campaign launch or each ad video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $3,000 per project; $1,500 to $10,000 per produced video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One-off launches, testing an agency before a retainer"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ranges come from "},{"type":"text","text":"Clicksgeek","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://clicksgeek.com/?p=17034"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"MarketerHire","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and our October 2026 review of agency prices."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Performance pricing exists too, where the agency is paid per lead or per sale. It is rare for YouTube because video ads often drive sales that show up later through search or direct visits, which makes the credit hard to agree on."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Flat fee or percentage: the break-even point"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To compare a flat quote with a percentage quote, divide the flat fee by the percentage:"}]},{"type":"codeBlock","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\\text{Break-even ad spend} = \\frac{\\text{flat monthly fee}}{\\text{percentage rate}}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Say Agency A quotes $1,500 a month and Agency B quotes 15% of spend. $1,500 divided by 0.15 is $10,000. Below $10,000 a month in ad spend, Agency B costs less. Above it, Agency A does."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep one thing in mind with percentage pricing: the agency earns more when you spend more, whether or not the extra spend is profitable. That doesn't make it a bad model, but it means you should agree on a target cost per lead or return on ad spend before you raise the budget."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Starting prices are a floor. Your quote will rise with ad spend, the number of ad videos you need, and how many campaigns run at once. For a wider sample, "},{"type":"text","text":"Clutch","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://clutch.co/agencies/ppc/youtube"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" reports minimum project sizes from $1,000 to over $50,000 across thousands of YouTube advertising firms, with $100 to $149 an hour as the most common rate."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What the ads themselves cost"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads are usually cheap per view. Across industries, the average cost per view is about $0.026 and the average cost per 1,000 impressions is about $3.53, according to Mega Digital's benchmark data. Competitive niches cost more: healthcare and insurance had the highest average view cost in the same data, at $0.071."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad format"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How you pay"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical cost"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Skippable in-stream"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per view (30 seconds watched, the full ad if shorter, or a click)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$0.02 to $0.30 per view"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Non-skippable in-stream"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per 1,000 impressions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$6 to $10 CPM"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Bumper (6 seconds)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per 1,000 impressions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10 to $20 CPM"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In-feed video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per view (thumbnail click)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $0.10 per view"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per 1,000 impressions"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $4 CPM"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Masthead (top of homepage)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reserved through Google sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$300,000 to $1M+"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sources: "},{"type":"text","text":"Mega Digital cost guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ads-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"JoinBrands","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://joinbrands.com/blog/how-much-do-youtube-ads-cost/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Top Growth Marketing","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://topgrowthmarketing.com/youtube-ads-cost/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What most small businesses actually spend is modest. In a "},{"type":"text","text":"WebFX survey","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.webfx.com/social-media/pricing/how-much-does-youtube-advertising-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" of 270 marketers, 53% of businesses spent between $101 and $1,000 a month on YouTube ads."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A cheap view isn't automatically a good one. Work backward from what a customer is worth. If you can afford to pay $40 to win a customer and 0.5% of viewers convert, you can pay up to $0.20 a view and still make your target."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three sample monthly budgets"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These are worked examples built from the ranges above, not quotes. Use them to sanity-check what an agency sends you."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scenario"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad spend"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agency fee"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Total per month"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Local business testing YouTube (clinic, law firm, trades)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,500"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 flat retainer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Uses one existing video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Growing e-commerce or SaaS brand"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,500 (15% of spend)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2 new ads at about $1,000 each"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$13,500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Established brand running several campaigns"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$50,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$4,500 (hybrid: $2,000 base plus 5%)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $5,000 of new creative"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$59,500"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the first scenario, the agency fee is two-thirds of the ad spend. That ratio is normal for small tests, but it means you're mostly paying for setup and learning. Plan to run it for at least three months before you judge results."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In the third, the fee is under 10% of the total. Past a certain size, the cost that matters most is ad spend that isn't working, so the agency's testing process matters more than its fee."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Extra costs to ask about"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The monthly fee rarely covers everything. Ask each agency whether these are included:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Setup or onboarding fees in the first month, for building campaigns, audiences and conversion tracking."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video production. Agencies that make ads charge anywhere from about $1,000 per video to $10,000 for a fully produced spot."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"New ad versions. Ads wear out as the same people see them again. Ask how many new versions per month the fee includes."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Revisions beyond the agreed rounds on each video."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stock footage and music licences, which can add a few hundred dollars a month."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tracking and reporting tools, if the agency passes software costs on to you."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Early exit fees on longer contracts."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If a quote doesn't mention production at all, assume you will need to supply the videos yourself."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When an agency is worth it, and when to run ads yourself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An agency usually pays for itself when at least two of these are true:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can spend $1,500 or more a month on ads for at least three months."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You don't have ad videos yet, or the ones you have were made for organic posting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nobody on your team has set up conversion tracking in Google Ads before."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You sell something with a clear value per customer, so results can be measured in leads or sales."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If your budget is a few hundred dollars a month, an agency fee will likely cost more than the ads. You'll learn more by running a small campaign yourself in Google Ads, or by paying a freelancer for a one-time setup. MarketerHire makes a similar point: an agency that takes on an account spending around $1,500 a month is mostly selling its time, because YouTube campaigns need enough volume to learn."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One rule applies at any budget: the Google Ads account should be in your name. If you part ways, you keep the history, audiences and conversion data."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to compare agency quotes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Get at least three quotes, then put them side by side using the same questions:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is this price the fee only, or fee plus ad spend?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which pricing model is it, and what happens to the fee if my ad budget doubles?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many new ad versions do you test each month?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is video production included? If not, what does one ad video cost?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who owns the Google Ads account and the YouTube channel?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the minimum contract, and what does it cost to leave early?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which one or two numbers will you report on? Cost per lead and return on ad spend are more useful than views."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can you show results from a business like mine, with real numbers and links?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run the break-even formula from earlier on any percentage quote, then look at the total for your planned budget, not the headline fee."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much should a small business spend on YouTube ads?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Many small businesses start between $100 and $1,000 a month; 53% of businesses in a WebFX survey spent in that range. If you're hiring an agency, budget at least $1,500 a month in ad spend so the campaign has enough data to improve."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is the ad spend included in the agency's fee?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually not. Most agencies charge their fee separately, and you pay Google directly for the ads. Always ask, because some quotes combine both."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is a percentage-of-spend fee a bad deal?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not by itself. It's often cheaper than a flat fee at low budgets and more expensive at high ones. Use the break-even formula to see where your budget falls."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is there a minimum ad spend on YouTube?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Google doesn't set a minimum. Agencies often do, because small budgets don't give them enough data to test and improve."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long before YouTube ads show results?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Expect the first month to go to setup and early testing. Most agencies and advertisers judge results after about three months of steady spend."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do YouTube ads agencies make the videos?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some do and some don't. Ask before you sign, since production can cost more than management."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>A YouTube ads agency usually costs $1,000 to $5,000 a month for a small or mid-sized business. That is the agency's fee only. The money you spend on the ads themselves is a separate bill paid to Google.</p><p>In our October 2026 review of publicly listed agency prices, most YouTube ads agencies started between $1,000 and $3,000. Agencies that bill as a share of your budget usually take 10% to 20% of monthly ad spend, and some YouTube specialists quote 15% to 25%.</p><p>Bigger brands that want strategy, ad production and testing in one package often pay $5,000 to $12,000 or more a month on top of ad spend.</p><p>Your own number depends on three things: how the agency charges, how much you plan to spend on ads, and whether you need them to make the videos. Each one is covered below, followed by three sample budgets you can copy.</p><h2>You pay two bills: the agency and Google</h2><p>Every YouTube ads budget splits into two parts. The first is the agency's fee for strategy, setup, targeting, testing and reporting. The second is your ad spend, which goes straight to Google Ads, usually charged to your own card or invoice.</p><p>This is where most quotes get confusing. One agency says "$1,500 a month" and means the fee only. Another says "$5,000 a month" and means fee plus ad spend combined. Before you compare two quotes, ask each agency to split them.</p><p>A few agencies buy the media for you and bill you one total. That can work, but ask to see the Google Ads invoices. Otherwise you can't tell how much of your money reached YouTube.</p><h2>The four ways YouTube ads agencies charge</h2><p>Most agencies use one of four pricing models. Flat monthly retainers are by far the most common. In a <a href="https://www.getcredo.com/guide/digital-marketing-industry-pricing-survey/ppc-pricing/">Credo survey</a> of 146 firms that run paid ads, about 83% billed a flat retainer and about 13% billed only a percentage of spend.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Best for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flat monthly retainer</td><td>Same fee every month, whatever you spend</td><td>$1,000 to $5,000 a month for most small and mid-sized accounts</td><td>Steady budgets, businesses that want a predictable bill</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage of ad spend</td><td>Fee is a share of what you spend that month</td><td>10% to 20% of spend, often with a minimum of about $500</td><td>Accounts spending $10,000+ a month that plan to scale</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid</td><td>A base fee plus a smaller percentage</td><td>Example: $1,000 base plus 5% of spend</td><td>Budgets that change a lot month to month</td></tr><tr><td>Per project or per video</td><td>One price for a campaign launch or each ad video</td><td>$1,000 to $3,000 per project; $1,500 to $10,000 per produced video</td><td>One-off launches, testing an agency before a retainer</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ranges come from <a href="https://clicksgeek.com/?p=17034">Clicksgeek</a>, <a href="https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency">MarketerHire</a> and our October 2026 review of agency prices.</p><p>Performance pricing exists too, where the agency is paid per lead or per sale. It is rare for YouTube because video ads often drive sales that show up later through search or direct visits, which makes the credit hard to agree on.</p><h3>Flat fee or percentage: the break-even point</h3><p>To compare a flat quote with a percentage quote, divide the flat fee by the percentage:</p><pre><code>\text{Break-even ad spend} = \frac{\text{flat monthly fee}}{\text{percentage rate}}</code></pre><p>Say Agency A quotes $1,500 a month and Agency B quotes 15% of spend. $1,500 divided by 0.15 is $10,000. Below $10,000 a month in ad spend, Agency B costs less. Above it, Agency A does.</p><p>Keep one thing in mind with percentage pricing: the agency earns more when you spend more, whether or not the extra spend is profitable. That doesn't make it a bad model, but it means you should agree on a target cost per lead or return on ad spend before you raise the budget.</p><p>Starting prices are a floor. Your quote will rise with ad spend, the number of ad videos you need, and how many campaigns run at once. For a wider sample, <a href="https://clutch.co/agencies/ppc/youtube">Clutch</a> reports minimum project sizes from $1,000 to over $50,000 across thousands of YouTube advertising firms, with $100 to $149 an hour as the most common rate.</p><h2>What the ads themselves cost</h2><p>YouTube ads are usually cheap per view. Across industries, the average cost per view is about $0.026 and the average cost per 1,000 impressions is about $3.53, according to Mega Digital's benchmark data. Competitive niches cost more: healthcare and insurance had the highest average view cost in the same data, at $0.071.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Ad format</th><th>How you pay</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Skippable in-stream</td><td>Per view (30 seconds watched, the full ad if shorter, or a click)</td><td>$0.02 to $0.30 per view</td></tr><tr><td>Non-skippable in-stream</td><td>Per 1,000 impressions</td><td>$6 to $10 CPM</td></tr><tr><td>Bumper (6 seconds)</td><td>Per 1,000 impressions</td><td>$10 to $20 CPM</td></tr><tr><td>In-feed video</td><td>Per view (thumbnail click)</td><td>About $0.10 per view</td></tr><tr><td>Shorts</td><td>Per 1,000 impressions</td><td>About $4 CPM</td></tr><tr><td>Masthead (top of homepage)</td><td>Reserved through Google sales</td><td>$300,000 to $1M+</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Sources: <a href="https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ads-cost">Mega Digital cost guide</a>, <a href="https://joinbrands.com/blog/how-much-do-youtube-ads-cost/">JoinBrands</a>, <a href="https://topgrowthmarketing.com/youtube-ads-cost/">Top Growth Marketing</a>.</p><p>What most small businesses actually spend is modest. In a <a href="https://www.webfx.com/social-media/pricing/how-much-does-youtube-advertising-cost">WebFX survey</a> of 270 marketers, 53% of businesses spent between $101 and $1,000 a month on YouTube ads.</p><p>A cheap view isn't automatically a good one. Work backward from what a customer is worth. If you can afford to pay $40 to win a customer and 0.5% of viewers convert, you can pay up to $0.20 a view and still make your target.</p><h2>Three sample monthly budgets</h2><p>These are worked examples built from the ranges above, not quotes. Use them to sanity-check what an agency sends you.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Ad spend</th><th>Agency fee</th><th>Ad videos</th><th>Total per month</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Local business testing YouTube (clinic, law firm, trades)</td><td>$1,500</td><td>$1,000 flat retainer</td><td>Uses one existing video</td><td>$2,500</td></tr><tr><td>Growing e-commerce or SaaS brand</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$1,500 (15% of spend)</td><td>2 new ads at about $1,000 each</td><td>$13,500</td></tr><tr><td>Established brand running several campaigns</td><td>$50,000</td><td>$4,500 (hybrid: $2,000 base plus 5%)</td><td>About $5,000 of new creative</td><td>$59,500</td></tr></tbody></table><p>In the first scenario, the agency fee is two-thirds of the ad spend. That ratio is normal for small tests, but it means you're mostly paying for setup and learning. Plan to run it for at least three months before you judge results.</p><p>In the third, the fee is under 10% of the total. Past a certain size, the cost that matters most is ad spend that isn't working, so the agency's testing process matters more than its fee.</p><h2>Extra costs to ask about</h2><p>The monthly fee rarely covers everything. Ask each agency whether these are included:</p><ul><li><p>Setup or onboarding fees in the first month, for building campaigns, audiences and conversion tracking.</p></li><li><p>Video production. Agencies that make ads charge anywhere from about $1,000 per video to $10,000 for a fully produced spot.</p></li><li><p>New ad versions. Ads wear out as the same people see them again. Ask how many new versions per month the fee includes.</p></li><li><p>Revisions beyond the agreed rounds on each video.</p></li><li><p>Stock footage and music licences, which can add a few hundred dollars a month.</p></li><li><p>Tracking and reporting tools, if the agency passes software costs on to you.</p></li><li><p>Early exit fees on longer contracts.</p></li></ul><p>If a quote doesn't mention production at all, assume you will need to supply the videos yourself.</p><h2>When an agency is worth it, and when to run ads yourself</h2><p>An agency usually pays for itself when at least two of these are true:</p><ul><li><p>You can spend $1,500 or more a month on ads for at least three months.</p></li><li><p>You don't have ad videos yet, or the ones you have were made for organic posting.</p></li><li><p>Nobody on your team has set up conversion tracking in Google Ads before.</p></li><li><p>You sell something with a clear value per customer, so results can be measured in leads or sales.</p></li></ul><p>If your budget is a few hundred dollars a month, an agency fee will likely cost more than the ads. You'll learn more by running a small campaign yourself in Google Ads, or by paying a freelancer for a one-time setup. MarketerHire makes a similar point: an agency that takes on an account spending around $1,500 a month is mostly selling its time, because YouTube campaigns need enough volume to learn.</p><p>One rule applies at any budget: the Google Ads account should be in your name. If you part ways, you keep the history, audiences and conversion data.</p><h2>How to compare agency quotes</h2><p>Get at least three quotes, then put them side by side using the same questions:</p><ol><li><p>Is this price the fee only, or fee plus ad spend?</p></li><li><p>Which pricing model is it, and what happens to the fee if my ad budget doubles?</p></li><li><p>How many new ad versions do you test each month?</p></li><li><p>Is video production included? If not, what does one ad video cost?</p></li><li><p>Who owns the Google Ads account and the YouTube channel?</p></li><li><p>What is the minimum contract, and what does it cost to leave early?</p></li><li><p>Which one or two numbers will you report on? Cost per lead and return on ad spend are more useful than views.</p></li><li><p>Can you show results from a business like mine, with real numbers and links?</p></li></ol><p>Run the break-even formula from earlier on any percentage quote, then look at the total for your planned budget, not the headline fee.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much should a small business spend on YouTube ads?</h3><p>Many small businesses start between $100 and $1,000 a month; 53% of businesses in a WebFX survey spent in that range. If you're hiring an agency, budget at least $1,500 a month in ad spend so the campaign has enough data to improve.</p><h3>Is the ad spend included in the agency's fee?</h3><p>Usually not. Most agencies charge their fee separately, and you pay Google directly for the ads. Always ask, because some quotes combine both.</p><h3>Is a percentage-of-spend fee a bad deal?</h3><p>Not by itself. It's often cheaper than a flat fee at low budgets and more expensive at high ones. Use the break-even formula to see where your budget falls.</p><h3>Is there a minimum ad spend on YouTube?</h3><p>Google doesn't set a minimum. Agencies often do, because small budgets don't give them enough data to test and improve.</p><h3>How long before YouTube ads show results?</h3><p>Expect the first month to go to setup and early testing. Most agencies and advertisers judge results after about three months of steady spend.</p><h3>Do YouTube ads agencies make the videos?</h3><p>Some do and some don't. Ask before you sign, since production can cost more than management.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-ads-agency-cost'
  and body_json::text like '%On YT Agencies, the five YouTube ads age%';

-- youtube-channel-management-agency-cost
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A YouTube channel management agency usually costs $1,000 to $5,000 a month for a small business or creator, and $5,000 to $10,000 or more for a full-service team that plans, edits, optimizes and reports on several videos a week."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In our October 2026 review of publicly listed agency prices, most channel management agencies started at around $1,000 a month on a monthly retainer. What you pay above that depends mostly on how many videos a month the agency handles and whether filming is included."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unlike YouTube ads, there's no second bill for ad spend. The retainer is usually the whole cost, plus any production extras."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A $1,000 starting price is an entry package. Expect it to cover a small number of videos a month with editing, thumbnails and upload optimization. Ask each agency exactly what their entry tier includes before comparing."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Price tiers and what you get"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Published agency price guides put channel work in roughly these bands ("},{"type":"text","text":"Humble & Brag","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://humbleandbrag.com/blog/youtube-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"MarketerHire","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"):"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tier"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical monthly cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What's usually included"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Packaging only"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$500 to $2,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Titles and thumbnails, sometimes A/B testing"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Editing and upload"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2,000 to $5,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Editing of your footage, thumbnails, descriptions, scheduling"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Growth management"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$3,000 to $8,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strategy, topic research, SEO, editing, Shorts, monthly reporting"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Full service"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10,000 to $30,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"All of the above plus scripting, filming days, community management and often ads"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Humble & Brag publishes its tiers in euros; the dollar figures treat them as roughly equal. These ranges overlap because agencies bundle services differently, so compare what's included, not the tier name."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A rough rule: cost per video"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Divide the monthly fee by the number of finished videos. A $3,000 retainer for four long videos and eight Shorts works out to $750 per long video, with the Shorts and strategy included. This number makes very different packages easier to compare."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What drives the price up or down"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Number of videos a month. The biggest factor by far."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who films. Editing your footage is cheap; sending a crew is not. On-location shoot days often cost $2,000 to $5,000 each on top of the retainer."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video length and editing style. A 30-minute podcast edit costs more than a 6-minute talking-head video; heavy motion graphics cost more again."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strategy depth. Topic research, competitor analysis and testing titles add senior time."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your niche. Finance, B2B and medical channels need more research and review, so agencies often charge more."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Team seniority. Ask who actually works on your channel day to day."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Costs that aren't in the headline price"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Onboarding or setup fees in the first month"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Extra revision rounds beyond what the package includes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stock footage and music licences"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Filming days and travel"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad spend, if the agency also runs YouTube ads (see our "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency cost guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is channel management worth it?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It usually is when YouTube already brings in customers or income and the work is eating your week. Add up the hours you spend planning, editing and uploading each month and multiply by what your time is worth. If that's more than the retainer, hiring makes sense."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It usually isn't when you have fewer than 10 videos, no clear audience yet, or can't commit for three months. Start with a freelance editor or a one-off "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" instead."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does a YouTube manager cost per month?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freelance YouTube manager often charges $500 to $3,000 a month. Most agencies we reviewed start at around $1,000 a month and rise with the number of videos and services."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do channel management agencies take a percentage of revenue?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most charge a flat monthly fee. Some creator-focused agencies take a share of channel revenue or brand deals instead; read the contract to see which income it covers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is there a minimum contract?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It varies, so check the contract before you sign."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What should a channel management package include?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At minimum: editing, thumbnails, titles and descriptions, scheduling, and a monthly report. Better packages add topic research, Shorts and a clear plan for next month."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who owns the channel if I hire an agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You should. Give the agency access through YouTube Studio permissions, never your password, and keep the owner role yourself."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>A YouTube channel management agency usually costs $1,000 to $5,000 a month for a small business or creator, and $5,000 to $10,000 or more for a full-service team that plans, edits, optimizes and reports on several videos a week.</p><p>In our October 2026 review of publicly listed agency prices, most channel management agencies started at around $1,000 a month on a monthly retainer. What you pay above that depends mostly on how many videos a month the agency handles and whether filming is included.</p><p>Unlike YouTube ads, there's no second bill for ad spend. The retainer is usually the whole cost, plus any production extras.</p><p>A $1,000 starting price is an entry package. Expect it to cover a small number of videos a month with editing, thumbnails and upload optimization. Ask each agency exactly what their entry tier includes before comparing.</p><h2>Price tiers and what you get</h2><p>Published agency price guides put channel work in roughly these bands (<a href="https://humbleandbrag.com/blog/youtube-agency-cost">Humble &amp; Brag</a>, <a href="https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency">MarketerHire</a>):</p><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Typical monthly cost</th><th>What's usually included</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Packaging only</td><td>$500 to $2,000</td><td>Titles and thumbnails, sometimes A/B testing</td></tr><tr><td>Editing and upload</td><td>$2,000 to $5,000</td><td>Editing of your footage, thumbnails, descriptions, scheduling</td></tr><tr><td>Growth management</td><td>$3,000 to $8,000</td><td>Strategy, topic research, SEO, editing, Shorts, monthly reporting</td></tr><tr><td>Full service</td><td>$10,000 to $30,000</td><td>All of the above plus scripting, filming days, community management and often ads</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Humble &amp; Brag publishes its tiers in euros; the dollar figures treat them as roughly equal. These ranges overlap because agencies bundle services differently, so compare what's included, not the tier name.</p><h3>A rough rule: cost per video</h3><p>Divide the monthly fee by the number of finished videos. A $3,000 retainer for four long videos and eight Shorts works out to $750 per long video, with the Shorts and strategy included. This number makes very different packages easier to compare.</p><h2>What drives the price up or down</h2><ul><li><p>Number of videos a month. The biggest factor by far.</p></li><li><p>Who films. Editing your footage is cheap; sending a crew is not. On-location shoot days often cost $2,000 to $5,000 each on top of the retainer.</p></li><li><p>Video length and editing style. A 30-minute podcast edit costs more than a 6-minute talking-head video; heavy motion graphics cost more again.</p></li><li><p>Strategy depth. Topic research, competitor analysis and testing titles add senior time.</p></li><li><p>Your niche. Finance, B2B and medical channels need more research and review, so agencies often charge more.</p></li><li><p>Team seniority. Ask who actually works on your channel day to day.</p></li></ul><h2>Costs that aren't in the headline price</h2><ul><li><p>Onboarding or setup fees in the first month</p></li><li><p>Extra revision rounds beyond what the package includes</p></li><li><p>Stock footage and music licences</p></li><li><p>Filming days and travel</p></li><li><p>Ad spend, if the agency also runs YouTube ads (see our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agency cost guide</a>)</p></li></ul><h2>Is channel management worth it?</h2><p>It usually is when YouTube already brings in customers or income and the work is eating your week. Add up the hours you spend planning, editing and uploading each month and multiply by what your time is worth. If that's more than the retainer, hiring makes sense.</p><p>It usually isn't when you have fewer than 10 videos, no clear audience yet, or can't commit for three months. Start with a freelance editor or a one-off channel audit instead.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does a YouTube manager cost per month?</h3><p>A freelance YouTube manager often charges $500 to $3,000 a month. Most agencies we reviewed start at around $1,000 a month and rise with the number of videos and services.</p><h3>Do channel management agencies take a percentage of revenue?</h3><p>Most charge a flat monthly fee. Some creator-focused agencies take a share of channel revenue or brand deals instead; read the contract to see which income it covers.</p><h3>Is there a minimum contract?</h3><p>It varies, so check the contract before you sign.</p><h3>What should a channel management package include?</h3><p>At minimum: editing, thumbnails, titles and descriptions, scheduling, and a monthly report. Better packages add topic research, Shorts and a clear plan for next month.</p><h3>Who owns the channel if I hire an agency?</h3><p>You should. Give the agency access through YouTube Studio permissions, never your password, and keep the owner role yourself.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-channel-management-agency-cost'
  and body_json::text like '%Every channel management agency listed o%';

-- facebook-vs-youtube-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube is better for being found. People search it for answers, and a useful video can bring in customers for years. Facebook is better for staying in front of people who already know you, local community reach, and cheap retargeting ads."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you can only do one well, pick YouTube when your customers research before buying (services, software, considered purchases), and Facebook when they buy on impulse or locally (restaurants, events, simple products). Most businesses get the best results by making videos for YouTube and sharing clips on Facebook."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Facebook vs YouTube: side by side"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Facebook"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How people find you"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Search and recommendations"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"News feed, groups, shares"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lifespan of a post"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Months to years"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually a day or two"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Viewer intent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often searching for an answer or researching a purchase"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually scrolling, not looking for you"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best video length"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-form (5 to 20+ minutes) plus Shorts"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Short clips and Reels"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Organic reach for a new account"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Possible through search, even with zero subscribers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Low without paid boosts"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Watched on TVs"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, a large and growing share of viewing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rarely"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Community features"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comments, community posts, memberships"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Groups, events, Messenger, reviews"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Local business tools"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Limited"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strong: reviews, events, local groups, Marketplace"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical ad cost (CPM)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $3.50 average; $4 to $10 for most formats"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $8 to $11 average; Reels $4 to $8"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best ad use"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reaching new people with a message they'll watch"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Retargeting site visitors and people who engaged"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad cost figures: "},{"type":"text","text":"Mega Digital","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ad-benchmarks/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"Mega Digital cost guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ads-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for YouTube; "},{"type":"text","text":"Influee","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://influee.co/blog/good-cpm-facebook-ads"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for Facebook. CPMs swing widely by industry, season and targeting, so treat them as a rough guide."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The real difference: search vs feed"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"On YouTube, a video titled \"How much does a kitchen remodel cost in Denver?\" can show up every time someone types that question, next month and three years from now. On Facebook, the same video reaches some of your followers this week and then mostly disappears from feeds."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That makes YouTube an asset that builds over time and Facebook a channel you keep feeding. Both are useful, but they do different jobs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which is better for your type of business?"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Business type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lead with"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Clinics, dentists, law firms"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Patients and clients research questions and want to trust you first"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trades (plumbing, roofing, HVAC)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Homeowners search for costs and fixes before calling"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SaaS and B2B services"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Buyers watch demos, comparisons and tutorials during evaluation"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coaches, consultants, courses"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long videos show expertise and build an audience you can sell to"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"E-commerce"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube for reviews and demos, Facebook and Instagram for retargeting"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Restaurants, cafes, gyms"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Facebook"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Local followers, events and reviews matter more than search"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Local events and community groups"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Facebook"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Groups and event pages reach nearby people fast"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to use both without doubling the work"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Record one long video for YouTube that answers a real customer question."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cut two or three short clips from it for Facebook Reels and Shorts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Post each clip on Facebook with a line pointing to the full video."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Install the Meta pixel on your website, then run small Facebook retargeting ads to people who visited after watching."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put your best-performing YouTube video behind a YouTube ads budget to reach people who haven't heard of you yet."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you want help with the ads side, see our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"what a YouTube ads agency costs","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is YouTube or Facebook better for small businesses?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It depends on how your customers buy. If they search and compare before buying, YouTube is usually better. If they buy locally or on impulse, Facebook is often the easier start."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Are YouTube ads cheaper than Facebook ads?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per thousand impressions, YouTube is often cheaper: around $3.50 on average versus $8 to $11 on Facebook in the benchmarks above. Cost per lead or sale is what matters, and that depends on your offer and creative."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can I post the same video on both?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, but edit it for each. Upload the full version to YouTube, and post a shorter, vertical, captioned clip on Facebook, where many people scroll with the sound off."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which has better analytics?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube Studio is stronger for video, with watch time, audience retention and traffic sources for each video. Facebook's tools are stronger for ad targeting and retargeting."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I give up on Facebook?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Keep a Facebook page for reviews, local reach and retargeting, even if YouTube becomes your main video channel."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>YouTube is better for being found. People search it for answers, and a useful video can bring in customers for years. Facebook is better for staying in front of people who already know you, local community reach, and cheap retargeting ads.</p><p>If you can only do one well, pick YouTube when your customers research before buying (services, software, considered purchases), and Facebook when they buy on impulse or locally (restaurants, events, simple products). Most businesses get the best results by making videos for YouTube and sharing clips on Facebook.</p><h2>Facebook vs YouTube: side by side</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>YouTube</th><th>Facebook</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How people find you</td><td>Search and recommendations</td><td>News feed, groups, shares</td></tr><tr><td>Lifespan of a post</td><td>Months to years</td><td>Usually a day or two</td></tr><tr><td>Viewer intent</td><td>Often searching for an answer or researching a purchase</td><td>Usually scrolling, not looking for you</td></tr><tr><td>Best video length</td><td>Long-form (5 to 20+ minutes) plus Shorts</td><td>Short clips and Reels</td></tr><tr><td>Organic reach for a new account</td><td>Possible through search, even with zero subscribers</td><td>Low without paid boosts</td></tr><tr><td>Watched on TVs</td><td>Yes, a large and growing share of viewing</td><td>Rarely</td></tr><tr><td>Community features</td><td>Comments, community posts, memberships</td><td>Groups, events, Messenger, reviews</td></tr><tr><td>Local business tools</td><td>Limited</td><td>Strong: reviews, events, local groups, Marketplace</td></tr><tr><td>Typical ad cost (CPM)</td><td>About $3.50 average; $4 to $10 for most formats</td><td>About $8 to $11 average; Reels $4 to $8</td></tr><tr><td>Best ad use</td><td>Reaching new people with a message they'll watch</td><td>Retargeting site visitors and people who engaged</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ad cost figures: <a href="https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ad-benchmarks/">Mega Digital</a> and <a href="https://megadigital.ai/en/blog/youtube-ads-cost">Mega Digital cost guide</a> for YouTube; <a href="https://influee.co/blog/good-cpm-facebook-ads">Influee</a> for Facebook. CPMs swing widely by industry, season and targeting, so treat them as a rough guide.</p><h3>The real difference: search vs feed</h3><p>On YouTube, a video titled "How much does a kitchen remodel cost in Denver?" can show up every time someone types that question, next month and three years from now. On Facebook, the same video reaches some of your followers this week and then mostly disappears from feeds.</p><p>That makes YouTube an asset that builds over time and Facebook a channel you keep feeding. Both are useful, but they do different jobs.</p><h2>Which is better for your type of business?</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Business type</th><th>Lead with</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Clinics, dentists, law firms</td><td>YouTube</td><td>Patients and clients research questions and want to trust you first</td></tr><tr><td>Trades (plumbing, roofing, HVAC)</td><td>YouTube</td><td>Homeowners search for costs and fixes before calling</td></tr><tr><td>SaaS and B2B services</td><td>YouTube</td><td>Buyers watch demos, comparisons and tutorials during evaluation</td></tr><tr><td>Coaches, consultants, courses</td><td>YouTube</td><td>Long videos show expertise and build an audience you can sell to</td></tr><tr><td>E-commerce</td><td>Both</td><td>YouTube for reviews and demos, Facebook and Instagram for retargeting</td></tr><tr><td>Restaurants, cafes, gyms</td><td>Facebook</td><td>Local followers, events and reviews matter more than search</td></tr><tr><td>Local events and community groups</td><td>Facebook</td><td>Groups and event pages reach nearby people fast</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How to use both without doubling the work</h2><ol><li><p>Record one long video for YouTube that answers a real customer question.</p></li><li><p>Cut two or three short clips from it for Facebook Reels and Shorts.</p></li><li><p>Post each clip on Facebook with a line pointing to the full video.</p></li><li><p>Install the Meta pixel on your website, then run small Facebook retargeting ads to people who visited after watching.</p></li><li><p>Put your best-performing YouTube video behind a YouTube ads budget to reach people who haven't heard of you yet.</p></li></ol><p>If you want help with the ads side, see our guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">what a YouTube ads agency costs</a>.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is YouTube or Facebook better for small businesses?</h3><p>It depends on how your customers buy. If they search and compare before buying, YouTube is usually better. If they buy locally or on impulse, Facebook is often the easier start.</p><h3>Are YouTube ads cheaper than Facebook ads?</h3><p>Per thousand impressions, YouTube is often cheaper: around $3.50 on average versus $8 to $11 on Facebook in the benchmarks above. Cost per lead or sale is what matters, and that depends on your offer and creative.</p><h3>Can I post the same video on both?</h3><p>Yes, but edit it for each. Upload the full version to YouTube, and post a shorter, vertical, captioned clip on Facebook, where many people scroll with the sound off.</p><h3>Which has better analytics?</h3><p>YouTube Studio is stronger for video, with watch time, audience retention and traffic sources for each video. Facebook's tools are stronger for ad targeting and retargeting.</p><h3>Should I give up on Facebook?</h3><p>No. Keep a Facebook page for reviews, local reach and retargeting, even if YouTube becomes your main video channel.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'facebook-vs-youtube-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%If you want help with the ads side, comp%';

-- find-youtube-influencers-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The fastest way to find YouTube influencers for your business is to search YouTube for the topics your customers care about, note the creators who keep appearing, and check their recent views and audience. You can also use YouTube's own creator search inside Google Ads, an influencer platform, or an influencer marketing agency."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Look past subscriber counts. A creator with 20,000 subscribers whose viewers are exactly your buyers will usually beat a creator with a million general viewers, and cost a fraction of the price."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7 ways to find the right YouTube creators"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Search YouTube the way your customers do"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type in the questions and product categories your buyers search for, like \"best CRM for small business\" or \"how to fix a leaking tap\". Filter results to the last year. The creators who rank repeatedly already have your buyers' attention."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Use YouTube Creator Partnerships in Google Ads"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube's own creator marketplace, formerly called BrandConnect, now lives inside Google Ads. Brands can search more than 3 million creators in the YouTube Partner Program and build shortlists ("},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube blog","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://blog.youtube/news-and-events/youtube-creator-partnerships-newfronts-2026/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"). Businesses with a Google Ads account in a country where the YouTube Partner Program runs can use the search ("},{"type":"text","text":"MakeInfluence","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.makeinfluence.com/en/academy/youtube-creator-partnerships-formerly-brandconnect-youtubes-own-marketplace-for-brand-deals"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Check who your competitors sponsor"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Watch competitors' sponsored videos and note the creators. Those creators already know your category, and their audiences are proven buyers of products like yours."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Ask your customers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add \"Which YouTube channels do you watch about [topic]?\" to your onboarding survey or sales calls. The answers often surface small, trusted creators you would never find through search."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Follow the collaborations of creators you like"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creators in the same niche often appear in each other's videos and podcasts. Once you find one good fit, check who they collaborate with; the next creator on your list is often one video away."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Use an influencer marketing platform"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid databases let you filter creators by niche, country, audience age and average views. They save time if you plan to run several campaigns."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Hire an influencer marketing agency"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An agency finds creators, negotiates rates, writes briefs and tracks results. It costs more but makes sense for campaigns across many creators."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to vet a creator before you pay"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check these on the creator's channel yourself, then ask the creator for screenshots of their YouTube Studio analytics:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Average views on the last 10 to 15 videos. This matters far more than subscribers. A healthy channel often gets views equal to 10% to 40% of its subscriber count per video ("},{"type":"text","text":"Elev8or","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.elev8or.io/blog/youtube-influencer-marketing-guide-for-brands"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Audience country and age. A creator with mostly overseas viewers won't help a local US clinic."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comment quality. Real questions and discussion are a good sign; generic \"great video\" spam is not."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Past sponsorships. Watch one. Does the creator make it natural, and do the comments react well?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brand fit. Would you be comfortable with their last 20 videos sitting next to your name?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What YouTube influencers charge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creators usually price on expected views, not subscribers. Integrations (a 30 to 90 second segment inside a video) typically cost $20 to $50 per 1,000 views, depending on niche ("},{"type":"text","text":"CollabPals","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.collabpals.com/tools/youtube-sponsorship-rate-calculator"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"). Finance, B2B and tech audiences sit at the top of the range."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel size"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical price per integration"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under 10,000 subscribers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$50 to $500"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,000 to 50,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$200 to $2,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"50,000 to 250,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $10,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"250,000 to 1 million"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$5,000 to $50,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 1 million"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$25,000 to $250,000+"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ranges from "},{"type":"text","text":"OutlierKit","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://outlierkit.com/resources/youtube-sponsorship-rates/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" (under 10,000) and "},{"type":"text","text":"Argil","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.argil.ai/blog/how-much-do-youtubers-make"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" (larger tiers). A full dedicated video usually costs several times an integration, and sponsored Shorts cost less per view."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other ways to pay:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Affiliate or commission only: the creator earns a share of each sale. Good for e-commerce, but top creators rarely accept it alone."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free product: works with small creators who genuinely like the product."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hybrid: a lower flat fee plus commission."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usage rights: pay extra to run the creator's video as your own YouTube ad."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to reach out"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep the first message short and specific. Say which of their videos you watched, why your product fits their audience, what you're offering, and ask for their rates. Use the business email on their channel's About page. Don't send a script before they've agreed; good creators know their audience better than you do."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Disclosure rules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sponsored videos must be disclosed. On YouTube, the creator ticks the paid promotion box when uploading, which adds an \"Includes paid promotion\" label to the video. Advertising regulators, such as the FTC in the US and the ASA in the UK, also expect a clear spoken or on-screen mention that the video is sponsored. Put both requirements in your written agreement, since your brand can share the blame for a hidden ad."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to measure results"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Give each creator a unique link with UTM tags, so your analytics show exactly which video sent each visitor."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Give each creator a unique discount code, which catches buyers who don't click the link."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask how customers heard about you at checkout or on intake forms."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Track results for at least 90 days. YouTube videos keep getting views long after launch, so sales often keep coming."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare cost per customer across creators, then put next quarter's budget behind the ones that delivered."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does it cost to work with a YouTube influencer?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"From free product for very small creators to $250,000 or more for the largest. Most small and mid-sized businesses start with creators in the 10,000 to 100,000 subscriber range, at a few hundred to a few thousand dollars per video."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Are micro-influencers worth it for small businesses?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often, yes. Small creators usually cost less, are more flexible, and have closer relationships with their audience. Working with several small creators also spreads your risk."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I contact a YouTuber for a sponsorship?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use the business email on their channel's About page, or search for them in YouTube Creator Partnerships inside Google Ads. Some larger creators work through managers or agencies."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can I use a creator's video in my own ads?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, if your agreement includes usage rights. YouTube also lets brands run eligible creator videos as ads through Google Ads. Agree on duration and fee in writing first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I use an agency or do it myself?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For one to three creators, doing it yourself is manageable. For larger campaigns, an influencer marketing agency saves time on finding, negotiating and tracking."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>The fastest way to find YouTube influencers for your business is to search YouTube for the topics your customers care about, note the creators who keep appearing, and check their recent views and audience. You can also use YouTube's own creator search inside Google Ads, an influencer platform, or an influencer marketing agency.</p><p>Look past subscriber counts. A creator with 20,000 subscribers whose viewers are exactly your buyers will usually beat a creator with a million general viewers, and cost a fraction of the price.</p><h2>7 ways to find the right YouTube creators</h2><h3>1. Search YouTube the way your customers do</h3><p>Type in the questions and product categories your buyers search for, like "best CRM for small business" or "how to fix a leaking tap". Filter results to the last year. The creators who rank repeatedly already have your buyers' attention.</p><h3>2. Use YouTube Creator Partnerships in Google Ads</h3><p>YouTube's own creator marketplace, formerly called BrandConnect, now lives inside Google Ads. Brands can search more than 3 million creators in the YouTube Partner Program and build shortlists (<a href="https://blog.youtube/news-and-events/youtube-creator-partnerships-newfronts-2026/">YouTube blog</a>). Businesses with a Google Ads account in a country where the YouTube Partner Program runs can use the search (<a href="https://www.makeinfluence.com/en/academy/youtube-creator-partnerships-formerly-brandconnect-youtubes-own-marketplace-for-brand-deals">MakeInfluence</a>).</p><h3>3. Check who your competitors sponsor</h3><p>Watch competitors' sponsored videos and note the creators. Those creators already know your category, and their audiences are proven buyers of products like yours.</p><h3>4. Ask your customers</h3><p>Add "Which YouTube channels do you watch about [topic]?" to your onboarding survey or sales calls. The answers often surface small, trusted creators you would never find through search.</p><h3>5. Follow the collaborations of creators you like</h3><p>Creators in the same niche often appear in each other's videos and podcasts. Once you find one good fit, check who they collaborate with; the next creator on your list is often one video away.</p><h3>6. Use an influencer marketing platform</h3><p>Paid databases let you filter creators by niche, country, audience age and average views. They save time if you plan to run several campaigns.</p><h3>7. Hire an influencer marketing agency</h3><p>An agency finds creators, negotiates rates, writes briefs and tracks results. It costs more but makes sense for campaigns across many creators.</p><h2>How to vet a creator before you pay</h2><p>Check these on the creator's channel yourself, then ask the creator for screenshots of their YouTube Studio analytics:</p><ul><li><p>Average views on the last 10 to 15 videos. This matters far more than subscribers. A healthy channel often gets views equal to 10% to 40% of its subscriber count per video (<a href="https://www.elev8or.io/blog/youtube-influencer-marketing-guide-for-brands">Elev8or</a>).</p></li><li><p>Audience country and age. A creator with mostly overseas viewers won't help a local US clinic.</p></li><li><p>Comment quality. Real questions and discussion are a good sign; generic "great video" spam is not.</p></li><li><p>Past sponsorships. Watch one. Does the creator make it natural, and do the comments react well?</p></li><li><p>Brand fit. Would you be comfortable with their last 20 videos sitting next to your name?</p></li></ul><h2>What YouTube influencers charge</h2><p>Creators usually price on expected views, not subscribers. Integrations (a 30 to 90 second segment inside a video) typically cost $20 to $50 per 1,000 views, depending on niche (<a href="https://www.collabpals.com/tools/youtube-sponsorship-rate-calculator">CollabPals</a>). Finance, B2B and tech audiences sit at the top of the range.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Channel size</th><th>Typical price per integration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under 10,000 subscribers</td><td>$50 to $500</td></tr><tr><td>10,000 to 50,000</td><td>$200 to $2,000</td></tr><tr><td>50,000 to 250,000</td><td>$1,000 to $10,000</td></tr><tr><td>250,000 to 1 million</td><td>$5,000 to $50,000</td></tr><tr><td>Over 1 million</td><td>$25,000 to $250,000+</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ranges from <a href="https://outlierkit.com/resources/youtube-sponsorship-rates/">OutlierKit</a> (under 10,000) and <a href="https://www.argil.ai/blog/how-much-do-youtubers-make">Argil</a> (larger tiers). A full dedicated video usually costs several times an integration, and sponsored Shorts cost less per view.</p><p>Other ways to pay:</p><ul><li><p>Affiliate or commission only: the creator earns a share of each sale. Good for e-commerce, but top creators rarely accept it alone.</p></li><li><p>Free product: works with small creators who genuinely like the product.</p></li><li><p>Hybrid: a lower flat fee plus commission.</p></li><li><p>Usage rights: pay extra to run the creator's video as your own YouTube ad.</p></li></ul><h2>How to reach out</h2><p>Keep the first message short and specific. Say which of their videos you watched, why your product fits their audience, what you're offering, and ask for their rates. Use the business email on their channel's About page. Don't send a script before they've agreed; good creators know their audience better than you do.</p><h2>Disclosure rules</h2><p>Sponsored videos must be disclosed. On YouTube, the creator ticks the paid promotion box when uploading, which adds an "Includes paid promotion" label to the video. Advertising regulators, such as the FTC in the US and the ASA in the UK, also expect a clear spoken or on-screen mention that the video is sponsored. Put both requirements in your written agreement, since your brand can share the blame for a hidden ad.</p><h2>How to measure results</h2><ol><li><p>Give each creator a unique link with UTM tags, so your analytics show exactly which video sent each visitor.</p></li><li><p>Give each creator a unique discount code, which catches buyers who don't click the link.</p></li><li><p>Ask how customers heard about you at checkout or on intake forms.</p></li><li><p>Track results for at least 90 days. YouTube videos keep getting views long after launch, so sales often keep coming.</p></li><li><p>Compare cost per customer across creators, then put next quarter's budget behind the ones that delivered.</p></li></ol><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does it cost to work with a YouTube influencer?</h3><p>From free product for very small creators to $250,000 or more for the largest. Most small and mid-sized businesses start with creators in the 10,000 to 100,000 subscriber range, at a few hundred to a few thousand dollars per video.</p><h3>Are micro-influencers worth it for small businesses?</h3><p>Often, yes. Small creators usually cost less, are more flexible, and have closer relationships with their audience. Working with several small creators also spreads your risk.</p><h3>How do I contact a YouTuber for a sponsorship?</h3><p>Use the business email on their channel's About page, or search for them in YouTube Creator Partnerships inside Google Ads. Some larger creators work through managers or agencies.</p><h3>Can I use a creator's video in my own ads?</h3><p>Yes, if your agreement includes usage rights. YouTube also lets brands run eligible creator videos as ads through Google Ads. Agree on duration and fee in writing first.</p><h3>Should I use an agency or do it myself?</h3><p>For one to three creators, doing it yourself is manageable. For larger campaigns, an influencer marketing agency saves time on finding, negotiating and tracking.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'find-youtube-influencers-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%r campaigns across many creators. Compar%';

-- how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To create a YouTube channel for your business, sign in to YouTube with a Google account made for the business, choose \"Create a channel\", and enter your business name. Then add a logo, banner, description and links, and invite your team as channel managers. The whole setup takes about 30 minutes and costs nothing."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What to have ready"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A Google account for the business (more on which email below)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your logo as a square image, at least 98 x 98 pixels"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A banner image, 2048 x 1152 pixels, with text kept inside the central safe area"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A one-paragraph description of who you help and what the channel covers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your website, booking page and social links"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The email addresses of anyone who will help manage the channel"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should you make a business email for YouTube?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. Sign in with a Google account tied to the business, not the owner's personal Gmail. If you use Google Workspace, use an address like marketing@yourbusiness.com. If you don't, you can create a Google account with your existing business email, or make a new Gmail just for the business."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reason is ownership. Channels set up on an employee's or agency's personal account become a problem the day that person leaves. With a business account as the owner, you can add and remove people without ever handing over the main login."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Personal channel vs business-name channel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube offers two kinds of channel ("},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube Help","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1646861"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"):"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Personal channel"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel with a business name"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Name shown"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Google account's name"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Any name you choose"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Managers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only you"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Several owners and managers, each with their own login"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What it creates"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A channel on your Google account"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A channel on a new Brand Account"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best for"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Solo creators using their own name"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Almost every business"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a business, pick the second option. It lets your marketing person, editor or agency manage the channel without sharing a password."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to set up a YouTube channel for business, step by step"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Sign in with the business Google account"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Go to youtube.com and sign in with the account from the section above."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Create the channel with your business name"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Open your channel list from your account settings, then choose \"Create a new channel\". Add a profile picture, your business name and a handle (your @name, ideally the same as your business name or website). Click \"Create channel\". This creates a Brand Account for the business automatically ("},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube Help","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1646861"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Verify the channel with a phone number"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In YouTube Studio, verify the channel by phone. Verification unlocks features most businesses need, including custom thumbnails and uploads longer than 15 minutes."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Add your branding"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In YouTube Studio, open Customization, then Branding:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Profile picture: your logo, square, at least 98 x 98 pixels (800 x 800 looks sharper)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Banner: 2048 x 1152 pixels, with any text and logos inside the central area so it isn't cropped on phones"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video watermark: an optional small logo or subscribe button shown on every video"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Write your channel description and add links"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under Customization, then Basic info, write two or three short paragraphs: who you help, what the videos cover, and how often you post. Put your main search phrase in the first sentence (for example, \"Plumbing repair tips for homeowners in Leeds\"). Add your website, booking page and contact email in the links section."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Add your team as managers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In YouTube Studio, go to Settings, then Permissions, and invite people by email. Give editors and agencies the Manager or Editor role, and keep the Owner role inside the business."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Set upload defaults"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under Settings, then Upload defaults, add text that should appear on every video description, such as your website and phone number. This saves time and keeps every video linked back to your business."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Create a channel trailer and your first playlists"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A short trailer (30 to 60 seconds) tells new visitors who the channel is for. Create two or three playlists named after your main services or customer questions, so new videos have a home from day one."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your first five videos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"An empty channel doesn't convince anyone. Before you share the channel, publish five videos that answer the questions customers ask most:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The price question: \"How much does [your service] cost?\" Give real ranges and what changes the price."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The comparison: \"[Option A] vs [option B]: which is right for you?\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The mistake video: \"5 mistakes people make with [problem]\"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The process: \"What happens when you hire us\", step by step."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A customer story, with the customer on camera if they agree."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our guide to the "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube script structure for business owners","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-script-structure-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows how to plan each one in under an hour."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Example: starting a channel for a trade business"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trades like plumbing, electrical, roofing and HVAC are a strong fit for YouTube, because homeowners search before they call. A roofer's first ten videos might be:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does a new roof cost in [city]?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Repair or replace? How to tell from the ground"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What a roof inspection actually checks"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Metal vs asphalt shingles"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Walkarounds of finished jobs, filmed on a phone"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep it simple: film on site with a phone and a clip-on mic, show the work, and explain it the way you would to a customer. Put your service area in titles and descriptions, and link the channel from your Google Business Profile."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mistakes to avoid"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creating the channel on an employee's or agency's personal account"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Choosing a handle that doesn't match your business name"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Uploading ads or company announcements nobody searches for"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Leaving the description and links empty"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Posting ten videos in a week, then nothing for two months"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Turning off comments, which removes useful feedback and signals"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is it free to create a YouTube channel for a business?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. Creating the channel, uploading videos and using YouTube Studio are all free."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can I change my business channel's name later?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, in YouTube Studio under Customization. Pick a name you plan to keep, though, because frequent changes confuse viewers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do I need a Brand Account?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you create the channel with a business name, YouTube creates the Brand Account for you. It's what lets several people manage the channel."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can I turn my personal channel into a business channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. YouTube lets you move a channel to a Brand Account, though the steps change from time to time. Check "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube Help","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/3056283"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the current process before you start."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I hire someone to set up the channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Setup is easy enough to do yourself. Help is more useful for what comes after: planning, editing and growing the channel."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>To create a YouTube channel for your business, sign in to YouTube with a Google account made for the business, choose "Create a channel", and enter your business name. Then add a logo, banner, description and links, and invite your team as channel managers. The whole setup takes about 30 minutes and costs nothing.</p><h3>What to have ready</h3><ul><li><p>A Google account for the business (more on which email below)</p></li><li><p>Your logo as a square image, at least 98 x 98 pixels</p></li><li><p>A banner image, 2048 x 1152 pixels, with text kept inside the central safe area</p></li><li><p>A one-paragraph description of who you help and what the channel covers</p></li><li><p>Your website, booking page and social links</p></li><li><p>The email addresses of anyone who will help manage the channel</p></li></ul><h2>Should you make a business email for YouTube?</h2><p>Yes. Sign in with a Google account tied to the business, not the owner's personal Gmail. If you use Google Workspace, use an address like marketing@yourbusiness.com. If you don't, you can create a Google account with your existing business email, or make a new Gmail just for the business.</p><p>The reason is ownership. Channels set up on an employee's or agency's personal account become a problem the day that person leaves. With a business account as the owner, you can add and remove people without ever handing over the main login.</p><h3>Personal channel vs business-name channel</h3><p>YouTube offers two kinds of channel (<a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1646861">YouTube Help</a>):</p><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Personal channel</th><th>Channel with a business name</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Name shown</td><td>The Google account's name</td><td>Any name you choose</td></tr><tr><td>Managers</td><td>Only you</td><td>Several owners and managers, each with their own login</td></tr><tr><td>What it creates</td><td>A channel on your Google account</td><td>A channel on a new Brand Account</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Solo creators using their own name</td><td>Almost every business</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For a business, pick the second option. It lets your marketing person, editor or agency manage the channel without sharing a password.</p><h2>How to set up a YouTube channel for business, step by step</h2><h3>1. Sign in with the business Google account</h3><p>Go to youtube.com and sign in with the account from the section above.</p><h3>2. Create the channel with your business name</h3><p>Open your channel list from your account settings, then choose "Create a new channel". Add a profile picture, your business name and a handle (your @name, ideally the same as your business name or website). Click "Create channel". This creates a Brand Account for the business automatically (<a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/1646861">YouTube Help</a>).</p><h3>3. Verify the channel with a phone number</h3><p>In YouTube Studio, verify the channel by phone. Verification unlocks features most businesses need, including custom thumbnails and uploads longer than 15 minutes.</p><h3>4. Add your branding</h3><p>In YouTube Studio, open Customization, then Branding:</p><ul><li><p>Profile picture: your logo, square, at least 98 x 98 pixels (800 x 800 looks sharper)</p></li><li><p>Banner: 2048 x 1152 pixels, with any text and logos inside the central area so it isn't cropped on phones</p></li><li><p>Video watermark: an optional small logo or subscribe button shown on every video</p></li></ul><h3>5. Write your channel description and add links</h3><p>Under Customization, then Basic info, write two or three short paragraphs: who you help, what the videos cover, and how often you post. Put your main search phrase in the first sentence (for example, "Plumbing repair tips for homeowners in Leeds"). Add your website, booking page and contact email in the links section.</p><h3>6. Add your team as managers</h3><p>In YouTube Studio, go to Settings, then Permissions, and invite people by email. Give editors and agencies the Manager or Editor role, and keep the Owner role inside the business.</p><h3>7. Set upload defaults</h3><p>Under Settings, then Upload defaults, add text that should appear on every video description, such as your website and phone number. This saves time and keeps every video linked back to your business.</p><h3>8. Create a channel trailer and your first playlists</h3><p>A short trailer (30 to 60 seconds) tells new visitors who the channel is for. Create two or three playlists named after your main services or customer questions, so new videos have a home from day one.</p><h2>Your first five videos</h2><p>An empty channel doesn't convince anyone. Before you share the channel, publish five videos that answer the questions customers ask most:</p><ol><li><p>The price question: "How much does [your service] cost?" Give real ranges and what changes the price.</p></li><li><p>The comparison: "[Option A] vs [option B]: which is right for you?"</p></li><li><p>The mistake video: "5 mistakes people make with [problem]".</p></li><li><p>The process: "What happens when you hire us", step by step.</p></li><li><p>A customer story, with the customer on camera if they agree.</p></li></ol><p>Our guide to the <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-script-structure-for-business">YouTube script structure for business owners</a> shows how to plan each one in under an hour.</p><h2>Example: starting a channel for a trade business</h2><p>Trades like plumbing, electrical, roofing and HVAC are a strong fit for YouTube, because homeowners search before they call. A roofer's first ten videos might be:</p><ul><li><p>How much does a new roof cost in [city]?</p></li><li><p>Repair or replace? How to tell from the ground</p></li><li><p>What a roof inspection actually checks</p></li><li><p>Metal vs asphalt shingles</p></li><li><p>Walkarounds of finished jobs, filmed on a phone</p></li></ul><p>Keep it simple: film on site with a phone and a clip-on mic, show the work, and explain it the way you would to a customer. Put your service area in titles and descriptions, and link the channel from your Google Business Profile.</p><h2>Mistakes to avoid</h2><ul><li><p>Creating the channel on an employee's or agency's personal account</p></li><li><p>Choosing a handle that doesn't match your business name</p></li><li><p>Uploading ads or company announcements nobody searches for</p></li><li><p>Leaving the description and links empty</p></li><li><p>Posting ten videos in a week, then nothing for two months</p></li><li><p>Turning off comments, which removes useful feedback and signals</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is it free to create a YouTube channel for a business?</h3><p>Yes. Creating the channel, uploading videos and using YouTube Studio are all free.</p><h3>Can I change my business channel's name later?</h3><p>Yes, in YouTube Studio under Customization. Pick a name you plan to keep, though, because frequent changes confuse viewers.</p><h3>Do I need a Brand Account?</h3><p>If you create the channel with a business name, YouTube creates the Brand Account for you. It's what lets several people manage the channel.</p><h3>Can I turn my personal channel into a business channel?</h3><p>Yes. YouTube lets you move a channel to a Brand Account, though the steps change from time to time. Check <a href="https://support.google.com/youtube/answer/3056283">YouTube Help</a> for the current process before you start.</p><h3>Should I hire someone to set up the channel?</h3><p>Setup is easy enough to do yourself. Help is more useful for what comes after: planning, editing and growing the channel.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%diting and growing the channel. YT Agenc%';

-- how-to-hire-a-youtube-agency
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To hire a YouTube agency, decide the one result you want, shortlist three to five agencies that have worked in your industry, check their client channels yourself, compare quotes on identical terms, and start with a 90-day trial with clear numbers to hit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The most common mistake is choosing on the sales call. An agency's pitch tells you how well it sells; its clients' channels tell you how well it works. If you're still deciding whether you need an agency at all, read "},{"type":"text","text":"when to hire a YouTube agency","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to hire a YouTube agency in 8 steps"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Write down the one result you want"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"More leads from YouTube ads\", \"a weekly video I don't have to edit\" or \"double views on our existing videos\". One sentence. It decides which type of agency you need: ads, channel management, production, SEO or packaging. See "},{"type":"text","text":"what a YouTube agency does","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/what-is-a-youtube-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the main types."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Set a budget you can hold for three months"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most YouTube work needs about three months before results settle. Use our cost guides for "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agencies","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"channel management agencies","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-channel-management-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" to set a realistic number. Remember that ads agencies charge a fee on top of ad spend."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Write a one-page brief"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Include what you sell and to whom, your goal, your budget, your existing videos or channel link, what you can provide (footage, a spokesperson, product), and your deadline. A clear brief gets sharper quotes and makes them easier to compare."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Shortlist 3 to 5 agencies that know your industry"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Filter for agencies that have worked with businesses like yours. A dental clinic and a gaming creator need different hooks, targeting and tone."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Check their work yourself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask for two or three client channels, then look them up. Check whether views per video rose and held during the agency's time, not just subscriber totals ("},{"type":"text","text":"OutlierKit","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://outlierkit.com/resources/hire-youtube-agency/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"). For ads agencies, ask for cost per lead or return on ad spend with the client's permission."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Compare quotes on the same terms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put every quote into one table: monthly fee, what's included (videos per month, ad versions tested, revisions), minimum contract, setup fees, and who owns the channel and ad account. Quotes that look far apart often include very different amounts of work."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Meet the people who'll do the work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask who handles your account day to day, and talk to them before signing. Senior people often pitch; make sure you know who executes."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Start with a 90-day trial and agreed numbers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agree on one or two numbers that define success, such as cost per lead, views per video or watch time, and review them at 30, 60 and 90 days. A common standard is a 90-day first term, then month to month ("},{"type":"text","text":"MarketerHire","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12 questions to ask before you sign"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which clients like us have you worked with, and can I see their channels?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What results did you get for them, in numbers?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who will work on my account each week?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What exactly does the monthly fee include?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many videos, or ad versions, will you deliver each month?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do you decide what to make or test next?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which one or two numbers will you report on, and how often?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What do you need from me each month, and how much of my time?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who owns the channel, the ad account, the files and the footage?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What's the minimum contract, and what does leaving early cost?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Are there setup fees or extra costs not in this quote?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What happened with a client where things didn't work?"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The last question tells you a lot. Good agencies can describe a failure plainly and what they changed."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Red flags"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Guaranteed views, subscribers or \"going viral\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No client channels they're willing to show"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A 12-month contract with no exit for the first project"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Asking for your channel password instead of access through YouTube Studio permissions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Running ads from the agency's own Google Ads account, so you lose the data when you leave"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reports that only show views and impressions, never leads, sales or watch time"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Contract terms to get in writing"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scope: videos or ad versions per month, revision rounds, turnaround times"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fee, billing date and what happens to the fee if your ad spend changes"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Minimum term and notice period"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ownership: channel, ad account, raw footage, edited files and thumbnails stay yours"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The success numbers and review dates you agreed in step 8"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How access is removed when the contract ends"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long does it take to hire a YouTube agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two to four weeks is typical: a few days to shortlist, a week of calls and quotes, and a week to agree terms and onboard."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many agencies should I talk to?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three is the minimum for a fair comparison. More than five usually slows you down without improving the choice."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I hire a specialist or a full-service agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A specialist if you have one clear gap, such as ads, editing or thumbnails. Full service if you want one team accountable for the whole channel."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What should I prepare before contacting agencies?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your one-sentence goal, monthly budget, channel link, examples of videos you like, and what you can provide yourself."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>To hire a YouTube agency, decide the one result you want, shortlist three to five agencies that have worked in your industry, check their client channels yourself, compare quotes on identical terms, and start with a 90-day trial with clear numbers to hit.</p><p>The most common mistake is choosing on the sales call. An agency's pitch tells you how well it sells; its clients' channels tell you how well it works. If you're still deciding whether you need an agency at all, read <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency">when to hire a YouTube agency</a> first.</p><h2>How to hire a YouTube agency in 8 steps</h2><h3>1. Write down the one result you want</h3><p>"More leads from YouTube ads", "a weekly video I don't have to edit" or "double views on our existing videos". One sentence. It decides which type of agency you need: ads, channel management, production, SEO or packaging. See <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/what-is-a-youtube-agency">what a YouTube agency does</a> for the main types.</p><h3>2. Set a budget you can hold for three months</h3><p>Most YouTube work needs about three months before results settle. Use our cost guides for <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agencies</a> and <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-channel-management-agency-cost">channel management agencies</a> to set a realistic number. Remember that ads agencies charge a fee on top of ad spend.</p><h3>3. Write a one-page brief</h3><p>Include what you sell and to whom, your goal, your budget, your existing videos or channel link, what you can provide (footage, a spokesperson, product), and your deadline. A clear brief gets sharper quotes and makes them easier to compare.</p><h3>4. Shortlist 3 to 5 agencies that know your industry</h3><p>Filter for agencies that have worked with businesses like yours. A dental clinic and a gaming creator need different hooks, targeting and tone.</p><h3>5. Check their work yourself</h3><p>Ask for two or three client channels, then look them up. Check whether views per video rose and held during the agency's time, not just subscriber totals (<a href="https://outlierkit.com/resources/hire-youtube-agency/">OutlierKit</a>). For ads agencies, ask for cost per lead or return on ad spend with the client's permission.</p><h3>6. Compare quotes on the same terms</h3><p>Put every quote into one table: monthly fee, what's included (videos per month, ad versions tested, revisions), minimum contract, setup fees, and who owns the channel and ad account. Quotes that look far apart often include very different amounts of work.</p><h3>7. Meet the people who'll do the work</h3><p>Ask who handles your account day to day, and talk to them before signing. Senior people often pitch; make sure you know who executes.</p><h3>8. Start with a 90-day trial and agreed numbers</h3><p>Agree on one or two numbers that define success, such as cost per lead, views per video or watch time, and review them at 30, 60 and 90 days. A common standard is a 90-day first term, then month to month (<a href="https://marketerhire.com/blog/youtube-video-marketing-agency">MarketerHire</a>).</p><h2>12 questions to ask before you sign</h2><ol><li><p>Which clients like us have you worked with, and can I see their channels?</p></li><li><p>What results did you get for them, in numbers?</p></li><li><p>Who will work on my account each week?</p></li><li><p>What exactly does the monthly fee include?</p></li><li><p>How many videos, or ad versions, will you deliver each month?</p></li><li><p>How do you decide what to make or test next?</p></li><li><p>Which one or two numbers will you report on, and how often?</p></li><li><p>What do you need from me each month, and how much of my time?</p></li><li><p>Who owns the channel, the ad account, the files and the footage?</p></li><li><p>What's the minimum contract, and what does leaving early cost?</p></li><li><p>Are there setup fees or extra costs not in this quote?</p></li><li><p>What happened with a client where things didn't work?</p></li></ol><p>The last question tells you a lot. Good agencies can describe a failure plainly and what they changed.</p><h2>Red flags</h2><ul><li><p>Guaranteed views, subscribers or "going viral"</p></li><li><p>No client channels they're willing to show</p></li><li><p>A 12-month contract with no exit for the first project</p></li><li><p>Asking for your channel password instead of access through YouTube Studio permissions</p></li><li><p>Running ads from the agency's own Google Ads account, so you lose the data when you leave</p></li><li><p>Reports that only show views and impressions, never leads, sales or watch time</p></li></ul><h2>Contract terms to get in writing</h2><ul><li><p>Scope: videos or ad versions per month, revision rounds, turnaround times</p></li><li><p>Fee, billing date and what happens to the fee if your ad spend changes</p></li><li><p>Minimum term and notice period</p></li><li><p>Ownership: channel, ad account, raw footage, edited files and thumbnails stay yours</p></li><li><p>The success numbers and review dates you agreed in step 8</p></li><li><p>How access is removed when the contract ends</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long does it take to hire a YouTube agency?</h3><p>Two to four weeks is typical: a few days to shortlist, a week of calls and quotes, and a week to agree terms and onboard.</p><h3>How many agencies should I talk to?</h3><p>Three is the minimum for a fair comparison. More than five usually slows you down without improving the choice.</p><h3>Should I hire a specialist or a full-service agency?</h3><p>A specialist if you have one clear gap, such as ads, editing or thumbnails. Full service if you want one team accountable for the whole channel.</p><h3>What should I prepare before contacting agencies?</h3><p>Your one-sentence goal, monthly budget, channel link, examples of videos you like, and what you can provide yourself.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-to-hire-a-youtube-agency'
  and body_json::text like '%ou can filter by service and industry an%';

-- how-to-start-a-youtube-ad-agency
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To start a YouTube ad agency, learn to run YouTube campaigns through Google Ads, pick one niche, get one client result you can show, then package that into a clear offer with a starting price. Most new agencies begin as one person with a laptop, a Google Ads account and a handful of clients."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The business model is simple. Clients pay you a monthly fee to plan, launch and improve their YouTube ads, and pay Google directly for the ad spend. Most agencies we reviewed start at around $1,000 a month or per project, so a handful of clients can support a one-person agency."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What separates agencies that last is results: a lower cost per lead or a higher return on ad spend for clients in one industry, shown with real numbers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9 steps to start a YouTube ad agency"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Learn to run YouTube campaigns properly"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads run through Google Ads. Learn the campaign types, bidding options, audience targeting, and above all conversion tracking. Google's free Skillshop courses cover the basics, and its video advertising certification is a credible first credential. Then practice with a small budget of your own before you touch a client's money."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Learn what makes a video ad work"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most YouTube ad results come down to the creative: a hook in the first five seconds, a clear offer and a clear next step. You don't need to film, but you need to write ad scripts and brief editors well. Agencies that can only press buttons in Google Ads compete on price; agencies that improve the creative compete on results."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Pick one niche"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"YouTube ads for dental clinics\" is easier to sell than \"YouTube ads for everyone\". A niche lets you reuse what works, speak the client's language and build case studies that convince the next client. Choose an industry where customers are worth a lot, so clients can afford ads and your fee: clinics, law firms, SaaS, real estate, coaching and e-commerce are common choices."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Get your first result"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your first case study matters more than your website. Offer a reduced fee to one or two businesses in your niche in exchange for permission to share their numbers. Track cost per lead, cost per sale or return on ad spend from day one."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Package a clear offer"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Define what the monthly fee includes: number of ad versions tested, campaigns managed, reporting frequency and calls. Clients compare offers, so specific is easier to buy than vague."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Set your pricing"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pick a model (see the pricing table below) and publish a starting price. Hidden pricing makes busy owners leave before the first call."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Set up the business basics"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Register the business, open a business bank account, and use a simple client contract that covers scope, fees, notice period and ownership. Clients should own their Google Ads account and grant you access, which builds trust and protects you both."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Build your toolkit"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You'll need Google Ads, Google Analytics, a way to track calls and form leads, a reporting dashboard, and a reliable video editor, freelance or in-house. Start lean and add tools only when a client needs them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Find clients"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The channels that tend to work for new agencies: referrals from your first clients, direct outreach to businesses in your niche with a short video audit of their current ads, partnerships with web designers and SEO agencies who don't run video ads, and agency directories where buyers are already comparing options."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to price your YouTube ads services"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Model"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How it works"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical range"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good for new agencies?"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Flat monthly retainer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Same fee every month"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $5,000 for small and mid-sized clients"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes: predictable income, simple to sell"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Percentage of ad spend"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A share of what the client spends"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10% to 20% of spend, often with a minimum fee"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Later, once clients spend $10,000+ a month"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per project or per video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One price per launch or ad video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $3,000 per project"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, as a trial before a retainer"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Performance-based"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid per lead or sale"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Varies"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Risky: tracking disputes are common"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ranges from our "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency cost guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":". Most paid-ads agencies bill flat retainers; in one survey of 146 firms, about 83% did ("},{"type":"text","text":"TeamAI summary of Credo data","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://teamai.com/?p=2005"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mistakes new agencies make"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Taking clients with tiny ad budgets. Under a few hundred dollars a month, campaigns can't gather enough data to improve."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Running ads in the agency's own account. When the client leaves, the dispute starts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reporting views instead of leads or sales."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Serving every industry, so no case study is convincing to anyone."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Underpricing to win the first clients, then being unable to raise prices."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does it cost to start a YouTube ad agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Very little. Google Ads and its training are free, and most tools have free tiers. The main costs are a small practice ad budget, business registration, and your time."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do I need a certification?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No, but Google's video advertising certification from Skillshop shows clients you know the platform. Case studies matter more."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many clients do I need to earn a full-time income?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It depends on your prices and costs. At $1,500 a month per client, five clients bring in $7,500 a month before costs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can I start a YouTube ad agency with no experience?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can, but run campaigns on your own or a friend's business first. Your first paying client should come after you can show at least one real result."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I also offer video production?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It helps, because many small businesses don't have ad-ready videos. Many agencies partner with freelance editors rather than hiring at the start."}]},{"type":"paragraph"}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>To start a YouTube ad agency, learn to run YouTube campaigns through Google Ads, pick one niche, get one client result you can show, then package that into a clear offer with a starting price. Most new agencies begin as one person with a laptop, a Google Ads account and a handful of clients.</p><p>The business model is simple. Clients pay you a monthly fee to plan, launch and improve their YouTube ads, and pay Google directly for the ad spend. Most agencies we reviewed start at around $1,000 a month or per project, so a handful of clients can support a one-person agency.</p><p>What separates agencies that last is results: a lower cost per lead or a higher return on ad spend for clients in one industry, shown with real numbers.</p><h2>9 steps to start a YouTube ad agency</h2><h3>1. Learn to run YouTube campaigns properly</h3><p>YouTube ads run through Google Ads. Learn the campaign types, bidding options, audience targeting, and above all conversion tracking. Google's free Skillshop courses cover the basics, and its video advertising certification is a credible first credential. Then practice with a small budget of your own before you touch a client's money.</p><h3>2. Learn what makes a video ad work</h3><p>Most YouTube ad results come down to the creative: a hook in the first five seconds, a clear offer and a clear next step. You don't need to film, but you need to write ad scripts and brief editors well. Agencies that can only press buttons in Google Ads compete on price; agencies that improve the creative compete on results.</p><h3>3. Pick one niche</h3><p>"YouTube ads for dental clinics" is easier to sell than "YouTube ads for everyone". A niche lets you reuse what works, speak the client's language and build case studies that convince the next client. Choose an industry where customers are worth a lot, so clients can afford ads and your fee: clinics, law firms, SaaS, real estate, coaching and e-commerce are common choices.</p><h3>4. Get your first result</h3><p>Your first case study matters more than your website. Offer a reduced fee to one or two businesses in your niche in exchange for permission to share their numbers. Track cost per lead, cost per sale or return on ad spend from day one.</p><h3>5. Package a clear offer</h3><p>Define what the monthly fee includes: number of ad versions tested, campaigns managed, reporting frequency and calls. Clients compare offers, so specific is easier to buy than vague.</p><h3>6. Set your pricing</h3><p>Pick a model (see the pricing table below) and publish a starting price. Hidden pricing makes busy owners leave before the first call.</p><h3>7. Set up the business basics</h3><p>Register the business, open a business bank account, and use a simple client contract that covers scope, fees, notice period and ownership. Clients should own their Google Ads account and grant you access, which builds trust and protects you both.</p><h3>8. Build your toolkit</h3><p>You'll need Google Ads, Google Analytics, a way to track calls and form leads, a reporting dashboard, and a reliable video editor, freelance or in-house. Start lean and add tools only when a client needs them.</p><h3>9. Find clients</h3><p>The channels that tend to work for new agencies: referrals from your first clients, direct outreach to businesses in your niche with a short video audit of their current ads, partnerships with web designers and SEO agencies who don't run video ads, and agency directories where buyers are already comparing options.</p><h2>How to price your YouTube ads services</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>How it works</th><th>Typical range</th><th>Good for new agencies?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Flat monthly retainer</td><td>Same fee every month</td><td>$1,000 to $5,000 for small and mid-sized clients</td><td>Yes: predictable income, simple to sell</td></tr><tr><td>Percentage of ad spend</td><td>A share of what the client spends</td><td>10% to 20% of spend, often with a minimum fee</td><td>Later, once clients spend $10,000+ a month</td></tr><tr><td>Per project or per video</td><td>One price per launch or ad video</td><td>$1,000 to $3,000 per project</td><td>Yes, as a trial before a retainer</td></tr><tr><td>Performance-based</td><td>Paid per lead or sale</td><td>Varies</td><td>Risky: tracking disputes are common</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Ranges from our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agency cost guide</a>. Most paid-ads agencies bill flat retainers; in one survey of 146 firms, about 83% did (<a href="https://teamai.com/?p=2005">TeamAI summary of Credo data</a>).</p><h2>Mistakes new agencies make</h2><ul><li><p>Taking clients with tiny ad budgets. Under a few hundred dollars a month, campaigns can't gather enough data to improve.</p></li><li><p>Running ads in the agency's own account. When the client leaves, the dispute starts.</p></li><li><p>Reporting views instead of leads or sales.</p></li><li><p>Serving every industry, so no case study is convincing to anyone.</p></li><li><p>Underpricing to win the first clients, then being unable to raise prices.</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does it cost to start a YouTube ad agency?</h3><p>Very little. Google Ads and its training are free, and most tools have free tiers. The main costs are a small practice ad budget, business registration, and your time.</p><h3>Do I need a certification?</h3><p>No, but Google's video advertising certification from Skillshop shows clients you know the platform. Case studies matter more.</p><h3>How many clients do I need to earn a full-time income?</h3><p>It depends on your prices and costs. At $1,500 a month per client, five clients bring in $7,500 a month before costs.</p><h3>Can I start a YouTube ad agency with no experience?</h3><p>You can, but run campaigns on your own or a friend's business first. Your first paying client should come after you can show at least one real result.</p><h3>Should I also offer video production?</h3><p>It helps, because many small businesses don't have ad-ready videos. Many agencies partner with freelance editors rather than hiring at the start.</p><p></p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-to-start-a-youtube-ad-agency'
  and body_json::text like '% pay Google directly for the ad spend. A%';

-- talent-agencies-for-new-youtubers
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, there are talent agencies and creator management companies that work with new YouTubers, but most new channels don't need one yet. Talent agencies mainly sell brand deals, and brands pay for audiences. Until you have steady views, an agency has little to sell and will either turn you down or take a cut for very little."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What helps a starting channel more is skill-specific help: an editor, a thumbnail designer or a one-off channel audit, paid by the job with no share of your future income. Bring in a manager or agency once brand inquiries start arriving faster than you can handle them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The four kinds of help, and what each takes"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Talent agency\" gets used for several different businesses. They work differently and charge differently:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What they do"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How they're paid"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical cut"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Talent agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finds and negotiates brand deals, sometimes TV, books or speaking"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Commission on deals they bring"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Around 20% to 30% for full-service talent management"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Freelance manager"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One person handling your inbox, deals and scheduling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Commission"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Around 10% to 15%"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"MCN (multi-channel network)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Groups many channels; may offer rights management, tools, sponsorship sales"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of your channel revenue"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roughly 10% to 40%, often including ad revenue"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Growth agency or freelancer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Editing, thumbnails, strategy, audits"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Flat fee per job or per month"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No share of income"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Commission ranges: "},{"type":"text","text":"EarnifyHub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://earnifyhub.com/creator-economy/creator-agency-vs-independent-2026"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for talent managers, "},{"type":"text","text":"Interspace Music","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://interspacemusic.com/blog/youtube-mcn-revenue-split-fees-contracts-what-to-watch-for/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for MCNs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why MCNs cost more than they look"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube pays creators 55% of net ad revenue on long-form videos. Joining an MCN doesn't change that split; the network takes its cut from your 55% before it reaches you. A 30% MCN cut on $1,000 of ad revenue leaves you $700, before any separate commission on sponsorships."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most of what MCNs once offered, like analytics, copyright tools and direct monetization, is now available to any creator in the YouTube Partner Program at no extra cost."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The real first milestone: the Partner Program"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Before you think about agencies, aim to join the YouTube Partner Program. The bar is 1,000 subscribers plus either 4,000 public watch hours in a year or 10 million Shorts views in 90 days. From February 1, 2027, new applicants will need 8,000 watch hours or 20 million Shorts views instead; channels already in the program aren't affected. If you're close to the current bar, it's worth pushing to qualify before that date."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What a startup channel should do instead"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel stage"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What actually helps"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical cost"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0 to 1,000 subscribers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Post consistently; get feedback from other creators; one "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" after 10 to 20 videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free to a few hundred dollars"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000 to 10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freelance "},{"type":"text","text":"editor"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"thumbnail designer"},{"type":"text","text":" to free up time; join the Partner Program"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Paid per video"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10,000 to 50,000, first brand emails"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Handle deals yourself with a simple rate card; consider a freelance manager if deals arrive weekly"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"10% to 15% commission if you hire one"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"50,000+ with regular brand demand"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A talent agency or full manager"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"20% to 30% of deals"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two other free options: YouTube's own creator marketplace lets eligible Partner Program creators receive brand inquiries inside YouTube Studio, and creator communities and Discord groups often share rates and vetted contacts."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Red flags to walk away from"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They ask you to pay an upfront \"signing\" or \"onboarding\" fee to represent you."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They promise a number of subscribers or views. Nobody can guarantee that."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They contact you first with a generic message when your channel is tiny. Real agencies rarely chase channels with no brand demand."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The contract takes a percentage of all income, including ad revenue, memberships and merch, not only the deals they bring."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The contract runs for years with no exit clause, or claims ownership of your channel or content."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They want your channel password instead of access through YouTube's permissions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They call themselves a \"network\" but can't explain what they'll do in your first 90 days."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Checklist before you sign anything"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which income does the commission cover: only deals they bring, or everything?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What's the commission rate, and does it drop as you grow?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long is the contract, and how do you exit early?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do they keep earning on deals after the contract ends, and for how long?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who owns the channel, the videos and the audience data? (It should be you.)"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What will they deliver in the first 90 days, in writing?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can you speak to two creators they currently represent at your size?"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Has a lawyer or an experienced creator read the contract?"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Are there talent agencies for YouTubers starting out?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, but few take on very small channels, because their income comes from brand deals. Channels with a few thousand subscribers usually get more from paying for editing or an audit than from giving away a percentage."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many subscribers do you need to get a talent agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There's no fixed number. What matters is steady views and brand interest. Many creators start talking to managers once they get regular sponsorship inquiries, often somewhere past 10,000 to 50,000 subscribers in a niche brands care about."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is an MCN worth it for a small channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rarely. MCNs take a share of revenue, often including ad revenue, and most of their old tools are now free to Partner Program members. Read any MCN contract closely before signing."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do talent agencies help you grow?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mostly no. Talent agencies sell your existing audience to brands. Growth comes from better videos, titles and thumbnails, which is what editing, packaging and audit specialists help with."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>Yes, there are talent agencies and creator management companies that work with new YouTubers, but most new channels don't need one yet. Talent agencies mainly sell brand deals, and brands pay for audiences. Until you have steady views, an agency has little to sell and will either turn you down or take a cut for very little.</p><p>What helps a starting channel more is skill-specific help: an editor, a thumbnail designer or a one-off channel audit, paid by the job with no share of your future income. Bring in a manager or agency once brand inquiries start arriving faster than you can handle them.</p><h2>The four kinds of help, and what each takes</h2><p>"Talent agency" gets used for several different businesses. They work differently and charge differently:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What they do</th><th>How they're paid</th><th>Typical cut</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Talent agency</td><td>Finds and negotiates brand deals, sometimes TV, books or speaking</td><td>Commission on deals they bring</td><td>Around 20% to 30% for full-service talent management</td></tr><tr><td>Freelance manager</td><td>One person handling your inbox, deals and scheduling</td><td>Commission</td><td>Around 10% to 15%</td></tr><tr><td>MCN (multi-channel network)</td><td>Groups many channels; may offer rights management, tools, sponsorship sales</td><td>Share of your channel revenue</td><td>Roughly 10% to 40%, often including ad revenue</td></tr><tr><td>Growth agency or freelancer</td><td>Editing, thumbnails, strategy, audits</td><td>Flat fee per job or per month</td><td>No share of income</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Commission ranges: <a href="https://earnifyhub.com/creator-economy/creator-agency-vs-independent-2026">EarnifyHub</a> for talent managers, <a href="https://interspacemusic.com/blog/youtube-mcn-revenue-split-fees-contracts-what-to-watch-for/">Interspace Music</a> for MCNs.</p><h3>Why MCNs cost more than they look</h3><p>YouTube pays creators 55% of net ad revenue on long-form videos. Joining an MCN doesn't change that split; the network takes its cut from your 55% before it reaches you. A 30% MCN cut on $1,000 of ad revenue leaves you $700, before any separate commission on sponsorships.</p><p>Most of what MCNs once offered, like analytics, copyright tools and direct monetization, is now available to any creator in the YouTube Partner Program at no extra cost.</p><h3>The real first milestone: the Partner Program</h3><p>Before you think about agencies, aim to join the YouTube Partner Program. The bar is 1,000 subscribers plus either 4,000 public watch hours in a year or 10 million Shorts views in 90 days. From February 1, 2027, new applicants will need 8,000 watch hours or 20 million Shorts views instead; channels already in the program aren't affected. If you're close to the current bar, it's worth pushing to qualify before that date.</p><h2>What a startup channel should do instead</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Channel stage</th><th>What actually helps</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 to 1,000 subscribers</td><td>Post consistently; get feedback from other creators; one channel audit after 10 to 20 videos</td><td>Free to a few hundred dollars</td></tr><tr><td>1,000 to 10,000</td><td>A freelance editor or thumbnail designer to free up time; join the Partner Program</td><td>Paid per video</td></tr><tr><td>10,000 to 50,000, first brand emails</td><td>Handle deals yourself with a simple rate card; consider a freelance manager if deals arrive weekly</td><td>10% to 15% commission if you hire one</td></tr><tr><td>50,000+ with regular brand demand</td><td>A talent agency or full manager</td><td>20% to 30% of deals</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Two other free options: YouTube's own creator marketplace lets eligible Partner Program creators receive brand inquiries inside YouTube Studio, and creator communities and Discord groups often share rates and vetted contacts.</p><h2>Red flags to walk away from</h2><ul><li><p>They ask you to pay an upfront "signing" or "onboarding" fee to represent you.</p></li><li><p>They promise a number of subscribers or views. Nobody can guarantee that.</p></li><li><p>They contact you first with a generic message when your channel is tiny. Real agencies rarely chase channels with no brand demand.</p></li><li><p>The contract takes a percentage of all income, including ad revenue, memberships and merch, not only the deals they bring.</p></li><li><p>The contract runs for years with no exit clause, or claims ownership of your channel or content.</p></li><li><p>They want your channel password instead of access through YouTube's permissions.</p></li><li><p>They call themselves a "network" but can't explain what they'll do in your first 90 days.</p></li></ul><h2>Checklist before you sign anything</h2><ul><li><p>Which income does the commission cover: only deals they bring, or everything?</p></li><li><p>What's the commission rate, and does it drop as you grow?</p></li><li><p>How long is the contract, and how do you exit early?</p></li><li><p>Do they keep earning on deals after the contract ends, and for how long?</p></li><li><p>Who owns the channel, the videos and the audience data? (It should be you.)</p></li><li><p>What will they deliver in the first 90 days, in writing?</p></li><li><p>Can you speak to two creators they currently represent at your size?</p></li><li><p>Has a lawyer or an experienced creator read the contract?</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Are there talent agencies for YouTubers starting out?</h3><p>Yes, but few take on very small channels, because their income comes from brand deals. Channels with a few thousand subscribers usually get more from paying for editing or an audit than from giving away a percentage.</p><h3>How many subscribers do you need to get a talent agency?</h3><p>There's no fixed number. What matters is steady views and brand interest. Many creators start talking to managers once they get regular sponsorship inquiries, often somewhere past 10,000 to 50,000 subscribers in a niche brands care about.</p><h3>Is an MCN worth it for a small channel?</h3><p>Rarely. MCNs take a share of revenue, often including ad revenue, and most of their old tools are now free to Partner Program members. Read any MCN contract closely before signing.</p><h3>Do talent agencies help you grow?</h3><p>Mostly no. Talent agencies sell your existing audience to brands. Growth comes from better videos, titles and thumbnails, which is what editing, packaging and audit specialists help with.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'talent-agencies-for-new-youtubers'
  and body_json::text like '%Where can I find help for a new YouTube %';

-- when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hire a YouTube agency when YouTube has started working for you and you can't keep up, or when you want results that need skills you don't have, like running video ads with proper tracking."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wait if you haven't posted at least a handful of videos yourself, can't commit a budget for three months, or don't yet know who your customer is. An agency speeds up a channel that has a direction. It can't pick the direction for a business that hasn't."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8 signs it's time to hire"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. You keep missing your upload schedule"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You planned one video a week and you've posted three in two months. Consistency matters on YouTube because each upload gives the algorithm and your audience a new reason to come back. If filming and editing keep losing to client work, a channel management or editing agency fixes the bottleneck directly."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. YouTube already brings in customers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If people say \"I found you on YouTube\" on sales calls, you have proof the channel works. That's the best time to invest, because you're scaling something real instead of guessing."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Your views have stalled for three months or more"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A flat line after early growth usually means a packaging problem (titles and thumbnails), a topic problem, or both. A "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" is a cheap way to find out which before you commit to a retainer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. You want to run YouTube ads and have never set up conversion tracking"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ads without tracking spend money you can't measure. If \"conversion action\" and \"audience segments\" are new words to you, a "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency"},{"type":"text","text":" will save more than it costs once you're spending around $1,500 a month or more."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Your time is worth more than the agency's fee"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add up the hours you spend scripting, filming, editing and uploading each month, then multiply by what an hour of your time earns. If that number is higher than a $1,000 to $3,000 monthly fee, the maths favors hiring."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. You're launching something with a deadline"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A product launch, a new clinic location or a course enrollment window doesn't leave time to learn YouTube ads by trial and error. Agencies are most useful when timing matters."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Your videos look and sound amateur next to competitors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If competitors' videos have clear audio, tight editing and strong thumbnails, and yours don't, viewers notice in the first few seconds. A "},{"type":"text","text":"video production"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"editing"},{"type":"text","text":" agency closes that gap fastest."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. You've hired freelancers and become the project manager"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One editor, one thumbnail designer and one scriptwriter can work, but someone has to brief and chase them. If that someone is you and it's eating your week, an agency puts those roles under one contract."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When you should wait"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You haven't made a single video yet. Post five to ten yourself first, even on a phone. You'll learn what your customers ask about and what you're comfortable saying on camera, which makes any agency brief far better."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can't fund three months. Most YouTube work needs about three months of testing before results settle. Stopping after one month means paying for setup and walking away before the payoff."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can't describe your ideal customer in one sentence. An agency will ask this in the first call. If you can't answer, work on it before you spend money."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your ad budget is under a few hundred dollars a month. The agency fee would be larger than the ads. Run a small campaign yourself or pay a freelancer for setup."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You want someone to \"go viral\" for you. No agency can promise that. Look for one that talks about leads, sales and watch time instead."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Which agency to hire at each stage"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your stage"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What usually holds you back"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agency type to start with"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Starting out, under 20 videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unclear topics, slow editing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Freelance editor, or a "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" after 10 videos"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Posting regularly, views flat"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Titles, thumbnails, topic choice"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thumbnails and packaging"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel brings in leads, no time"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Upload consistency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel management"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ready to pay for growth"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reach beyond your own subscribers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"text","text":"influencer marketing"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quick readiness checklist"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you can tick five or more, you're ready to talk to agencies:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I know the one result I want from YouTube (leads, sales, subscribers or watch time)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can describe my ideal customer in one sentence."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've published at least a few videos, or I have an existing video to use in ads."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I can commit a budget for at least three months."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I know roughly what a new customer is worth to my business."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Someone on my side can review videos and reply to the agency within a few days."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I will own the YouTube channel and the Google Ads account."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do small businesses need a YouTube agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not always. Many small businesses do well with a freelancer for editing and the owner on camera. An agency makes more sense once YouTube brings in customers or you plan to run ads."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does it cost to hire a YouTube agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most agencies we reviewed start around $1,000 a month or per project. See our full guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency costs","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for pricing models and sample budgets."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long should I give an agency before judging results?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About three months for ads and three to six months for organic growth. Agree on the numbers you'll judge them by before you start."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I hire an agency or a freelancer first?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freelancer if you need one skill, such as editing. An agency if you need several skills working together, or someone accountable for the overall result."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What should I ask a YouTube agency before hiring?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask for case studies from your industry with real numbers, who will work on your account, what's included in the fee, the minimum contract, and who owns the channel and ad account."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>Hire a YouTube agency when YouTube has started working for you and you can't keep up, or when you want results that need skills you don't have, like running video ads with proper tracking.</p><p>Wait if you haven't posted at least a handful of videos yourself, can't commit a budget for three months, or don't yet know who your customer is. An agency speeds up a channel that has a direction. It can't pick the direction for a business that hasn't.</p><h2>8 signs it's time to hire</h2><h3>1. You keep missing your upload schedule</h3><p>You planned one video a week and you've posted three in two months. Consistency matters on YouTube because each upload gives the algorithm and your audience a new reason to come back. If filming and editing keep losing to client work, a channel management or editing agency fixes the bottleneck directly.</p><h3>2. YouTube already brings in customers</h3><p>If people say "I found you on YouTube" on sales calls, you have proof the channel works. That's the best time to invest, because you're scaling something real instead of guessing.</p><h3>3. Your views have stalled for three months or more</h3><p>A flat line after early growth usually means a packaging problem (titles and thumbnails), a topic problem, or both. A channel audit is a cheap way to find out which before you commit to a retainer.</p><h3>4. You want to run YouTube ads and have never set up conversion tracking</h3><p>Ads without tracking spend money you can't measure. If "conversion action" and "audience segments" are new words to you, a YouTube ads agency will save more than it costs once you're spending around $1,500 a month or more.</p><h3>5. Your time is worth more than the agency's fee</h3><p>Add up the hours you spend scripting, filming, editing and uploading each month, then multiply by what an hour of your time earns. If that number is higher than a $1,000 to $3,000 monthly fee, the maths favors hiring.</p><h3>6. You're launching something with a deadline</h3><p>A product launch, a new clinic location or a course enrollment window doesn't leave time to learn YouTube ads by trial and error. Agencies are most useful when timing matters.</p><h3>7. Your videos look and sound amateur next to competitors</h3><p>If competitors' videos have clear audio, tight editing and strong thumbnails, and yours don't, viewers notice in the first few seconds. A video production or editing agency closes that gap fastest.</p><h3>8. You've hired freelancers and become the project manager</h3><p>One editor, one thumbnail designer and one scriptwriter can work, but someone has to brief and chase them. If that someone is you and it's eating your week, an agency puts those roles under one contract.</p><h2>When you should wait</h2><ul><li><p>You haven't made a single video yet. Post five to ten yourself first, even on a phone. You'll learn what your customers ask about and what you're comfortable saying on camera, which makes any agency brief far better.</p></li><li><p>You can't fund three months. Most YouTube work needs about three months of testing before results settle. Stopping after one month means paying for setup and walking away before the payoff.</p></li><li><p>You can't describe your ideal customer in one sentence. An agency will ask this in the first call. If you can't answer, work on it before you spend money.</p></li><li><p>Your ad budget is under a few hundred dollars a month. The agency fee would be larger than the ads. Run a small campaign yourself or pay a freelancer for setup.</p></li><li><p>You want someone to "go viral" for you. No agency can promise that. Look for one that talks about leads, sales and watch time instead.</p></li></ul><h2>Which agency to hire at each stage</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Your stage</th><th>What usually holds you back</th><th>Agency type to start with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Starting out, under 20 videos</td><td>Unclear topics, slow editing</td><td>Freelance editor, or a channel audit after 10 videos</td></tr><tr><td>Posting regularly, views flat</td><td>Titles, thumbnails, topic choice</td><td>Thumbnails and packaging or YouTube SEO</td></tr><tr><td>Channel brings in leads, no time</td><td>Upload consistency</td><td>Channel management</td></tr><tr><td>Ready to pay for growth</td><td>Reach beyond your own subscribers</td><td>YouTube ads or influencer marketing</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>Quick readiness checklist</h2><p>If you can tick five or more, you're ready to talk to agencies:</p><ul><li><p>I know the one result I want from YouTube (leads, sales, subscribers or watch time).</p></li><li><p>I can describe my ideal customer in one sentence.</p></li><li><p>I've published at least a few videos, or I have an existing video to use in ads.</p></li><li><p>I can commit a budget for at least three months.</p></li><li><p>I know roughly what a new customer is worth to my business.</p></li><li><p>Someone on my side can review videos and reply to the agency within a few days.</p></li><li><p>I will own the YouTube channel and the Google Ads account.</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do small businesses need a YouTube agency?</h3><p>Not always. Many small businesses do well with a freelancer for editing and the owner on camera. An agency makes more sense once YouTube brings in customers or you plan to run ads.</p><h3>How much does it cost to hire a YouTube agency?</h3><p>Most agencies we reviewed start around $1,000 a month or per project. See our full guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agency costs</a> for pricing models and sample budgets.</p><h3>How long should I give an agency before judging results?</h3><p>About three months for ads and three to six months for organic growth. Agree on the numbers you'll judge them by before you start.</p><h3>Should I hire an agency or a freelancer first?</h3><p>A freelancer if you need one skill, such as editing. An agency if you need several skills working together, or someone accountable for the overall result.</p><h3>What should I ask a YouTube agency before hiring?</h3><p>Ask for case studies from your industry with real numbers, who will work on your account, what's included in the fee, the minimum contract, and who owns the channel and ad account.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency'
  and body_json::text like '%When you''re ready, you can compare agenc%';

-- youtube-agency
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A YouTube agency is a company that grows a business or creator on YouTube for a fee. Depending on the agency, that means running YouTube ads, making videos, optimizing them for search, or managing the whole channel from strategy to monthly reports."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most YouTube agencies focus on one or two of those jobs. A clinic that wants new patients from ads needs a different agency than a creator who wants an editor and a thumbnail designer. Knowing which type you need is most of the work of hiring one."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This guide covers what YouTube agencies do, the six main types, how they compare with freelancers and in-house teams, and what they cost."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What a YouTube agency does day to day"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The work varies by agency, but it usually falls into five areas:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strategy. Deciding who the channel or ads are for, which topics to cover, and which numbers count as success (leads, sales, subscribers or watch time)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Content. Writing scripts, filming, editing, and designing thumbnails and titles."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Optimization. Researching what people search for on YouTube, then writing titles, descriptions, chapters and tags that match."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Distribution. Running YouTube ads, cutting long videos into Shorts, and arranging creator partnerships."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reporting. A monthly report on what grew, what didn't, and what changes next month."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A small agency might do only items 2 and 3 for creators. A paid-media agency might do only 1, 4 and 5 for businesses that already have videos. Full-service agencies cover all five."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The six main types of YouTube agencies"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What they do"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who hires them"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical result they're judged on"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad scripts, targeting, conversion tracking, budget management"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Businesses selling a product or service"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost per lead, return on ad spend"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keyword research, titles, descriptions, topic planning"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Businesses and creators who want search traffic"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Views from search, ranking for target terms"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel management agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strategy, uploads, optimization and monthly reporting"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Busy owners who want the channel handled"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subscriber and watch-time growth, leads"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video production agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Scripts, filming and editing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brands without in-house video skills"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finished videos on time and on brief"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Influencer marketing agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finds creators, negotiates deals, manages sponsored videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brands that want to reach an existing audience"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cost per acquisition from sponsored videos"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creator or talent management (including MCNs)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brand deals, rights management, career support for creators"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Creators, especially once they have an audience"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sponsorship income, channel revenue"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There are also narrower specialists: "},{"type":"text","text":"video editing"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"scriptwriting"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"thumbnails and packaging"},{"type":"text","text":", "},{"type":"text","text":"Shorts"},{"type":"text","text":" and "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audits"},{"type":"text","text":". These are useful when one weak spot is holding a channel back, for example low click-through rates from weak thumbnails."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first five types mostly work for businesses. The sixth works for creators, and it's the one people usually mean by a \"YouTuber agency\". It often takes a share of the creator's income instead of a monthly fee."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube agency vs freelancer vs in-house team"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Option"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monthly cost"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strengths"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Weak spots"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Freelancer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often $500 to $3,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cheap, flexible, good for one skill like editing or thumbnails"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One person, so holidays and sick days stop the work; you manage the strategy"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agency"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often $1,000 to $10,000+"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Several skills under one contract, a process, someone accountable for results"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Costs more; you may get a junior team after the pitch"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In-house team"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$6,000+ for even one full-time editor"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Knows your business best, fastest turnaround"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hiring takes months; one editor rarely covers strategy, ads and design"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The in-house figure is based on US wages: the mean pay for film and video editors is around $77,000 a year, per MarketerHire's reading of Bureau of Labor Statistics data. The same source suggests building in-house only once you spend $30,000 or more a month on YouTube and publish weekly."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A common path is freelancer first, agency once YouTube is bringing in money, then in-house once the channel is a core sales channel."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How YouTube agencies charge"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most charge one of four ways: a flat monthly retainer, a percentage of ad spend (ads agencies only), a price per video or project, or a share of revenue (common with creator management)."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rough monthly ranges by type, drawn from published agency pricing guides."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical monthly fee"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Titles and thumbnails only"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$500 to $2,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video production or editing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2,000 to $5,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO and optimization"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$3,000 to $8,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads management"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $12,000, plus ad spend"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Full-service growth"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10,000 to $30,000"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Humble & Brag publishes its ranges in euros; the dollar figures above round them. Many agencies we reviewed start around $1,000. For a detailed breakdown of ads pricing, see our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency costs","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to pick the right YouTube agency"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Name the one result you want. \"More leads from YouTube ads\" points to an ads agency. \"A channel that posts every week without me\" points to channel management."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shortlist agencies that have worked in your industry. A dental clinic and a gaming creator need very different hooks, thumbnails and targeting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask for case studies with real numbers and links to the channels, then check those channels yourself. Look at views per video over time, not subscriber count."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Compare at least three quotes on the same terms: fee, what's included, minimum contract, and who owns the channel and ad account."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start small. A one to three month trial tells you more than any sales call."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What does a YouTube agency do?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It grows a channel or business on YouTube through some mix of strategy, video production, optimization, ads and reporting. Most agencies specialize in one or two of these."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is a YouTube agency the same as an MCN?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. An MCN (multi-channel network) is a type of creator management company that partners with many channels, usually for a share of their revenue. Most YouTube agencies work for businesses and charge a fee."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do YouTube agencies work with small channels?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Many do, especially editing, thumbnail and channel audit specialists. Ads agencies usually want a minimum ad budget, so very small businesses may be better off starting with a freelancer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long does it take to see results?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ads can show early data within weeks. Organic growth from SEO and regular uploads usually takes three to six months to judge."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is YT Agencies a YouTube agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. YT Agencies is an independent publication of YouTube guides. It isn't affiliated with YouTube or Google."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>A YouTube agency is a company that grows a business or creator on YouTube for a fee. Depending on the agency, that means running YouTube ads, making videos, optimizing them for search, or managing the whole channel from strategy to monthly reports.</p><p>Most YouTube agencies focus on one or two of those jobs. A clinic that wants new patients from ads needs a different agency than a creator who wants an editor and a thumbnail designer. Knowing which type you need is most of the work of hiring one.</p><p>This guide covers what YouTube agencies do, the six main types, how they compare with freelancers and in-house teams, and what they cost.</p><h2>What a YouTube agency does day to day</h2><p>The work varies by agency, but it usually falls into five areas:</p><ol><li><p>Strategy. Deciding who the channel or ads are for, which topics to cover, and which numbers count as success (leads, sales, subscribers or watch time).</p></li><li><p>Content. Writing scripts, filming, editing, and designing thumbnails and titles.</p></li><li><p>Optimization. Researching what people search for on YouTube, then writing titles, descriptions, chapters and tags that match.</p></li><li><p>Distribution. Running YouTube ads, cutting long videos into Shorts, and arranging creator partnerships.</p></li><li><p>Reporting. A monthly report on what grew, what didn't, and what changes next month.</p></li></ol><p>A small agency might do only items 2 and 3 for creators. A paid-media agency might do only 1, 4 and 5 for businesses that already have videos. Full-service agencies cover all five.</p><h2>The six main types of YouTube agencies</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>What they do</th><th>Who hires them</th><th>Typical result they're judged on</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>YouTube ads agency</td><td>Ad scripts, targeting, conversion tracking, budget management</td><td>Businesses selling a product or service</td><td>Cost per lead, return on ad spend</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube SEO agency</td><td>Keyword research, titles, descriptions, topic planning</td><td>Businesses and creators who want search traffic</td><td>Views from search, ranking for target terms</td></tr><tr><td>Channel management agency</td><td>Strategy, uploads, optimization and monthly reporting</td><td>Busy owners who want the channel handled</td><td>Subscriber and watch-time growth, leads</td></tr><tr><td>Video production agency</td><td>Scripts, filming and editing</td><td>Brands without in-house video skills</td><td>Finished videos on time and on brief</td></tr><tr><td>Influencer marketing agency</td><td>Finds creators, negotiates deals, manages sponsored videos</td><td>Brands that want to reach an existing audience</td><td>Cost per acquisition from sponsored videos</td></tr><tr><td>Creator or talent management (including MCNs)</td><td>Brand deals, rights management, career support for creators</td><td>Creators, especially once they have an audience</td><td>Sponsorship income, channel revenue</td></tr></tbody></table><p>There are also narrower specialists: video editing, scriptwriting, thumbnails and packaging, Shorts and channel audits. These are useful when one weak spot is holding a channel back, for example low click-through rates from weak thumbnails.</p><p>The first five types mostly work for businesses. The sixth works for creators, and it's the one people usually mean by a "YouTuber agency". It often takes a share of the creator's income instead of a monthly fee.</p><h2>YouTube agency vs freelancer vs in-house team</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Monthly cost</th><th>Strengths</th><th>Weak spots</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Freelancer</td><td>Often $500 to $3,000</td><td>Cheap, flexible, good for one skill like editing or thumbnails</td><td>One person, so holidays and sick days stop the work; you manage the strategy</td></tr><tr><td>Agency</td><td>Often $1,000 to $10,000+</td><td>Several skills under one contract, a process, someone accountable for results</td><td>Costs more; you may get a junior team after the pitch</td></tr><tr><td>In-house team</td><td>$6,000+ for even one full-time editor</td><td>Knows your business best, fastest turnaround</td><td>Hiring takes months; one editor rarely covers strategy, ads and design</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The in-house figure is based on US wages: the mean pay for film and video editors is around $77,000 a year, per MarketerHire's reading of Bureau of Labor Statistics data. The same source suggests building in-house only once you spend $30,000 or more a month on YouTube and publish weekly.</p><p>A common path is freelancer first, agency once YouTube is bringing in money, then in-house once the channel is a core sales channel.</p><h2>How YouTube agencies charge</h2><p>Most charge one of four ways: a flat monthly retainer, a percentage of ad spend (ads agencies only), a price per video or project, or a share of revenue (common with creator management).</p><p>Rough monthly ranges by type, drawn from published agency pricing guides.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Typical monthly fee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Titles and thumbnails only</td><td>$500 to $2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Video production or editing</td><td>$2,000 to $5,000</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube SEO and optimization</td><td>$3,000 to $8,000</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube ads management</td><td>$1,000 to $12,000, plus ad spend</td></tr><tr><td>Full-service growth</td><td>$10,000 to $30,000</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Humble &amp; Brag publishes its ranges in euros; the dollar figures above round them. Many agencies we reviewed start around $1,000. For a detailed breakdown of ads pricing, see our guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agency costs</a>.</p><h2>How to pick the right YouTube agency</h2><ol><li><p>Name the one result you want. "More leads from YouTube ads" points to an ads agency. "A channel that posts every week without me" points to channel management.</p></li><li><p>Shortlist agencies that have worked in your industry. A dental clinic and a gaming creator need very different hooks, thumbnails and targeting.</p></li><li><p>Ask for case studies with real numbers and links to the channels, then check those channels yourself. Look at views per video over time, not subscriber count.</p></li><li><p>Compare at least three quotes on the same terms: fee, what's included, minimum contract, and who owns the channel and ad account.</p></li><li><p>Start small. A one to three month trial tells you more than any sales call.</p></li></ol><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>What does a YouTube agency do?</h3><p>It grows a channel or business on YouTube through some mix of strategy, video production, optimization, ads and reporting. Most agencies specialize in one or two of these.</p><h3>Is a YouTube agency the same as an MCN?</h3><p>No. An MCN (multi-channel network) is a type of creator management company that partners with many channels, usually for a share of their revenue. Most YouTube agencies work for businesses and charge a fee.</p><h3>Do YouTube agencies work with small channels?</h3><p>Many do, especially editing, thumbnail and channel audit specialists. Ads agencies usually want a minimum ad budget, so very small businesses may be better off starting with a freelancer.</p><h3>How long does it take to see results?</h3><p>Ads can show early data within weeks. Organic growth from SEO and regular uploads usually takes three to six months to judge.</p><h3>Is YT Agencies a YouTube agency?</h3><p>No. YT Agencies is an independent publication of YouTube guides. It isn't affiliated with YouTube or Google.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-agency'
  and body_json::text like '%ollar figures above round them. Agencies%';

-- youtube-agency-for-small-creators
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For most small creators, a full-service YouTube agency isn't worth it yet. Full management usually starts around $1,000 a month, which is more than most small channels earn. What usually is worth it is paying for one specific skill that's holding the channel back: editing, thumbnails and titles, or a one-off channel audit."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The best YouTube agency for a small creator is the one that fixes your biggest weak spot, at a price you can keep paying for three months, and can prove it has done that for channels your size."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What to hire at each stage"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your channel"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Biggest problem usually"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What to pay for"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rough cost"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Under 1,000 subscribers, posting regularly"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unclear topics and packaging"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A one-off "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" after 10 to 20 videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One-time fee"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Views come in, but editing eats your week"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freelance or agency "},{"type":"text","text":"editor"},{"type":"text","text":", paid per video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Per video"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impressions but few clicks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Titles and thumbnails"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thumbnails and packaging"},{"type":"text","text":" specialist"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$500 to $2,000 a month for an ongoing retainer, less per thumbnail"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Steady income, can't keep up"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Everything at once"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel management"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"From about $1,000 a month"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Brand emails arriving every week"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deals and negotiation"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A freelance manager (see our "},{"type":"text","text":"talent agencies guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/talent-agencies-for-new-youtubers"}}]},{"type":"text","text":")"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually 10% to 15% of deals"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thumbnail retainer range from "},{"type":"text","text":"OutlierKit","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://outlierkit.com/resources/hire-youtube-agency/"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"; manager commission from "},{"type":"text","text":"EarnifyHub","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://earnifyhub.com/creator-economy/creator-agency-vs-independent-2026"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to find your weak spot"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Open YouTube Studio and compare two numbers for your last 10 videos:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Impressions click-through rate. If it's low compared with your own best videos, people see your videos but don't click. That's packaging: titles and thumbnails."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Average percentage viewed. If people click but leave early, the problem is the video itself: the hook, pacing or editing."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If both look fine but impressions are low, the topics may not be ones people are looking for. That's where an audit or strategy help pays off."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A budget rule for small creators"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't spend more on help each month than you can comfortably pay for three months with no return. Growth help takes time to show up in the numbers, and stopping after one month wastes most of what you spent."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to check an agency's reviews and results yourself"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reviews on an agency's own site are hand-picked. Check these instead:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ask for two or three client channels about your size, then look them up yourself."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sort each channel's videos by date and compare views per video before and after the agency started. Subscriber totals can rise from one lucky video; steady views per video show real improvement."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Message one of those creators and ask how the work went. Most will answer honestly."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Look for independent reviews on third-party sites and creator forums, not testimonials the agency chose."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check whether the agency's own YouTube channel, if it has one, follows its own advice."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Red flags for small creators"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Promising a set number of subscribers or views, or \"monetization in 30 days\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Selling subscribers, views or \"promotion packages\". Fake engagement breaks YouTube's rules and can get a channel removed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Asking for your channel password instead of access through YouTube Studio permissions"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long contracts or a share of all your future income for basic editing work"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No examples of channels your size"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What is the best YouTube agency for small creators?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The one that fixes your channel's biggest weak spot, has results with channels your size, and fits a budget you can hold for three months. For most small creators, that's an editor, thumbnail specialist or audit, not a full-service agency."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do YouTube growth agencies work for small channels?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They can, when the work improves the videos: better packaging, stronger hooks, clearer topics. Agencies that promise growth through promotion or ads without improving the videos rarely produce lasting results."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much should a small creator spend on a YouTube agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Only what you can keep paying for three months without income from it. For many small creators, that means paying per video or per audit rather than a monthly retainer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is it safe to give an agency access to my channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, if you use YouTube Studio permissions to give them an Editor or Manager role and keep the Owner role yourself. Never share your password."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I know if a YouTube agency review is real?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Find the channel being reviewed, check its views per video over time, and if possible contact the creator directly. Real results show up on the channel."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>For most small creators, a full-service YouTube agency isn't worth it yet. Full management usually starts around $1,000 a month, which is more than most small channels earn. What usually is worth it is paying for one specific skill that's holding the channel back: editing, thumbnails and titles, or a one-off channel audit.</p><p>The best YouTube agency for a small creator is the one that fixes your biggest weak spot, at a price you can keep paying for three months, and can prove it has done that for channels your size.</p><h2>What to hire at each stage</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Your channel</th><th>Biggest problem usually</th><th>What to pay for</th><th>Rough cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Under 1,000 subscribers, posting regularly</td><td>Unclear topics and packaging</td><td>A one-off channel audit after 10 to 20 videos</td><td>One-time fee</td></tr><tr><td>Views come in, but editing eats your week</td><td>Time</td><td>A freelance or agency editor, paid per video</td><td>Per video</td></tr><tr><td>Impressions but few clicks</td><td>Titles and thumbnails</td><td>Thumbnails and packaging specialist</td><td>$500 to $2,000 a month for an ongoing retainer, less per thumbnail</td></tr><tr><td>Steady income, can't keep up</td><td>Everything at once</td><td>Channel management</td><td>From about $1,000 a month</td></tr><tr><td>Brand emails arriving every week</td><td>Deals and negotiation</td><td>A freelance manager (see our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/talent-agencies-for-new-youtubers">talent agencies guide</a>)</td><td>Usually 10% to 15% of deals</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Thumbnail retainer range from <a href="https://outlierkit.com/resources/hire-youtube-agency/">OutlierKit</a>; manager commission from <a href="https://earnifyhub.com/creator-economy/creator-agency-vs-independent-2026">EarnifyHub</a>.</p><h3>How to find your weak spot</h3><p>Open YouTube Studio and compare two numbers for your last 10 videos:</p><ul><li><p>Impressions click-through rate. If it's low compared with your own best videos, people see your videos but don't click. That's packaging: titles and thumbnails.</p></li><li><p>Average percentage viewed. If people click but leave early, the problem is the video itself: the hook, pacing or editing.</p></li></ul><p>If both look fine but impressions are low, the topics may not be ones people are looking for. That's where an audit or strategy help pays off.</p><h3>A budget rule for small creators</h3><p>Don't spend more on help each month than you can comfortably pay for three months with no return. Growth help takes time to show up in the numbers, and stopping after one month wastes most of what you spent.</p><h2>How to check an agency's reviews and results yourself</h2><p>Reviews on an agency's own site are hand-picked. Check these instead:</p><ol><li><p>Ask for two or three client channels about your size, then look them up yourself.</p></li><li><p>Sort each channel's videos by date and compare views per video before and after the agency started. Subscriber totals can rise from one lucky video; steady views per video show real improvement.</p></li><li><p>Message one of those creators and ask how the work went. Most will answer honestly.</p></li><li><p>Look for independent reviews on third-party sites and creator forums, not testimonials the agency chose.</p></li><li><p>Check whether the agency's own YouTube channel, if it has one, follows its own advice.</p></li></ol><h2>Red flags for small creators</h2><ul><li><p>Promising a set number of subscribers or views, or "monetization in 30 days"</p></li><li><p>Selling subscribers, views or "promotion packages". Fake engagement breaks YouTube's rules and can get a channel removed.</p></li><li><p>Asking for your channel password instead of access through YouTube Studio permissions</p></li><li><p>Long contracts or a share of all your future income for basic editing work</p></li><li><p>No examples of channels your size</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>What is the best YouTube agency for small creators?</h3><p>The one that fixes your channel's biggest weak spot, has results with channels your size, and fits a budget you can hold for three months. For most small creators, that's an editor, thumbnail specialist or audit, not a full-service agency.</p><h3>Do YouTube growth agencies work for small channels?</h3><p>They can, when the work improves the videos: better packaging, stronger hooks, clearer topics. Agencies that promise growth through promotion or ads without improving the videos rarely produce lasting results.</p><h3>How much should a small creator spend on a YouTube agency?</h3><p>Only what you can keep paying for three months without income from it. For many small creators, that means paying per video or per audit rather than a monthly retainer.</p><h3>Is it safe to give an agency access to my channel?</h3><p>Yes, if you use YouTube Studio permissions to give them an Editor or Manager role and keep the Owner role yourself. Never share your password.</p><h3>How do I know if a YouTube agency review is real?</h3><p>Find the channel being reviewed, check its views per video over time, and if possible contact the creator directly. Real results show up on the channel.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-agency-for-small-creators'
  and body_json::text like '%an filter by service, by the Creators & %';

-- youtube-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Using YouTube for business means publishing videos that help your customers, so they find you when they search and trust you before they buy. You can also pay for YouTube ads to reach people who haven't found you yet."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"There is no special \"YouTube for Business\" account or price plan. A business channel is a normal YouTube channel, and it's free. You sign in with a Google account, create a channel under your business name, and upload. Our guide on "},{"type":"text","text":"how to create a YouTube channel for your business","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" walks through the setup."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The real costs are your time and any help you pay for:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Item"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical cost"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Basic gear (phone, clip-on mic, light)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$100 to $300 one time"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Freelance editor"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$50 to $300 per video, depending on length"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Agency help"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often from $1,000 a month"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Any budget you set, often $0.02 to $0.30 per view"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The agency and ad figures come from our "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube ads agency cost guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":". Editing rates vary widely by market and video length, so treat that row as a starting estimate."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why businesses use YouTube: the benefits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube is where people go to learn how to do something, compare products and watch reviews before they buy. That makes it unusually good for businesses that sell anything people research first."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A few numbers show the scale:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fact"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Figure"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Source"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube's 2025 revenue (ads and subscriptions)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"More than $60 billion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Alphabet Q4 2025 earnings call, via "},{"type":"text","text":"EliteContentMarketer","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.elitecontentmarketer.com/youtube-statistics/"}}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Daily Shorts views"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 200 billion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube CEO Neal Mohan, June 2025, via "},{"type":"text","text":"Revid","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.revid.ai/youtube-shorts-statistics"}}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Daily watch time on TV screens"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 1 billion hours"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube Partner Program update, August 2026, via "},{"type":"text","text":"Revid","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.revid.ai/youtube-shorts-statistics"}}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share of all US TV viewing time, December 2025"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"12.7%, the most of any media company"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Nielsen, via "},{"type":"text","text":"SocialPilot","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.socialpilot.co/youtube-marketing/youtube-statistics"}}]}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monthly viewers of podcast content"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Over 1 billion"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube blog, via "},{"type":"text","text":"Podder","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.podderapp.com/post/youtube-podcast-stats"}}]}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You'll often see \"2.7 billion monthly users\" quoted. YouTube hasn't published that number; it's a third-party estimate of ad reach. The real audience is clearly in the billions, but don't put that exact figure in a pitch deck."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The business benefits:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Videos keep working for years. A blog post can drop off Google; a good how-to video can keep pulling views and leads long after you post it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You show up in two search engines. YouTube videos often appear in Google results too, especially for how-to searches."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video builds trust before the first call. A prospect who has watched you explain their problem for ten minutes arrives half sold."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reach beyond your followers. Recommendations and Shorts put your videos in front of people who have never heard of you. One analysis found 74% of Shorts views come from non-subscribers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can reach people on the living room TV, which used to need a TV ad budget."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One video feeds every other channel. Cut it into Shorts, clips for LinkedIn and Instagram, quotes for email, and a transcript for your blog."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pros and cons of YouTube for business"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pros"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cons"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free to start and host unlimited video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Takes 6 to 12 months of steady posting to build momentum"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Videos are found through search for years"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video takes more time to make than a post or blog"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Builds trust faster than text"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Competitors may already own the top results in your niche"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reaches people on phones, laptops and TVs"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You don't control the algorithm or YouTube's rule changes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ads can be targeted by interest, intent and location"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comments need moderating, and negative ones are public"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Feeds Shorts, social clips and email from one recording"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube may show competitors' videos next to yours"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The biggest con is patience. Most business channels look slow for the first few months, then grow faster as their library of useful videos builds up. If you need leads this month, pair a new channel with YouTube ads."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to use YouTube for business: a 7-step strategy"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Pick one goal"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Choose the result the channel is for: leads, product sales, hiring, or support (fewer \"how do I\" emails). It decides every later choice. A dentist chasing new patients makes different videos from a SaaS company trying to cut support tickets."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Define your target audience"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your YouTube audience is your customer, described by the problem they're trying to solve. Write one sentence: \"Homeowners in Austin deciding whether to repair or replace an old roof.\" That sentence tells you what to film. Don't aim at \"everyone who watches YouTube\"; a narrow audience is easier to rank for and more likely to buy."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Collect the questions customers already ask"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List the questions from sales calls, support emails and reviews. Then type the main ones into YouTube's search bar and note the autocomplete suggestions. Each real question is a video idea with built-in demand."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Choose a format you can keep up with"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most business channels do well with a mix of:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How-to and explainer videos that answer one question each"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comparisons and \"is it worth it\" videos for people close to buying"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Customer stories and case studies"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Behind-the-scenes or team videos that build trust"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts cut from the long videos, to reach new people"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pick a schedule you can hold for six months. One video every two weeks that you actually publish beats a weekly plan you abandon in March."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Make each video easy to find and click"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put the main search phrase near the start of the title. Write a description that summarizes the video in its first two lines and links to the relevant page on your site. Add chapters. Design a thumbnail with a clear face or object and three words at most."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Turn viewers into customers"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tell viewers what to do next, once, near the end: book a call, download a checklist, or watch the next video. Add the link to the description and to a pinned comment. Use playlists to lead people from a beginner video to a buying-stage one."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Measure the right numbers and adjust"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In YouTube Studio, watch click-through rate (are titles and thumbnails working?), average view duration (is the video holding attention?) and traffic sources (search, suggested, or external). On your website, track how many leads come from YouTube. Review monthly, make more of what works, and drop what doesn't."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"12 tips for using YouTube for business"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hook viewers in the first 10 seconds by stating the problem the video solves. Skip the logo intro."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Answer one question per video. A focused video ranks better than a 40-minute catch-all."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Show your face, or your hands doing the work. People trust a person more than a logo."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Invest in sound before cameras. Viewers forgive a phone camera; they leave over bad audio."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Make a channel trailer for new visitors that says who the channel is for in 30 seconds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use your real expertise. Specific numbers, prices and mistakes you've seen make videos more useful than generic advice."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Turn every long video into two or three Shorts that point back to it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Embed videos on the matching pages of your website, such as a service page or FAQ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reply to comments in the first day. Early replies keep the conversation going and tell you what to film next."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Update old titles and thumbnails when a video underperforms. You can change them at any time."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Send new videos to your email list and sales team. Salespeople can use them to answer common questions."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run small ads behind your best organic video. If a video already converts, a modest budget can show it to many more of the right people."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube vs Facebook for business"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Facebook"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How people find you"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Search and recommendations, for years after posting"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Mostly the feed, for a day or two after posting"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best video length"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-form (5 to 20+ minutes) plus Shorts"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Short clips and Reels"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Viewer intent"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often actively researching or learning"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually scrolling, not looking for you"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Best for"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Explaining, comparing, building trust before a purchase"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Local community, events, retargeting people who know you"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Organic reach for new pages"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Possible through search, even with zero subscribers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Low without paid boosts"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most businesses don't need to choose. Publish the full video on YouTube, then post a short clip on Facebook that points to it."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Free YouTube vs paid YouTube ads"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Organic videos cost time, compound slowly and keep working. Ads cost money, work from day one and stop when the budget stops. A sensible split for most small businesses is to post organically every week or two, then put a small ad budget behind the one or two videos that already bring in leads. Our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"advertising your business on YouTube for free","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/advertise-business-free-on-youtube"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers the organic side in detail."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is YouTube for business free?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. Creating a business channel and uploading videos is free. You only pay if you run ads or hire help."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is YouTube good for small businesses?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, especially for businesses whose customers research before buying: clinics, law firms, trades, coaches, software and e-commerce. Local businesses can rank for searches like \"how much does a new roof cost in [city]\"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Who is the target audience on YouTube for a business?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your existing customers, described by the problem they're trying to solve. YouTube's audience covers nearly every age group, so the question is less \"who is on YouTube\" and more \"what are my customers searching for there\"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How often should a business post on YouTube?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"As often as you can keep up for six months or more. One useful video every week or two is a realistic target for most small teams."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should my business use YouTube Shorts?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts are good for reaching new people quickly; long videos are better for building trust and getting leads. Most businesses do best with both. See our guide on "},{"type":"text","text":"whether to use YouTube Shorts for your business","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do I need an agency to use YouTube for business?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Many businesses start on their own. An agency helps when YouTube already brings in customers and you can't keep up, or when you want to run ads. See "},{"type":"text","text":"when to hire a YouTube agency","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>Using YouTube for business means publishing videos that help your customers, so they find you when they search and trust you before they buy. You can also pay for YouTube ads to reach people who haven't found you yet.</p><p>There is no special "YouTube for Business" account or price plan. A business channel is a normal YouTube channel, and it's free. You sign in with a Google account, create a channel under your business name, and upload. Our guide on <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business">how to create a YouTube channel for your business</a> walks through the setup.</p><p>The real costs are your time and any help you pay for:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Channel</td><td>Free</td></tr><tr><td>Basic gear (phone, clip-on mic, light)</td><td>$100 to $300 one time</td></tr><tr><td>Freelance editor</td><td>$50 to $300 per video, depending on length</td></tr><tr><td>Agency help</td><td>Often from $1,000 a month</td></tr><tr><td>YouTube ads</td><td>Any budget you set, often $0.02 to $0.30 per view</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The agency and ad figures come from our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">YouTube ads agency cost guide</a>. Editing rates vary widely by market and video length, so treat that row as a starting estimate.</p><h2>Why businesses use YouTube: the benefits</h2><p>YouTube is where people go to learn how to do something, compare products and watch reviews before they buy. That makes it unusually good for businesses that sell anything people research first.</p><p>A few numbers show the scale:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Figure</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>YouTube's 2025 revenue (ads and subscriptions)</td><td>More than $60 billion</td><td>Alphabet Q4 2025 earnings call, via <a href="https://www.elitecontentmarketer.com/youtube-statistics/">EliteContentMarketer</a></td></tr><tr><td>Daily Shorts views</td><td>Over 200 billion</td><td>YouTube CEO Neal Mohan, June 2025, via <a href="https://www.revid.ai/youtube-shorts-statistics">Revid</a></td></tr><tr><td>Daily watch time on TV screens</td><td>Over 1 billion hours</td><td>YouTube Partner Program update, August 2026, via <a href="https://www.revid.ai/youtube-shorts-statistics">Revid</a></td></tr><tr><td>Share of all US TV viewing time, December 2025</td><td>12.7%, the most of any media company</td><td>Nielsen, via <a href="https://www.socialpilot.co/youtube-marketing/youtube-statistics">SocialPilot</a></td></tr><tr><td>Monthly viewers of podcast content</td><td>Over 1 billion</td><td>YouTube blog, via <a href="https://www.podderapp.com/post/youtube-podcast-stats">Podder</a></td></tr></tbody></table><p>You'll often see "2.7 billion monthly users" quoted. YouTube hasn't published that number; it's a third-party estimate of ad reach. The real audience is clearly in the billions, but don't put that exact figure in a pitch deck.</p><p>The business benefits:</p><ul><li><p>Videos keep working for years. A blog post can drop off Google; a good how-to video can keep pulling views and leads long after you post it.</p></li><li><p>You show up in two search engines. YouTube videos often appear in Google results too, especially for how-to searches.</p></li><li><p>Video builds trust before the first call. A prospect who has watched you explain their problem for ten minutes arrives half sold.</p></li><li><p>Reach beyond your followers. Recommendations and Shorts put your videos in front of people who have never heard of you. One analysis found 74% of Shorts views come from non-subscribers.</p></li><li><p>You can reach people on the living room TV, which used to need a TV ad budget.</p></li><li><p>One video feeds every other channel. Cut it into Shorts, clips for LinkedIn and Instagram, quotes for email, and a transcript for your blog.</p></li></ul><h2>Pros and cons of YouTube for business</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Pros</th><th>Cons</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Free to start and host unlimited video</td><td>Takes 6 to 12 months of steady posting to build momentum</td></tr><tr><td>Videos are found through search for years</td><td>Video takes more time to make than a post or blog</td></tr><tr><td>Builds trust faster than text</td><td>Competitors may already own the top results in your niche</td></tr><tr><td>Reaches people on phones, laptops and TVs</td><td>You don't control the algorithm or YouTube's rule changes</td></tr><tr><td>Ads can be targeted by interest, intent and location</td><td>Comments need moderating, and negative ones are public</td></tr><tr><td>Feeds Shorts, social clips and email from one recording</td><td>YouTube may show competitors' videos next to yours</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The biggest con is patience. Most business channels look slow for the first few months, then grow faster as their library of useful videos builds up. If you need leads this month, pair a new channel with YouTube ads.</p><h2>How to use YouTube for business: a 7-step strategy</h2><h3>1. Pick one goal</h3><p>Choose the result the channel is for: leads, product sales, hiring, or support (fewer "how do I" emails). It decides every later choice. A dentist chasing new patients makes different videos from a SaaS company trying to cut support tickets.</p><h3>2. Define your target audience</h3><p>Your YouTube audience is your customer, described by the problem they're trying to solve. Write one sentence: "Homeowners in Austin deciding whether to repair or replace an old roof." That sentence tells you what to film. Don't aim at "everyone who watches YouTube"; a narrow audience is easier to rank for and more likely to buy.</p><h3>3. Collect the questions customers already ask</h3><p>List the questions from sales calls, support emails and reviews. Then type the main ones into YouTube's search bar and note the autocomplete suggestions. Each real question is a video idea with built-in demand.</p><h3>4. Choose a format you can keep up with</h3><p>Most business channels do well with a mix of:</p><ul><li><p>How-to and explainer videos that answer one question each</p></li><li><p>Comparisons and "is it worth it" videos for people close to buying</p></li><li><p>Customer stories and case studies</p></li><li><p>Behind-the-scenes or team videos that build trust</p></li><li><p>Shorts cut from the long videos, to reach new people</p></li></ul><p>Pick a schedule you can hold for six months. One video every two weeks that you actually publish beats a weekly plan you abandon in March.</p><h3>5. Make each video easy to find and click</h3><p>Put the main search phrase near the start of the title. Write a description that summarizes the video in its first two lines and links to the relevant page on your site. Add chapters. Design a thumbnail with a clear face or object and three words at most.</p><h3>6. Turn viewers into customers</h3><p>Tell viewers what to do next, once, near the end: book a call, download a checklist, or watch the next video. Add the link to the description and to a pinned comment. Use playlists to lead people from a beginner video to a buying-stage one.</p><h3>7. Measure the right numbers and adjust</h3><p>In YouTube Studio, watch click-through rate (are titles and thumbnails working?), average view duration (is the video holding attention?) and traffic sources (search, suggested, or external). On your website, track how many leads come from YouTube. Review monthly, make more of what works, and drop what doesn't.</p><h2>12 tips for using YouTube for business</h2><ol><li><p>Hook viewers in the first 10 seconds by stating the problem the video solves. Skip the logo intro.</p></li><li><p>Answer one question per video. A focused video ranks better than a 40-minute catch-all.</p></li><li><p>Show your face, or your hands doing the work. People trust a person more than a logo.</p></li><li><p>Invest in sound before cameras. Viewers forgive a phone camera; they leave over bad audio.</p></li><li><p>Make a channel trailer for new visitors that says who the channel is for in 30 seconds.</p></li><li><p>Use your real expertise. Specific numbers, prices and mistakes you've seen make videos more useful than generic advice.</p></li><li><p>Turn every long video into two or three Shorts that point back to it.</p></li><li><p>Embed videos on the matching pages of your website, such as a service page or FAQ.</p></li><li><p>Reply to comments in the first day. Early replies keep the conversation going and tell you what to film next.</p></li><li><p>Update old titles and thumbnails when a video underperforms. You can change them at any time.</p></li><li><p>Send new videos to your email list and sales team. Salespeople can use them to answer common questions.</p></li><li><p>Run small ads behind your best organic video. If a video already converts, a modest budget can show it to many more of the right people.</p></li></ol><h2>YouTube vs Facebook for business</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>YouTube</th><th>Facebook</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>How people find you</td><td>Search and recommendations, for years after posting</td><td>Mostly the feed, for a day or two after posting</td></tr><tr><td>Best video length</td><td>Long-form (5 to 20+ minutes) plus Shorts</td><td>Short clips and Reels</td></tr><tr><td>Viewer intent</td><td>Often actively researching or learning</td><td>Usually scrolling, not looking for you</td></tr><tr><td>Best for</td><td>Explaining, comparing, building trust before a purchase</td><td>Local community, events, retargeting people who know you</td></tr><tr><td>Organic reach for new pages</td><td>Possible through search, even with zero subscribers</td><td>Low without paid boosts</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Most businesses don't need to choose. Publish the full video on YouTube, then post a short clip on Facebook that points to it.</p><h2>Free YouTube vs paid YouTube ads</h2><p>Organic videos cost time, compound slowly and keep working. Ads cost money, work from day one and stop when the budget stops. A sensible split for most small businesses is to post organically every week or two, then put a small ad budget behind the one or two videos that already bring in leads. Our guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/advertise-business-free-on-youtube">advertising your business on YouTube for free</a> covers the organic side in detail.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Is YouTube for business free?</h3><p>Yes. Creating a business channel and uploading videos is free. You only pay if you run ads or hire help.</p><h3>Is YouTube good for small businesses?</h3><p>Yes, especially for businesses whose customers research before buying: clinics, law firms, trades, coaches, software and e-commerce. Local businesses can rank for searches like "how much does a new roof cost in [city]".</p><h3>Who is the target audience on YouTube for a business?</h3><p>Your existing customers, described by the problem they're trying to solve. YouTube's audience covers nearly every age group, so the question is less "who is on YouTube" and more "what are my customers searching for there".</p><h3>How often should a business post on YouTube?</h3><p>As often as you can keep up for six months or more. One useful video every week or two is a realistic target for most small teams.</p><h3>Should my business use YouTube Shorts?</h3><p>Shorts are good for reaching new people quickly; long videos are better for building trust and getting leads. Most businesses do best with both. See our guide on <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business">whether to use YouTube Shorts for your business</a>.</p><h3>Do I need an agency to use YouTube for business?</h3><p>No. Many businesses start on their own. An agency helps when YouTube already brings in customers and you can't keep up, or when you want to run ads. See <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/when-to-hire-a-youtube-agency">when to hire a YouTube agency</a>.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%If you want help with any of these, YT A%';

-- youtube-script-structure-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A good YouTube script for a business follows six parts, in this order:"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hook: name the viewer's problem in the first 10 seconds."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Promise: say what they'll know by the end, and why you're the one to tell them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Problem: explain why the problem matters or what it costs them."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Answer: the main content, in three to five clear steps or points."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proof: one real example, number or customer story."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next step: one call to action that fits where the viewer is."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The structure works because it matches what a viewer asks themselves, in order: is this for me, can I trust this person, do I care, what's the answer, does it work, and what now? Business owners usually get the answer right and skip the hook and next step, which are the two parts that bring in customers."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The six parts, with timings"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Timings below are for an 8-minute video. At a natural speaking pace of roughly 140 to 160 words a minute, that's a script of about 1,200 words."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Part"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Rough word count"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What it must do"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Hook"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0:00 to 0:10"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"25"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Make the right viewer think \"this is about me\""}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Promise"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0:10 to 0:30"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"50"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Say what they'll get and why to trust you"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Problem"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"0:30 to 1:15"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"110"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Make the cost of the problem concrete"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Answer"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1:15 to 6:30"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"800"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Deliver the steps or points, one at a time"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Proof"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6:30 to 7:30"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"150"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Show it working with a real example"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Next step"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"7:30 to 8:00"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"75"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tell them exactly what to do now"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Hook (first 10 seconds)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with the viewer's problem or the result they want, in their words. Skip the logo animation, \"Hi everyone, welcome back\" and your life story."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Weak: \"Hi, I'm Dr. Patel from Smile Dental, and today we're talking about implants.\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Strong: \"If you've been told you need a dental implant and the price shocked you, here's what you're actually paying for.\""}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Promise and credibility (10 to 30 seconds)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tell them what they'll know by the end, then give one line of credibility. \"By the end you'll know the five costs in an implant quote and which ones you can negotiate. I've placed over 2,000 implants in 15 years, so I've seen every kind of quote.\" One sentence of credentials is enough."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. The problem (30 seconds to about 1 minute)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Show why this matters with a concrete cost: money lost, time wasted, a risk they hadn't considered. This keeps viewers through the slower middle of the video."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. The answer (the bulk of the video)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Break the content into three to five numbered points or steps, and say the number up front: \"There are five costs in every implant quote.\" Give each point a one-line headline, then explain it with a specific example. Add a chapter timestamp for each one in the description."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Proof"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One real story or number beats three general claims. A customer's before and after, a real quote broken down, or a result with numbers attached. Get permission before naming anyone."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Next step (last 30 seconds)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Give one call to action, matched to the viewer's stage:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Early-stage viewers: \"Watch this video next\" (with an end screen)."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comparing options: \"Download the checklist in the description.\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ready to buy: \"Book a free consultation, link below.\""}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One clear action works better than \"like, subscribe, comment, visit our site and follow us on Instagram\"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Fill-in script template"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Copy this and fill in the brackets. Most business owners can finish it in under an hour."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","content":[{"type":"text","text":"TITLE: [The exact question or result, in the customer's words]\n\n1. HOOK (10 sec)\n\"If you [situation the viewer is in], [the surprising fact, mistake or result].\"\n\n2. PROMISE (20 sec)\n\"In this video you'll learn [specific outcome].\nI'm [name], I [one line of credibility: years, number of clients, result].\"\n\n3. PROBLEM (45 sec)\n\"Most people [common mistake]. That usually costs them [money, time or risk].\"\n\n4. ANSWER (3 to 5 points)\n\"There are [number] things to know.\"\nPoint 1: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]\nPoint 2: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]\nPoint 3: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]\n\n5. PROOF (1 min)\n\"Here's what happened when [customer] [did this]: [result with a number].\"\n\n6. NEXT STEP (30 sec)\n\"If you [viewer's situation], [one action] - the link is in the description.\""}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Worked example: a roofing company"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Title: How Much Does a New Roof Cost in 2026? (Real Quote Breakdown)"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hook: \"If you've had two roofing quotes that are $8,000 apart, here's exactly why, line by line.\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Promise: \"By the end you'll be able to read any roofing quote and spot padding. I'm Mike, and my team has replaced over 1,500 roofs in Columbus.\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Problem: \"Most homeowners pick the cheapest quote, then pay again in five years when the shortcuts show.\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Answer: \"Every quote has five parts: materials, tear-off, underlayment, flashing and labor.\" Each part gets 60 to 90 seconds with a photo from a real job."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Proof: \"Here are two real quotes for the same house. The cheaper one left out new flashing, a $1,200 repair waiting to happen.\""}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Next step: \"If you've got a quote and you're not sure about it, send it to us for a free second opinion. Link below.\""}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The names and numbers above are illustrations; use your own real ones."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Common script mistakes"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Opening with your logo, name and company history instead of the viewer's problem."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Covering three topics in one video. One question per video ranks and converts better."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Writing like a brochure. Read your script aloud; if you wouldn't say it to a customer across the counter, rewrite it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Asking for five actions at the end. Pick one."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reading word for word in a flat voice. Use the script to plan, then speak from bullet points."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tips for owners who don't like being on camera"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Script the first 30 seconds word for word and speak the rest from bullet points. The opening is where nerves show most."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Film in short sections. You can cut between them in editing."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Talk to one real customer in your head, not \"the audience\"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use a teleprompter app on your phone or tablet for long sections."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you'd rather not appear at all, show your hands doing the work, a screen recording, or photos with a voiceover."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long should a YouTube script be for a business video?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For most how-to and explainer videos, 6 to 12 minutes, or roughly 900 to 1,800 words. Long enough to answer the question fully, and no longer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I write a script word for word?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Write the hook and call to action word for word. For the middle, bullet points usually sound more natural on camera."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I write a script for a YouTube Short?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use the same structure squeezed down: a hook in the first second, one point, and a next step, usually pointing to a long video. Aim for 30 to 60 seconds."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I find topics to script?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List the questions customers ask on sales calls and in emails, then check YouTube's search suggestions for how they phrase them. Each question is one script."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can AI write my YouTube scripts?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"AI tools can help with outlines and first drafts. Add your own examples, numbers and opinions, though, because those are what make viewers trust you over a competitor."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>A good YouTube script for a business follows six parts, in this order:</p><ol><li><p>Hook: name the viewer's problem in the first 10 seconds.</p></li><li><p>Promise: say what they'll know by the end, and why you're the one to tell them.</p></li><li><p>Problem: explain why the problem matters or what it costs them.</p></li><li><p>Answer: the main content, in three to five clear steps or points.</p></li><li><p>Proof: one real example, number or customer story.</p></li><li><p>Next step: one call to action that fits where the viewer is.</p></li></ol><p>The structure works because it matches what a viewer asks themselves, in order: is this for me, can I trust this person, do I care, what's the answer, does it work, and what now? Business owners usually get the answer right and skip the hook and next step, which are the two parts that bring in customers.</p><h2>The six parts, with timings</h2><p>Timings below are for an 8-minute video. At a natural speaking pace of roughly 140 to 160 words a minute, that's a script of about 1,200 words.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Time</th><th>Rough word count</th><th>What it must do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Hook</td><td>0:00 to 0:10</td><td>25</td><td>Make the right viewer think "this is about me"</td></tr><tr><td>2. Promise</td><td>0:10 to 0:30</td><td>50</td><td>Say what they'll get and why to trust you</td></tr><tr><td>3. Problem</td><td>0:30 to 1:15</td><td>110</td><td>Make the cost of the problem concrete</td></tr><tr><td>4. Answer</td><td>1:15 to 6:30</td><td>800</td><td>Deliver the steps or points, one at a time</td></tr><tr><td>5. Proof</td><td>6:30 to 7:30</td><td>150</td><td>Show it working with a real example</td></tr><tr><td>6. Next step</td><td>7:30 to 8:00</td><td>75</td><td>Tell them exactly what to do now</td></tr></tbody></table><h3>1. Hook (first 10 seconds)</h3><p>Start with the viewer's problem or the result they want, in their words. Skip the logo animation, "Hi everyone, welcome back" and your life story.</p><p>Weak: "Hi, I'm Dr. Patel from Smile Dental, and today we're talking about implants."</p><p>Strong: "If you've been told you need a dental implant and the price shocked you, here's what you're actually paying for."</p><h3>2. Promise and credibility (10 to 30 seconds)</h3><p>Tell them what they'll know by the end, then give one line of credibility. "By the end you'll know the five costs in an implant quote and which ones you can negotiate. I've placed over 2,000 implants in 15 years, so I've seen every kind of quote." One sentence of credentials is enough.</p><h3>3. The problem (30 seconds to about 1 minute)</h3><p>Show why this matters with a concrete cost: money lost, time wasted, a risk they hadn't considered. This keeps viewers through the slower middle of the video.</p><h3>4. The answer (the bulk of the video)</h3><p>Break the content into three to five numbered points or steps, and say the number up front: "There are five costs in every implant quote." Give each point a one-line headline, then explain it with a specific example. Add a chapter timestamp for each one in the description.</p><h3>5. Proof</h3><p>One real story or number beats three general claims. A customer's before and after, a real quote broken down, or a result with numbers attached. Get permission before naming anyone.</p><h3>6. Next step (last 30 seconds)</h3><p>Give one call to action, matched to the viewer's stage:</p><ul><li><p>Early-stage viewers: "Watch this video next" (with an end screen).</p></li><li><p>Comparing options: "Download the checklist in the description."</p></li><li><p>Ready to buy: "Book a free consultation, link below."</p></li></ul><p>One clear action works better than "like, subscribe, comment, visit our site and follow us on Instagram".</p><h2>Fill-in script template</h2><p>Copy this and fill in the brackets. Most business owners can finish it in under an hour.</p><pre><code>TITLE: [The exact question or result, in the customer's words]

1. HOOK (10 sec)
"If you [situation the viewer is in], [the surprising fact, mistake or result]."

2. PROMISE (20 sec)
"In this video you'll learn [specific outcome].
I'm [name], I [one line of credibility: years, number of clients, result]."

3. PROBLEM (45 sec)
"Most people [common mistake]. That usually costs them [money, time or risk]."

4. ANSWER (3 to 5 points)
"There are [number] things to know."
Point 1: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]
Point 2: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]
Point 3: [headline] - [explanation] - [example]

5. PROOF (1 min)
"Here's what happened when [customer] [did this]: [result with a number]."

6. NEXT STEP (30 sec)
"If you [viewer's situation], [one action] - the link is in the description."</code></pre><h2>Worked example: a roofing company</h2><p>Title: How Much Does a New Roof Cost in 2026? (Real Quote Breakdown)</p><ol><li><p>Hook: "If you've had two roofing quotes that are $8,000 apart, here's exactly why, line by line."</p></li><li><p>Promise: "By the end you'll be able to read any roofing quote and spot padding. I'm Mike, and my team has replaced over 1,500 roofs in Columbus."</p></li><li><p>Problem: "Most homeowners pick the cheapest quote, then pay again in five years when the shortcuts show."</p></li><li><p>Answer: "Every quote has five parts: materials, tear-off, underlayment, flashing and labor." Each part gets 60 to 90 seconds with a photo from a real job.</p></li><li><p>Proof: "Here are two real quotes for the same house. The cheaper one left out new flashing, a $1,200 repair waiting to happen."</p></li><li><p>Next step: "If you've got a quote and you're not sure about it, send it to us for a free second opinion. Link below."</p></li></ol><p>The names and numbers above are illustrations; use your own real ones.</p><h2>Common script mistakes</h2><ul><li><p>Opening with your logo, name and company history instead of the viewer's problem.</p></li><li><p>Covering three topics in one video. One question per video ranks and converts better.</p></li><li><p>Writing like a brochure. Read your script aloud; if you wouldn't say it to a customer across the counter, rewrite it.</p></li><li><p>Asking for five actions at the end. Pick one.</p></li><li><p>Reading word for word in a flat voice. Use the script to plan, then speak from bullet points.</p></li></ul><h2>Tips for owners who don't like being on camera</h2><ul><li><p>Script the first 30 seconds word for word and speak the rest from bullet points. The opening is where nerves show most.</p></li><li><p>Film in short sections. You can cut between them in editing.</p></li><li><p>Talk to one real customer in your head, not "the audience".</p></li><li><p>Use a teleprompter app on your phone or tablet for long sections.</p></li><li><p>If you'd rather not appear at all, show your hands doing the work, a screen recording, or photos with a voiceover.</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long should a YouTube script be for a business video?</h3><p>For most how-to and explainer videos, 6 to 12 minutes, or roughly 900 to 1,800 words. Long enough to answer the question fully, and no longer.</p><h3>Should I write a script word for word?</h3><p>Write the hook and call to action word for word. For the middle, bullet points usually sound more natural on camera.</p><h3>How do I write a script for a YouTube Short?</h3><p>Use the same structure squeezed down: a hook in the first second, one point, and a next step, usually pointing to a long video. Aim for 30 to 60 seconds.</p><h3>How do I find topics to script?</h3><p>List the questions customers ask on sales calls and in emails, then check YouTube's search suggestions for how they phrase them. Each question is one script.</p><h3>Can AI write my YouTube scripts?</h3><p>AI tools can help with outlines and first drafts. Add your own examples, numbers and opinions, though, because those are what make viewers trust you over a competitor.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-script-structure-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%If writing scripts isn''t a good use of y%';

-- youtube-seo
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO means making videos that match what people search for, then packaging them so the right viewers click and keep watching. YouTube says its search ranks videos on three things: relevance, engagement and quality."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Signal"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What YouTube looks at"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"What you control"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Relevance"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How well the title, tags, description and the video itself match the search"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Topic choice, title, description, spoken words, captions"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Engagement"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much viewers watch a video after searching that query"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hook, pacing, delivering on the title's promise"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Quality"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Signs a channel shows expertise, authority and trustworthiness on a topic"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accurate content, consistent focus, real credentials"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube also personalizes results using a viewer's watch and search history, so two people can see different results for the same search."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The practical takeaway: metadata gets you considered, but watch time decides whether you stay. A perfectly optimized title on a video people abandon won't hold its ranking."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO, step by step"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Find topics people actually search"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Start with YouTube's search bar. Type your main topic and note the autocomplete suggestions; they come from real searches. Add a letter after your topic (\"youtube seo a\", \"youtube seo b\") to surface more. Then check YouTube Studio's research tools for what your own audience searches. Pick specific questions over broad topics: \"how to rank a YouTube video for a local business\" is easier to win than \"YouTube SEO\"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Check what already ranks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Search your topic and watch the top five results. Note their length, angle and what they leave out. Your video needs a reason to exist: more current, more specific, better examples, or a clearer answer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Write a title that matches the search and earns the click"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put the main phrase near the start, keep the important words within the first 60 or so characters so they aren't cut off, and add a reason to click: a number, a year, a result. \"YouTube SEO: How to Rank Your Videos in 2026\" does both jobs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Say the keyword in the video"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube looks at the video content, not just the text around it. Say the main phrase naturally in the first 30 seconds, and upload accurate captions so the spoken words are easy to read for viewers and machines."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Write a useful description"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The first two lines show in search and above the fold, so summarize the video there with the main phrase. Below, add a fuller summary, links to related videos and your site, and chapters."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Add chapters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Timestamps in the description split the video into labeled sections. Viewers jump straight to their answer, and chapters can appear as \"key moments\" in Google results."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Design a thumbnail that matches the title"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The thumbnail and title work as a pair. Use a clear focal point, high contrast and three words at most, and don't repeat the title word for word. Low click-through rate on a video with good impressions almost always means packaging."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Hold attention"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is where most rankings are won or lost. Open by restating the viewer's question and promising the answer. Cut slow intros. Deliver what the title promised early, then go deeper. Our "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube script structure guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-script-structure-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows a structure that keeps people watching."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Keep viewers on your channel"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use end screens, cards and playlists to send viewers to your next relevant video. Longer viewing sessions are a good sign to YouTube that your channel answers this topic well."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Use tags lightly"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube lists tags among its relevance factors, but they matter mostly for commonly misspelled words or alternate names. Add a few, then spend your time on the title, thumbnail and video instead."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Review and update"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"After two to four weeks, check impressions, click-through rate, average view duration and traffic from YouTube search in YouTube Studio. Change the title or thumbnail on videos that get impressions but few clicks. You can update both at any time."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Using YouTube to rank in Google too"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube videos often appear in Google results, especially for how-to searches. To give a video the best chance:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Target questions where Google already shows videos. Search the phrase on Google; if a video carousel appears, a video can rank there."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add chapters, so Google can show key moments."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Embed the video on the matching page of your website, with a written summary or transcript around it. The page and the video support each other."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Link to the video from related pages and your newsletter."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This is the other meaning of \"YouTube for SEO\": using video to help your website rank and keep visitors on the page longer."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO myths"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"More tags rank you higher.\" Tags play a small role; the title, the video itself and viewer behavior matter far more."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Long descriptions stuffed with keywords help.\" A clear, useful description helps; repetition doesn't."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"You need lots of subscribers to rank.\" Small channels rank in search all the time when the video matches the query and holds attention."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Upload time decides success.\" It can affect the first hours, but search traffic builds over months."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO checklist"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Topic comes from a real search suggestion or customer question"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Main phrase near the start of the title"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thumbnail is clear, high contrast and complements the title"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Main phrase spoken in the first 30 seconds"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Accurate captions uploaded"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"First two lines of the description summarize the video"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Chapters added"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"End screen and playlist point to the next video"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Embedded on the matching page of your website"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Performance reviewed after two to four weeks"}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long does YouTube SEO take to work?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Some videos pick up search traffic within days; most build over weeks and months. Judge a video after at least a month, and a channel's SEO after three to six months."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do keywords in the title still matter?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. YouTube lists how well the title matches the query as part of relevance. But the title also has to earn the click, so write for people first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Are tags important for YouTube SEO?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"They're a minor factor. A handful covering misspellings and alternate names is enough."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Does video length affect ranking?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not directly. What matters is how much of the video people watch for that search. Make it as long as the answer needs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I hire a YouTube SEO agency?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you're publishing regularly and views from search are flat, an agency can help with topic research, titles, thumbnails and reviewing what's holding videos back."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>YouTube SEO means making videos that match what people search for, then packaging them so the right viewers click and keep watching. YouTube says its search ranks videos on three things: relevance, engagement and quality.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Signal</th><th>What YouTube looks at</th><th>What you control</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Relevance</td><td>How well the title, tags, description and the video itself match the search</td><td>Topic choice, title, description, spoken words, captions</td></tr><tr><td>Engagement</td><td>How much viewers watch a video after searching that query</td><td>Hook, pacing, delivering on the title's promise</td></tr><tr><td>Quality</td><td>Signs a channel shows expertise, authority and trustworthiness on a topic</td><td>Accurate content, consistent focus, real credentials</td></tr></tbody></table><p>YouTube also personalizes results using a viewer's watch and search history, so two people can see different results for the same search.</p><p>The practical takeaway: metadata gets you considered, but watch time decides whether you stay. A perfectly optimized title on a video people abandon won't hold its ranking.</p><h2>YouTube SEO, step by step</h2><h3>1. Find topics people actually search</h3><p>Start with YouTube's search bar. Type your main topic and note the autocomplete suggestions; they come from real searches. Add a letter after your topic ("youtube seo a", "youtube seo b") to surface more. Then check YouTube Studio's research tools for what your own audience searches. Pick specific questions over broad topics: "how to rank a YouTube video for a local business" is easier to win than "YouTube SEO".</p><h3>2. Check what already ranks</h3><p>Search your topic and watch the top five results. Note their length, angle and what they leave out. Your video needs a reason to exist: more current, more specific, better examples, or a clearer answer.</p><h3>3. Write a title that matches the search and earns the click</h3><p>Put the main phrase near the start, keep the important words within the first 60 or so characters so they aren't cut off, and add a reason to click: a number, a year, a result. "YouTube SEO: How to Rank Your Videos in 2026" does both jobs.</p><h3>4. Say the keyword in the video</h3><p>YouTube looks at the video content, not just the text around it. Say the main phrase naturally in the first 30 seconds, and upload accurate captions so the spoken words are easy to read for viewers and machines.</p><h3>5. Write a useful description</h3><p>The first two lines show in search and above the fold, so summarize the video there with the main phrase. Below, add a fuller summary, links to related videos and your site, and chapters.</p><h3>6. Add chapters</h3><p>Timestamps in the description split the video into labeled sections. Viewers jump straight to their answer, and chapters can appear as "key moments" in Google results.</p><h3>7. Design a thumbnail that matches the title</h3><p>The thumbnail and title work as a pair. Use a clear focal point, high contrast and three words at most, and don't repeat the title word for word. Low click-through rate on a video with good impressions almost always means packaging.</p><h3>8. Hold attention</h3><p>This is where most rankings are won or lost. Open by restating the viewer's question and promising the answer. Cut slow intros. Deliver what the title promised early, then go deeper. Our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-script-structure-for-business">YouTube script structure guide</a> shows a structure that keeps people watching.</p><h3>9. Keep viewers on your channel</h3><p>Use end screens, cards and playlists to send viewers to your next relevant video. Longer viewing sessions are a good sign to YouTube that your channel answers this topic well.</p><h3>10. Use tags lightly</h3><p>YouTube lists tags among its relevance factors, but they matter mostly for commonly misspelled words or alternate names. Add a few, then spend your time on the title, thumbnail and video instead.</p><h3>11. Review and update</h3><p>After two to four weeks, check impressions, click-through rate, average view duration and traffic from YouTube search in YouTube Studio. Change the title or thumbnail on videos that get impressions but few clicks. You can update both at any time.</p><h2>Using YouTube to rank in Google too</h2><p>YouTube videos often appear in Google results, especially for how-to searches. To give a video the best chance:</p><ul><li><p>Target questions where Google already shows videos. Search the phrase on Google; if a video carousel appears, a video can rank there.</p></li><li><p>Add chapters, so Google can show key moments.</p></li><li><p>Embed the video on the matching page of your website, with a written summary or transcript around it. The page and the video support each other.</p></li><li><p>Link to the video from related pages and your newsletter.</p></li></ul><p>This is the other meaning of "YouTube for SEO": using video to help your website rank and keep visitors on the page longer.</p><h2>YouTube SEO myths</h2><ul><li><p>"More tags rank you higher." Tags play a small role; the title, the video itself and viewer behavior matter far more.</p></li><li><p>"Long descriptions stuffed with keywords help." A clear, useful description helps; repetition doesn't.</p></li><li><p>"You need lots of subscribers to rank." Small channels rank in search all the time when the video matches the query and holds attention.</p></li><li><p>"Upload time decides success." It can affect the first hours, but search traffic builds over months.</p></li></ul><h2>YouTube SEO checklist</h2><ul><li><p>Topic comes from a real search suggestion or customer question</p></li><li><p>Main phrase near the start of the title</p></li><li><p>Thumbnail is clear, high contrast and complements the title</p></li><li><p>Main phrase spoken in the first 30 seconds</p></li><li><p>Accurate captions uploaded</p></li><li><p>First two lines of the description summarize the video</p></li><li><p>Chapters added</p></li><li><p>End screen and playlist point to the next video</p></li><li><p>Embedded on the matching page of your website</p></li><li><p>Performance reviewed after two to four weeks</p></li></ul><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long does YouTube SEO take to work?</h3><p>Some videos pick up search traffic within days; most build over weeks and months. Judge a video after at least a month, and a channel's SEO after three to six months.</p><h3>Do keywords in the title still matter?</h3><p>Yes. YouTube lists how well the title matches the query as part of relevance. But the title also has to earn the click, so write for people first.</p><h3>Are tags important for YouTube SEO?</h3><p>They're a minor factor. A handful covering misspellings and alternate names is enough.</p><h3>Does video length affect ranking?</h3><p>Not directly. What matters is how much of the video people watch for that search. Make it as long as the answer needs.</p><h3>Should I hire a YouTube SEO agency?</h3><p>If you're publishing regularly and views from search are flat, an agency can help with topic research, titles, thumbnails and reviewing what's holding videos back.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-seo'
  and body_json::text like '%ewing what''s holding videos back. Compar%';

-- youtube-shorts-for-business
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, most businesses should use YouTube Shorts, but as a way to reach new people, not as the whole strategy. Shorts are good at getting your business seen by viewers who don't know you. Long videos are better at earning the trust that turns those viewers into customers."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The reach is real. YouTube says Shorts average over 200 billion views a day and one analysis found 74% of Shorts views come from people who aren't subscribed to the channel. That's why Shorts work as a discovery tool even for brand-new channels."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts vs long videos"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph"}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long videos"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Length"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Up to 3 minutes, vertical"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Any length, usually 5 to 20+ minutes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Main job"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Get discovered by new viewers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Build trust, rank in search, bring in leads"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"How people find them"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The Shorts feed, scrolling"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Search, recommendations, Google"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lifespan"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Most views in the first days or weeks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can get views for years"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Effort per video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Low, especially when cut from a long video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Higher"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Links to your site"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Limited; best used to point to a long video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Description links, cards, end screens"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The best setup for most businesses is long videos as the core and Shorts cut from them as the reach engine."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When Shorts work well for a business"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your product or service is visual: before-and-after results, food, fitness, home improvement, products being used."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can share one useful tip in under a minute, such as a quick fix, a common mistake, or a myth."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You're starting a channel from zero and need first viewers. Shorts don't depend on subscribers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You already make long videos. Cutting Shorts from them costs almost no extra time."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You sell to a broad consumer audience, where reach matters more than depth."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When Shorts are a weak fit"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You sell a complex or expensive service, like B2B software or legal work, where buyers need depth before they trust you. Use Shorts only as teasers for long videos."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You need leads this month. Shorts viewers scroll fast and rarely click through to a website straight away."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can't post regularly. One Short a month rarely moves anything; the format rewards volume."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your only goal is search traffic. People searching a specific question usually land on long videos."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What Shorts cost if you advertise with them"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts ads are among YouTube's cheapest placements, at around $4 per 1,000 impressions in one 2026 benchmark. A paid Shorts view only counts once someone watches 10 seconds or more. That makes Shorts ads a low-cost way to test which hook gets attention before you spend on longer ads."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"21 Shorts ideas by business type"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Business"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts ideas"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Dentists and clinics"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One myth busted per video; \"what this procedure actually feels like\"; a 30-second answer to the most-asked patient question"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Law firms"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"Can I do X without a lawyer?\"; one term explained simply; the biggest mistake people make in [case type]"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Trades (plumbing, roofing, HVAC)"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time-lapse of a job; \"don't do this\" DIY mistakes; signs you need a repair now"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"SaaS"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One feature in 30 seconds; before and after a workflow; a customer's one-line result"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"E-commerce"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Unboxing; the product in real use; three ways to use one product"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Coaches and consultants"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One tip per Short; a common client mistake; a quick reaction to an industry myth"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Real estate"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A 60-second property walkthrough; neighborhood in 3 shots; \"what $X buys you here\""}]}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to make Shorts that bring in business"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Hook in the first second. Open with the result, the problem or a bold claim, not your logo or a greeting."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Film vertically and keep text inside the center of the frame, so it isn't covered by buttons."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add captions. Many people watch with the sound off."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Link each Short to a related long video so interested viewers have somewhere to go next."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Post on a schedule you can keep: three to five a week is a strong target, one a week is still worth it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check which Shorts bring subscribers and long-video views, not just Shorts views, and make more like those."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do YouTube Shorts help grow a business channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, mostly by bringing in new viewers and subscribers. They work best when they point people to long videos that do the selling."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long can a YouTube Short be?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Up to three minutes, in vertical or square format."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How many Shorts should a business post?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Three to five a week if you can manage it. If you can't, cut one or two from each long video you publish."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can Shorts get leads directly?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sometimes, but rarely in large numbers. Shorts are better at making people aware of you than at getting them to fill in a form."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Should I post the same video as a Short, a Reel and a TikTok?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, as long as you remove other platforms' watermarks. Re-using one vertical video across all three is an efficient way to test which platform your customers use."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>Yes, most businesses should use YouTube Shorts, but as a way to reach new people, not as the whole strategy. Shorts are good at getting your business seen by viewers who don't know you. Long videos are better at earning the trust that turns those viewers into customers.</p><p>The reach is real. YouTube says Shorts average over 200 billion views a day and one analysis found 74% of Shorts views come from people who aren't subscribed to the channel. That's why Shorts work as a discovery tool even for brand-new channels.</p><h2>Shorts vs long videos</h2><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Shorts</th><th>Long videos</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Length</td><td>Up to 3 minutes, vertical</td><td>Any length, usually 5 to 20+ minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Main job</td><td>Get discovered by new viewers</td><td>Build trust, rank in search, bring in leads</td></tr><tr><td>How people find them</td><td>The Shorts feed, scrolling</td><td>Search, recommendations, Google</td></tr><tr><td>Lifespan</td><td>Most views in the first days or weeks</td><td>Can get views for years</td></tr><tr><td>Effort per video</td><td>Low, especially when cut from a long video</td><td>Higher</td></tr><tr><td>Links to your site</td><td>Limited; best used to point to a long video</td><td>Description links, cards, end screens</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The best setup for most businesses is long videos as the core and Shorts cut from them as the reach engine.</p><h2>When Shorts work well for a business</h2><ul><li><p>Your product or service is visual: before-and-after results, food, fitness, home improvement, products being used.</p></li><li><p>You can share one useful tip in under a minute, such as a quick fix, a common mistake, or a myth.</p></li><li><p>You're starting a channel from zero and need first viewers. Shorts don't depend on subscribers.</p></li><li><p>You already make long videos. Cutting Shorts from them costs almost no extra time.</p></li><li><p>You sell to a broad consumer audience, where reach matters more than depth.</p></li></ul><h2>When Shorts are a weak fit</h2><ul><li><p>You sell a complex or expensive service, like B2B software or legal work, where buyers need depth before they trust you. Use Shorts only as teasers for long videos.</p></li><li><p>You need leads this month. Shorts viewers scroll fast and rarely click through to a website straight away.</p></li><li><p>You can't post regularly. One Short a month rarely moves anything; the format rewards volume.</p></li><li><p>Your only goal is search traffic. People searching a specific question usually land on long videos.</p></li></ul><h3>What Shorts cost if you advertise with them</h3><p>Shorts ads are among YouTube's cheapest placements, at around $4 per 1,000 impressions in one 2026 benchmark. A paid Shorts view only counts once someone watches 10 seconds or more. That makes Shorts ads a low-cost way to test which hook gets attention before you spend on longer ads.</p><h2>21 Shorts ideas by business type</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Business</th><th>Shorts ideas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dentists and clinics</td><td>One myth busted per video; "what this procedure actually feels like"; a 30-second answer to the most-asked patient question</td></tr><tr><td>Law firms</td><td>"Can I do X without a lawyer?"; one term explained simply; the biggest mistake people make in [case type]</td></tr><tr><td>Trades (plumbing, roofing, HVAC)</td><td>Time-lapse of a job; "don't do this" DIY mistakes; signs you need a repair now</td></tr><tr><td>SaaS</td><td>One feature in 30 seconds; before and after a workflow; a customer's one-line result</td></tr><tr><td>E-commerce</td><td>Unboxing; the product in real use; three ways to use one product</td></tr><tr><td>Coaches and consultants</td><td>One tip per Short; a common client mistake; a quick reaction to an industry myth</td></tr><tr><td>Real estate</td><td>A 60-second property walkthrough; neighborhood in 3 shots; "what $X buys you here"</td></tr></tbody></table><h2>How to make Shorts that bring in business</h2><ol><li><p>Hook in the first second. Open with the result, the problem or a bold claim, not your logo or a greeting.</p></li><li><p>Film vertically and keep text inside the center of the frame, so it isn't covered by buttons.</p></li><li><p>Add captions. Many people watch with the sound off.</p></li><li><p>Link each Short to a related long video so interested viewers have somewhere to go next.</p></li><li><p>Post on a schedule you can keep: three to five a week is a strong target, one a week is still worth it.</p></li><li><p>Check which Shorts bring subscribers and long-video views, not just Shorts views, and make more like those.</p></li></ol><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Do YouTube Shorts help grow a business channel?</h3><p>Yes, mostly by bringing in new viewers and subscribers. They work best when they point people to long videos that do the selling.</p><h3>How long can a YouTube Short be?</h3><p>Up to three minutes, in vertical or square format.</p><h3>How many Shorts should a business post?</h3><p>Three to five a week if you can manage it. If you can't, cut one or two from each long video you publish.</p><h3>Can Shorts get leads directly?</h3><p>Sometimes, but rarely in large numbers. Shorts are better at making people aware of you than at getting them to fill in a form.</p><h3>Should I post the same video as a Short, a Reel and a TikTok?</h3><p>Yes, as long as you remove other platforms' watermarks. Re-using one vertical video across all three is an efficient way to test which platform your customers use.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'youtube-shorts-for-business'
  and body_json::text like '%If you''d rather hand this off, YT Agenci%';

-- how-much-does-youtube-pay-per-view
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube pays most creators about $2,000 to $4,000 for 1 million long-form views, according to vidIQ's 2026 data. An "},{"type":"text","text":"AIR Media-Tech study of 300 channels","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/how-much-does-youtube-really-pay-in-2026-real-rpm-data-from-300-channels"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" found a median of about $2.30 per 1,000 views, or roughly $2,300 per million. Finance and business channels can earn several times that; 1 million Shorts views often pays well under $100."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Does YouTube pay you for views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not directly. YouTube pays you a share of the ad revenue your videos earn, and only once you've joined the YouTube Partner Program. On long-form videos, creators get 55% of the net ad revenue and YouTube keeps 45%. Views matter because more views mean more ads shown, but a view with no ad earns nothing."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The number that matters: RPM"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"RPM (revenue per mille) is what you earn per 1,000 views after YouTube's share, counting every view, including those without ads. You'll find it in YouTube Studio under Analytics, then Revenue."}]},{"type":"codeBlock","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\\text{Earnings} = \\frac{\\text{views}}{1{,}000} \\times \\text{RPM}"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"So 1 million views at a $3 RPM pays $3,000. Don't confuse RPM with CPM, which is what advertisers pay before YouTube's share and only on views that showed an ad."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much YouTube pays for 1,000, 100K and 1 million views"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"These figures apply the earnings formula to a low, typical and high long-form RPM, and to the typical Shorts range."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Views"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-form, low ($1 RPM)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-form, typical ($3 RPM)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Long-form, high ($10 RPM)"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts ($0.03 to $0.10 RPM)"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$3"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$0.03 to $0.10"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"100,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$100"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$300"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$3 to $10"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1,000,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$3,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$10,000"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$30 to $100"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts RPM range from. Some finance and education channels report long-form RPMs well above $10, so the top end can go higher."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why niche matters so much"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Advertisers pay more to reach people who are about to spend a lot of money. That's why a finance channel can earn several times what a gaming channel earns from the same number of views."}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Niche"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Typical earnings per 1 million long-form views"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Music and entertainment"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$1,000 to $2,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lifestyle and gaming"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$2,000 to $5,000"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Finance and business"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"$4,000 to $12,000+"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Other studies put the top niches higher still: AIR Media-Tech measured a median RPM of about $10 for education and science channels and about $2 for gaming. Published ranges disagree a lot, partly because some sources mix up RPM and CPM, so treat any single number with caution."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much YouTube Shorts pay per 1,000 views"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts are paid from a shared revenue pool, not per ad on your video, and they earn far less per view. Most creators see $0.03 to $0.10 per 1,000 Shorts views. In "},{"type":"text","text":"AIR Media-Tech's study of 274 channels","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/youtube-shorts-rpm-vs-long-form-how-much-do-shorts-earn-in-2026"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", Shorts earned 3% to 14% of long-form RPM, so it took 11,000 to 34,000 Shorts views to earn what 1,000 long-form views did."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's why most successful creators use Shorts to find new viewers and long-form videos to earn."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What changes how much you earn per view"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Niche: the biggest factor, as the table above shows."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Audience country: viewers in the US, UK, Canada and Australia usually earn more per view than viewers in lower-income markets."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time of year: RPM usually peaks in the fourth quarter, when advertisers spend year-end budgets, and dips in January."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Video length: videos of 8 minutes or more can carry mid-roll ads, which can raise earnings per view."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Watch time: viewers who watch longer see more ads."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Advertiser-friendly content: videos with limited ads (because of strong language or sensitive topics) earn much less."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to start earning: the Partner Program"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You need to be in the YouTube Partner Program to earn ad revenue. Today the bar is 1,000 subscribers plus 4,000 public watch hours in a year or 10 million Shorts views in 90 days. From February 1, 2027, new applicants will need 8,000 watch hours or 20 million Shorts views instead; channels already in the program keep their status. Also from that date, a channel needs at least 10 million Shorts views in 90 days to keep earning from Shorts."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Ad revenue is usually the smallest income stream"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Many creators earn more from other sources than from ads:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Sponsorships: often $20 to $50 per 1,000 views for an integrated segment, several times what ads pay. See our guide on "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube influencer rates","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/find-youtube-influencers-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Affiliate links: commission on products you recommend."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel memberships, Super Thanks and Super Chat."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Your own products, courses or services."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For businesses, the value of 1 million views is rarely ad revenue at all; it's the leads and customers those views bring in."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does YouTube pay for 1 million views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually $2,000 to $4,000 for long-form videos, with a range of roughly $1,000 to $12,000 or more depending on niche and audience. For Shorts, 1 million views typically pays $30 to $100."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does YouTube pay for 100,000 views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"About $200 to $400 for a typical long-form channel, and up to $1,000 or more in high-paying niches like finance."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much does YouTube pay per view?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roughly $0.001 to $0.005 per long-form view for most creators, or $1 to $5 per 1,000 views."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How much money do you get for 1,000 views on YouTube Shorts?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually $0.03 to $0.10."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do you get paid for views before joining the Partner Program?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. Ad revenue only starts once your channel is accepted into the YouTube Partner Program."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When does YouTube pay creators?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monthly, through AdSense, once your balance reaches the payment threshold."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>YouTube pays most creators about $2,000 to $4,000 for 1 million long-form views, according to vidIQ's 2026 data. An <a href="https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/how-much-does-youtube-really-pay-in-2026-real-rpm-data-from-300-channels">AIR Media-Tech study of 300 channels</a> found a median of about $2.30 per 1,000 views, or roughly $2,300 per million. Finance and business channels can earn several times that; 1 million Shorts views often pays well under $100.</p><h3>Does YouTube pay you for views?</h3><p>Not directly. YouTube pays you a share of the ad revenue your videos earn, and only once you've joined the YouTube Partner Program. On long-form videos, creators get 55% of the net ad revenue and YouTube keeps 45%. Views matter because more views mean more ads shown, but a view with no ad earns nothing.</p><h3>The number that matters: RPM</h3><p>RPM (revenue per mille) is what you earn per 1,000 views after YouTube's share, counting every view, including those without ads. You'll find it in YouTube Studio under Analytics, then Revenue.</p><pre><code>\text{Earnings} = \frac{\text{views}}{1{,}000} \times \text{RPM}</code></pre><p>So 1 million views at a $3 RPM pays $3,000. Don't confuse RPM with CPM, which is what advertisers pay before YouTube's share and only on views that showed an ad.</p><h2>How much YouTube pays for 1,000, 100K and 1 million views</h2><p>These figures apply the earnings formula to a low, typical and high long-form RPM, and to the typical Shorts range.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Views</th><th>Long-form, low ($1 RPM)</th><th>Long-form, typical ($3 RPM)</th><th>Long-form, high ($10 RPM)</th><th>Shorts ($0.03 to $0.10 RPM)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1,000</td><td>$1</td><td>$3</td><td>$10</td><td>$0.03 to $0.10</td></tr><tr><td>100,000</td><td>$100</td><td>$300</td><td>$1,000</td><td>$3 to $10</td></tr><tr><td>1,000,000</td><td>$1,000</td><td>$3,000</td><td>$10,000</td><td>$30 to $100</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Shorts RPM range from. Some finance and education channels report long-form RPMs well above $10, so the top end can go higher.</p><h2>Why niche matters so much</h2><p>Advertisers pay more to reach people who are about to spend a lot of money. That's why a finance channel can earn several times what a gaming channel earns from the same number of views.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Niche</th><th>Typical earnings per 1 million long-form views</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Music and entertainment</td><td>$1,000 to $2,000</td></tr><tr><td>Lifestyle and gaming</td><td>$2,000 to $5,000</td></tr><tr><td>Finance and business</td><td>$4,000 to $12,000+</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Other studies put the top niches higher still: AIR Media-Tech measured a median RPM of about $10 for education and science channels and about $2 for gaming. Published ranges disagree a lot, partly because some sources mix up RPM and CPM, so treat any single number with caution.</p><h2>How much YouTube Shorts pay per 1,000 views</h2><p>Shorts are paid from a shared revenue pool, not per ad on your video, and they earn far less per view. Most creators see $0.03 to $0.10 per 1,000 Shorts views. In <a href="https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/youtube-shorts-rpm-vs-long-form-how-much-do-shorts-earn-in-2026">AIR Media-Tech's study of 274 channels</a>, Shorts earned 3% to 14% of long-form RPM, so it took 11,000 to 34,000 Shorts views to earn what 1,000 long-form views did.</p><p>That's why most successful creators use Shorts to find new viewers and long-form videos to earn.</p><h2>What changes how much you earn per view</h2><ul><li><p>Niche: the biggest factor, as the table above shows.</p></li><li><p>Audience country: viewers in the US, UK, Canada and Australia usually earn more per view than viewers in lower-income markets.</p></li><li><p>Time of year: RPM usually peaks in the fourth quarter, when advertisers spend year-end budgets, and dips in January.</p></li><li><p>Video length: videos of 8 minutes or more can carry mid-roll ads, which can raise earnings per view.</p></li><li><p>Watch time: viewers who watch longer see more ads.</p></li><li><p>Advertiser-friendly content: videos with limited ads (because of strong language or sensitive topics) earn much less.</p></li></ul><h2>How to start earning: the Partner Program</h2><p>You need to be in the YouTube Partner Program to earn ad revenue. Today the bar is 1,000 subscribers plus 4,000 public watch hours in a year or 10 million Shorts views in 90 days. From February 1, 2027, new applicants will need 8,000 watch hours or 20 million Shorts views instead; channels already in the program keep their status. Also from that date, a channel needs at least 10 million Shorts views in 90 days to keep earning from Shorts.</p><h2>Ad revenue is usually the smallest income stream</h2><p>Many creators earn more from other sources than from ads:</p><ul><li><p>Sponsorships: often $20 to $50 per 1,000 views for an integrated segment, several times what ads pay. See our guide on <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/find-youtube-influencers-for-business">YouTube influencer rates</a>.</p></li><li><p>Affiliate links: commission on products you recommend.</p></li><li><p>Channel memberships, Super Thanks and Super Chat.</p></li><li><p>Your own products, courses or services.</p></li></ul><p>For businesses, the value of 1 million views is rarely ad revenue at all; it's the leads and customers those views bring in.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How much does YouTube pay for 1 million views?</h3><p>Usually $2,000 to $4,000 for long-form videos, with a range of roughly $1,000 to $12,000 or more depending on niche and audience. For Shorts, 1 million views typically pays $30 to $100.</p><h3>How much does YouTube pay for 100,000 views?</h3><p>About $200 to $400 for a typical long-form channel, and up to $1,000 or more in high-paying niches like finance.</p><h3>How much does YouTube pay per view?</h3><p>Roughly $0.001 to $0.005 per long-form view for most creators, or $1 to $5 per 1,000 views.</p><h3>How much money do you get for 1,000 views on YouTube Shorts?</h3><p>Usually $0.03 to $0.10.</p><h3>Do you get paid for views before joining the Partner Program?</h3><p>No. Ad revenue only starts once your channel is accepted into the YouTube Partner Program.</p><h3>When does YouTube pay creators?</h3><p>Monthly, through AdSense, once your balance reaches the payment threshold.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-much-does-youtube-pay-per-view'
  and body_json::text not like '%https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/how-much-does-youtube-really-pay-in-2026-real-rpm-data-from-300-channels%';

-- how-to-start-a-youtube-channel-for-kids
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\"A YouTube channel for kids\" can mean two things, and the rules apply to both:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A channel that makes videos for children to watch, such as songs, stories, crafts or learning videos."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A channel your child wants to appear on or help create."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Either way, an adult should own and run the channel. YouTube requires channel owners to meet a minimum age (13 in most countries), and children under that age use YouTube through supervised experiences, which may not allow creating a channel. Any video aimed at children must also be marked as \"made for kids\" in YouTube Studio, which turns off several features and changes how the channel earns."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"The \"made for kids\" rules"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube requires every creator to say whether their content is made for kids, either for the whole channel or video by video. This follows the US "},{"type":"text","text":"Children's Online Privacy Protection Act (COPPA)","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", and YouTube applies the setting to creators worldwide."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What counts as \"made for kids\""}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Following "},{"type":"text","text":"FTC guidance","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2019/11/youtube-channel-owners-your-content-directed-children"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", a video is made for kids if children are its main audience, or if it's directed at children through things like its subject, characters, themes, toys or games, even when children aren't the only audience. If you're unsure, YouTube suggests its own guidance or legal advice. Marking child-directed content as \"not made for kids\" to keep features is a serious compliance risk."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What's turned off on made-for-kids videos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"According to YouTube, these features aren't available on made-for-kids content:"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Turned off"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why it matters for you"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Comments and live chat"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No comment-based community or feedback"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Personalized ads"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Lower earnings; only non-personalized ads run"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Notifications"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Subscribers aren't alerted to new uploads"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cards and end screens"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Harder to send viewers to your next video"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Channel memberships, Super Chat, Super Stickers"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No fan funding"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Merchandise and ticketing"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No merch shelf"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Save to playlist and Watch Later"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Viewers can't save videos for later"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Autoplay on home, Miniplayer, video watermarks"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Less discovery and branding"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"At the channel level, posts and stories are also unavailable on channels set as made for kids."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How to set up a kids' YouTube channel"}]},{"type":"orderedList","attrs":{"start":1},"content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Use an adult's Google account. The parent, teacher or business running the channel should be the owner. Creating the channel under a business name lets you add other adults as managers later; our "},{"type":"text","text":"channel setup guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" walks through it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Set the audience. In YouTube Studio, go to Settings, then Channel, then Advanced settings, and choose whether the whole channel is made for kids. If you'll mix kids' and family content, set it video by video instead."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pick a clear theme. Songs, read-alouds, science experiments, crafts, simple language lessons or calm bedtime content are common formats."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Make it work without comments. Since viewers can't comment, plan your feedback another way, such as a parent email list or a website."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Design for parents too. Parents choose what young children watch, so titles and thumbnails should tell a parent clearly what the video teaches or offers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consider YouTube Kids. Videos that fit YouTube Kids' quality guidelines can appear in the separate YouTube Kids app, where many families watch."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keeping children safe on camera"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If your child appears in videos:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Never share their full name, school, home area or daily routine, and check backgrounds for street signs and house numbers."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep filming in shared family spaces, not bedrooms."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't post anything you wouldn't want them to see online as a teenager. Let older children say no to a video."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Avoid livestreams with young children; YouTube restricts live streaming by younger minors unless an adult is visibly present."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read the channel's analytics and any messages yourself, not your child."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How kids' channels earn"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Made-for-kids channels can still join the YouTube Partner Program and earn from non-personalized ads, but they usually earn much less per view. In "},{"type":"text","text":"AIR Media-Tech's 2026 data","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/how-much-does-youtube-really-pay-in-2026-real-rpm-data-from-300-channels"}}]},{"type":"text","text":", education and science channels had a median RPM over 30 times higher than kids and teens content. Memberships, Super Chat and merch shelves are switched off too."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's why many kids' channels earn more from other sources: licensing, books, music streaming, apps, or simply bringing customers to a related business such as a tutoring service or toy shop. See "},{"type":"text","text":"how much YouTube pays per view","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-much-does-youtube-pay-per-view"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the general picture."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can a child have their own YouTube channel?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Not on their own in most countries. Channel owners must meet YouTube's minimum age, usually 13. A parent can own a channel that features their child."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do I have to mark my channel as made for kids?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, if your content is directed at children. Every creator has to set their audience, and marking child-directed videos wrongly can lead to penalties under COPPA."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can made-for-kids videos make money?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, through non-personalized ads once you're in the YouTube Partner Program, but usually far less per view than other content."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Why are comments turned off on my kids' videos?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"YouTube turns comments off on all made-for-kids content to limit data collection from children, as required by COPPA."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is a kids' channel a good business idea?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It can be, but only with a plan that doesn't rely on ad revenue alone, and with a firm commitment to your child's privacy if they appear on camera."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>"A YouTube channel for kids" can mean two things, and the rules apply to both:</p><ul><li><p>A channel that makes videos for children to watch, such as songs, stories, crafts or learning videos.</p></li><li><p>A channel your child wants to appear on or help create.</p></li></ul><p>Either way, an adult should own and run the channel. YouTube requires channel owners to meet a minimum age (13 in most countries), and children under that age use YouTube through supervised experiences, which may not allow creating a channel. Any video aimed at children must also be marked as "made for kids" in YouTube Studio, which turns off several features and changes how the channel earns.</p><h2>The "made for kids" rules</h2><p>YouTube requires every creator to say whether their content is made for kids, either for the whole channel or video by video. This follows the US <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions">Children's Online Privacy Protection Act (COPPA)</a>, and YouTube applies the setting to creators worldwide.</p><h3>What counts as "made for kids"</h3><p>Following <a href="https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2019/11/youtube-channel-owners-your-content-directed-children">FTC guidance</a>, a video is made for kids if children are its main audience, or if it's directed at children through things like its subject, characters, themes, toys or games, even when children aren't the only audience. If you're unsure, YouTube suggests its own guidance or legal advice. Marking child-directed content as "not made for kids" to keep features is a serious compliance risk.</p><h3>What's turned off on made-for-kids videos</h3><p>According to YouTube, these features aren't available on made-for-kids content:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Turned off</th><th>Why it matters for you</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comments and live chat</td><td>No comment-based community or feedback</td></tr><tr><td>Personalized ads</td><td>Lower earnings; only non-personalized ads run</td></tr><tr><td>Notifications</td><td>Subscribers aren't alerted to new uploads</td></tr><tr><td>Cards and end screens</td><td>Harder to send viewers to your next video</td></tr><tr><td>Channel memberships, Super Chat, Super Stickers</td><td>No fan funding</td></tr><tr><td>Merchandise and ticketing</td><td>No merch shelf</td></tr><tr><td>Save to playlist and Watch Later</td><td>Viewers can't save videos for later</td></tr><tr><td>Autoplay on home, Miniplayer, video watermarks</td><td>Less discovery and branding</td></tr></tbody></table><p>At the channel level, posts and stories are also unavailable on channels set as made for kids.</p><h2>How to set up a kids' YouTube channel</h2><ol><li><p>Use an adult's Google account. The parent, teacher or business running the channel should be the owner. Creating the channel under a business name lets you add other adults as managers later; our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-to-create-a-youtube-channel-for-business">channel setup guide</a> walks through it.</p></li><li><p>Set the audience. In YouTube Studio, go to Settings, then Channel, then Advanced settings, and choose whether the whole channel is made for kids. If you'll mix kids' and family content, set it video by video instead.</p></li><li><p>Pick a clear theme. Songs, read-alouds, science experiments, crafts, simple language lessons or calm bedtime content are common formats.</p></li><li><p>Make it work without comments. Since viewers can't comment, plan your feedback another way, such as a parent email list or a website.</p></li><li><p>Design for parents too. Parents choose what young children watch, so titles and thumbnails should tell a parent clearly what the video teaches or offers.</p></li><li><p>Consider YouTube Kids. Videos that fit YouTube Kids' quality guidelines can appear in the separate YouTube Kids app, where many families watch.</p></li></ol><h2>Keeping children safe on camera</h2><p>If your child appears in videos:</p><ul><li><p>Never share their full name, school, home area or daily routine, and check backgrounds for street signs and house numbers.</p></li><li><p>Keep filming in shared family spaces, not bedrooms.</p></li><li><p>Don't post anything you wouldn't want them to see online as a teenager. Let older children say no to a video.</p></li><li><p>Avoid livestreams with young children; YouTube restricts live streaming by younger minors unless an adult is visibly present.</p></li><li><p>Read the channel's analytics and any messages yourself, not your child.</p></li></ul><h2>How kids' channels earn</h2><p>Made-for-kids channels can still join the YouTube Partner Program and earn from non-personalized ads, but they usually earn much less per view. In <a href="https://air.io/en/air-data-findings/how-much-does-youtube-really-pay-in-2026-real-rpm-data-from-300-channels">AIR Media-Tech's 2026 data</a>, education and science channels had a median RPM over 30 times higher than kids and teens content. Memberships, Super Chat and merch shelves are switched off too.</p><p>That's why many kids' channels earn more from other sources: licensing, books, music streaming, apps, or simply bringing customers to a related business such as a tutoring service or toy shop. See <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/how-much-does-youtube-pay-per-view">how much YouTube pays per view</a> for the general picture.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Can a child have their own YouTube channel?</h3><p>Not on their own in most countries. Channel owners must meet YouTube's minimum age, usually 13. A parent can own a channel that features their child.</p><h3>Do I have to mark my channel as made for kids?</h3><p>Yes, if your content is directed at children. Every creator has to set their audience, and marking child-directed videos wrongly can lead to penalties under COPPA.</p><h3>Can made-for-kids videos make money?</h3><p>Yes, through non-personalized ads once you're in the YouTube Partner Program, but usually far less per view than other content.</p><h3>Why are comments turned off on my kids' videos?</h3><p>YouTube turns comments off on all made-for-kids content to limit data collection from children, as required by COPPA.</p><h3>Is a kids' channel a good business idea?</h3><p>It can be, but only with a plan that doesn't rely on ad revenue alone, and with a firm commitment to your child's privacy if they appear on camera.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-to-start-a-youtube-channel-for-kids'
  and body_json::text not like '%https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions%';

-- advertise-business-free-on-youtube
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You can advertise your business on YouTube for free by publishing videos that answer what your customers search for, then making those videos easy to find, click and act on. You pay with time instead of money."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The catch is speed. Free promotion builds slowly, often over six months or more, while paid ads work from day one. The upside is that free videos keep working after you stop, and ads don't."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11 free ways to promote your business on YouTube"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Answer the exact questions customers search for"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Type your main service into YouTube's search bar and note the autocomplete suggestions. Those are real searches. A video titled with one of them, such as \"How long does a dental implant take to heal?\", can bring in viewers every week without a cent of ad spend."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Make \"cost\" and \"vs\" videos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Price and comparison searches come from people close to buying. \"How much does [service] cost in [city]?\" and \"[Option A] vs [option B]\" are two of the most valuable video types a business can make, and most competitors avoid them."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Write titles, descriptions and chapters for search"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put the search phrase near the start of the title. Summarize the video in the first two lines of the description, and add your website link. Chapters (timestamps in the description) help viewers jump to their answer and can show up in Google results."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Design thumbnails people want to click"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A clear face or object, high contrast, and three words at most. If a video gets impressions but few clicks, change the thumbnail; you can update it any time."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Post Shorts that point to your long videos"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Cut the best 30 to 60 seconds from each long video into a Short. Shorts reach people who don't follow you, and you can link a Short to the full video. Our guide on "},{"type":"text","text":"using YouTube Shorts for business","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" covers what works."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Add a clear next step to every video"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"One call to action near the end: book a call, download a checklist, or watch a related video. Repeat it in the description and a pinned comment. Free promotion only pays off if viewers know what to do next."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Embed videos on your website"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Put each video on the matching page of your site: the service page, the FAQ, the pricing page. It sends website visitors to your channel and gives the video more views."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Link your channel everywhere you already are"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Add it to your Google Business Profile, email signature, social profiles, invoices and quote templates. Send new videos to your email list. These are free placements most businesses forget."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Collaborate with creators and other businesses"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Appear on a creator's podcast, or film a joint video with a business that serves the same customers (a wedding photographer and a florist, say). Each side gets introduced to the other's audience. See our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"finding YouTube influencers for your business","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/find-youtube-influencers-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Use playlists, end screens and community posts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Group videos into playlists by customer question so viewers keep watching. Add end screens linking to your next best video. Use community posts to ask your audience what they want to see next."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"11. Reply to every comment, especially early on"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Answering comments builds trust with the person asking and everyone reading later. Comments are also your best source of new video ideas."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A simple weekly routine (about 4 hours)"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Day"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Task"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Time"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Monday"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Pick one customer question and outline the video"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"30 minutes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tuesday"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Film it on a phone with a clip-on mic"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1 hour"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Wednesday"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Edit, write the title and description, make the thumbnail"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1.5 hours"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Thursday"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Publish, cut one Short, embed the video on your site"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"45 minutes"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Friday"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Reply to comments, email the video to your list"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"15 minutes"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Keep this up for six months and you'll have around 25 videos answering your customers' most common questions. That library is the free advertising."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"When free promotion stops being enough"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Consider paying for help or ads when:"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A video already brings in leads, and you want more people to see it. A small YouTube ads budget behind a proven video is the safest first ad spend."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You've posted consistently for six months and views are still flat. A "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" can show whether the problem is topics, titles or thumbnails."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You don't have four hours a week. A freelance editor or a "},{"type":"text","text":"channel management agency"},{"type":"text","text":" can take most of the routine off your plate."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Our guide to "},{"type":"text","text":"what a YouTube ads agency costs","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" shows what each option costs."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Can you really advertise on YouTube for free?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes. Uploading videos, optimizing them, posting Shorts and community posts all cost nothing. Paid YouTube ads are optional."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long does free YouTube promotion take to work?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Usually three to six months of steady posting before results are clear. Videos that target specific search questions often start getting views sooner."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do I need a lot of subscribers to get customers?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. A business channel with a few hundred subscribers can bring in steady leads if its videos answer what buyers search for. Subscribers matter less than search traffic for most businesses."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What equipment do I need?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A phone, a clip-on microphone and a window or cheap light. Good sound matters more than the camera."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is it worth paying for YouTube ads?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Often, once you have a video that already converts. Ads speed up what's working; they rarely rescue a video that doesn't."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>You can advertise your business on YouTube for free by publishing videos that answer what your customers search for, then making those videos easy to find, click and act on. You pay with time instead of money.</p><p>The catch is speed. Free promotion builds slowly, often over six months or more, while paid ads work from day one. The upside is that free videos keep working after you stop, and ads don't.</p><h2>11 free ways to promote your business on YouTube</h2><h3>1. Answer the exact questions customers search for</h3><p>Type your main service into YouTube's search bar and note the autocomplete suggestions. Those are real searches. A video titled with one of them, such as "How long does a dental implant take to heal?", can bring in viewers every week without a cent of ad spend.</p><h3>2. Make "cost" and "vs" videos</h3><p>Price and comparison searches come from people close to buying. "How much does [service] cost in [city]?" and "[Option A] vs [option B]" are two of the most valuable video types a business can make, and most competitors avoid them.</p><h3>3. Write titles, descriptions and chapters for search</h3><p>Put the search phrase near the start of the title. Summarize the video in the first two lines of the description, and add your website link. Chapters (timestamps in the description) help viewers jump to their answer and can show up in Google results.</p><h3>4. Design thumbnails people want to click</h3><p>A clear face or object, high contrast, and three words at most. If a video gets impressions but few clicks, change the thumbnail; you can update it any time.</p><h3>5. Post Shorts that point to your long videos</h3><p>Cut the best 30 to 60 seconds from each long video into a Short. Shorts reach people who don't follow you, and you can link a Short to the full video. Our guide on <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business">using YouTube Shorts for business</a> covers what works.</p><h3>6. Add a clear next step to every video</h3><p>One call to action near the end: book a call, download a checklist, or watch a related video. Repeat it in the description and a pinned comment. Free promotion only pays off if viewers know what to do next.</p><h3>7. Embed videos on your website</h3><p>Put each video on the matching page of your site: the service page, the FAQ, the pricing page. It sends website visitors to your channel and gives the video more views.</p><h3>8. Link your channel everywhere you already are</h3><p>Add it to your Google Business Profile, email signature, social profiles, invoices and quote templates. Send new videos to your email list. These are free placements most businesses forget.</p><h3>9. Collaborate with creators and other businesses</h3><p>Appear on a creator's podcast, or film a joint video with a business that serves the same customers (a wedding photographer and a florist, say). Each side gets introduced to the other's audience. See our guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/find-youtube-influencers-for-business">finding YouTube influencers for your business</a>.</p><h3>10. Use playlists, end screens and community posts</h3><p>Group videos into playlists by customer question so viewers keep watching. Add end screens linking to your next best video. Use community posts to ask your audience what they want to see next.</p><h3>11. Reply to every comment, especially early on</h3><p>Answering comments builds trust with the person asking and everyone reading later. Comments are also your best source of new video ideas.</p><h2>A simple weekly routine (about 4 hours)</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>Task</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monday</td><td>Pick one customer question and outline the video</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Tuesday</td><td>Film it on a phone with a clip-on mic</td><td>1 hour</td></tr><tr><td>Wednesday</td><td>Edit, write the title and description, make the thumbnail</td><td>1.5 hours</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday</td><td>Publish, cut one Short, embed the video on your site</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr><tr><td>Friday</td><td>Reply to comments, email the video to your list</td><td>15 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Keep this up for six months and you'll have around 25 videos answering your customers' most common questions. That library is the free advertising.</p><h2>When free promotion stops being enough</h2><p>Consider paying for help or ads when:</p><ul><li><p>A video already brings in leads, and you want more people to see it. A small YouTube ads budget behind a proven video is the safest first ad spend.</p></li><li><p>You've posted consistently for six months and views are still flat. A channel audit can show whether the problem is topics, titles or thumbnails.</p></li><li><p>You don't have four hours a week. A freelance editor or a channel management agency can take most of the routine off your plate.</p></li></ul><p>Our guide to <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-ads-agency-cost">what a YouTube ads agency costs</a> shows what each option costs.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>Can you really advertise on YouTube for free?</h3><p>Yes. Uploading videos, optimizing them, posting Shorts and community posts all cost nothing. Paid YouTube ads are optional.</p><h3>How long does free YouTube promotion take to work?</h3><p>Usually three to six months of steady posting before results are clear. Videos that target specific search questions often start getting views sooner.</p><h3>Do I need a lot of subscribers to get customers?</h3><p>No. A business channel with a few hundred subscribers can bring in steady leads if its videos answer what buyers search for. Subscribers matter less than search traffic for most businesses.</p><h3>What equipment do I need?</h3><p>A phone, a clip-on microphone and a window or cheap light. Good sound matters more than the camera.</p><h3>Is it worth paying for YouTube ads?</h3><p>Often, once you have a video that already converts. Ads speed up what's working; they rarely rescue a video that doesn't.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'advertise-business-free-on-youtube'
  and body_json::text like '%tps://ytagencies.com/youtube-channel-aud%';

-- how-to-get-1000-views-on-youtube
update guides set
  body_json = $g${"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"To get your first 1,000 views for free, make videos that answer specific things people already search for, package each one with a clear title and thumbnail, post Shorts to reach new viewers, and share your videos where your audience already gathers. Most channels reach their first 1,000 views across several videos, not one."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Skip anything that sells views. Fake views break YouTube's rules, get removed, and teach the algorithm the wrong audience for your channel."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10 free ways to get your first 1,000 views"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"1. Answer specific searches"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Broad topics are crowded; specific questions are not. Type your topic into YouTube's search bar and use the autocomplete suggestions as video ideas. \"How to clean a cast iron pan after rust\" is easier to rank for than \"cooking tips\". See our "},{"type":"text","text":"YouTube SEO guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-seo"}}]},{"type":"text","text":" for the full process."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"2. Post Shorts"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Shorts are shown to people who don't follow you, which makes them the fastest free way to get views on a new channel. Cut 30 to 60 seconds from each long video into a Short and link it to the full version. More in our "},{"type":"text","text":"Shorts guide","marks":[{"type":"link","attrs":{"href":"https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business"}}]},{"type":"text","text":"."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"3. Make your thumbnail and title a pair"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"When YouTube shows your video and few people click, it tends to show it less. Use one clear subject in the thumbnail, high contrast, and three words at most; let the title add what the image doesn't say."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"4. Hook in the first 10 seconds"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Say what the viewer will get, right away. Cut the long intro and the channel logo. Videos that hold viewers past the opening get shown to more people."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"5. Post consistently on one topic"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A channel about one thing is easier for YouTube to recommend to the right people. Pick a schedule you can keep for three months, even if it's one video every two weeks."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"6. Share where your audience already is"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Post your video in relevant online communities, forums and groups, but only where it genuinely answers a question being asked. Read each community's self-promotion rules first."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"7. Embed videos in your other content"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"If you have a blog, website, newsletter or social profiles, embed or link each video there. Views from embedded videos count toward your total when people press play."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"8. Collaborate with a creator your size"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two small channels making a video together introduce each other to new viewers. Look for creators in a related, not identical, topic."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"9. Reply to every comment"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Early replies make viewers more likely to come back, and comments often suggest your next video idea."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"10. Update videos that get impressions but few clicks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In YouTube Studio, check impressions and click-through rate for each video. If a video is shown a lot but rarely clicked, change the title or thumbnail; you can do this any time."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"What not to do"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't buy views, subscribers or \"promotion packages\". YouTube's fake engagement policy prohibits them, fake views are often removed, and they confuse YouTube about who your real audience is."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't join \"sub for sub\" or view-exchange groups. Viewers who aren't interested leave quickly, which can hurt your videos."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't use misleading titles or thumbnails. Clicks that end in disappointment lead to short watch times, and YouTube shows those videos less."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Don't spam comment sections on bigger channels with your link."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"A 30-day plan for your first 1,000 views"}]},{"type":"table","content":[{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Week"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Focus"}]}]},{"type":"tableHeader","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Tasks"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"1"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Topics"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"List 20 specific questions from YouTube search suggestions; pick the 4 you can answer best"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"First videos"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Publish 2 long videos with tight hooks; cut 2 Shorts from each"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"3"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Distribution"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Share each video in 1 or 2 communities where it answers a real question; embed on your site or newsletter"}]}]}]},{"type":"tableRow","content":[{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"4"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Improve"}]}]},{"type":"tableCell","attrs":{"colspan":1,"rowspan":1},"content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Publish 2 more videos; change thumbnails on any video with low click-through rate; reply to every comment"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"That's 4 long videos and 8 Shorts in a month. Results vary a lot by topic, so treat 1,000 views as a first milestone, not a deadline."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":2},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"FAQ"}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How long does it take to get 1,000 views on YouTube?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For a new channel, anywhere from a few days to a few months. Specific search-based videos and regular Shorts usually get there fastest."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Do Shorts views count toward 1,000 views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, Shorts views count as views on your channel. For the YouTube Partner Program, though, Shorts views and long-form watch hours are counted separately."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Is it safe to buy YouTube views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No. It breaks YouTube's policies, fake views are often removed, and it can put your channel at risk."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Does sharing my video on social media help?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Yes, especially at the start, when your channel has no audience yet. Share in places where the video is genuinely useful."}]},{"type":"heading","attrs":{"level":3},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"How do I know what's stopping my views?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Check YouTube Studio. Low impressions mean a topic or discovery problem; low click-through rate means a title or thumbnail problem; short watch time means a problem with the video itself. If you're stuck, a "},{"type":"text","text":"channel audit"},{"type":"text","text":" can pinpoint which."}]}]}$g$::jsonb,
  body_html = $g$<p>To get your first 1,000 views for free, make videos that answer specific things people already search for, package each one with a clear title and thumbnail, post Shorts to reach new viewers, and share your videos where your audience already gathers. Most channels reach their first 1,000 views across several videos, not one.</p><p>Skip anything that sells views. Fake views break YouTube's rules, get removed, and teach the algorithm the wrong audience for your channel.</p><h2>10 free ways to get your first 1,000 views</h2><h3>1. Answer specific searches</h3><p>Broad topics are crowded; specific questions are not. Type your topic into YouTube's search bar and use the autocomplete suggestions as video ideas. "How to clean a cast iron pan after rust" is easier to rank for than "cooking tips". See our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-seo">YouTube SEO guide</a> for the full process.</p><h3>2. Post Shorts</h3><p>Shorts are shown to people who don't follow you, which makes them the fastest free way to get views on a new channel. Cut 30 to 60 seconds from each long video into a Short and link it to the full version. More in our <a href="https://ytagencies.com/guides/youtube-shorts-for-business">Shorts guide</a>.</p><h3>3. Make your thumbnail and title a pair</h3><p>When YouTube shows your video and few people click, it tends to show it less. Use one clear subject in the thumbnail, high contrast, and three words at most; let the title add what the image doesn't say.</p><h3>4. Hook in the first 10 seconds</h3><p>Say what the viewer will get, right away. Cut the long intro and the channel logo. Videos that hold viewers past the opening get shown to more people.</p><h3>5. Post consistently on one topic</h3><p>A channel about one thing is easier for YouTube to recommend to the right people. Pick a schedule you can keep for three months, even if it's one video every two weeks.</p><h3>6. Share where your audience already is</h3><p>Post your video in relevant online communities, forums and groups, but only where it genuinely answers a question being asked. Read each community's self-promotion rules first.</p><h3>7. Embed videos in your other content</h3><p>If you have a blog, website, newsletter or social profiles, embed or link each video there. Views from embedded videos count toward your total when people press play.</p><h3>8. Collaborate with a creator your size</h3><p>Two small channels making a video together introduce each other to new viewers. Look for creators in a related, not identical, topic.</p><h3>9. Reply to every comment</h3><p>Early replies make viewers more likely to come back, and comments often suggest your next video idea.</p><h3>10. Update videos that get impressions but few clicks</h3><p>In YouTube Studio, check impressions and click-through rate for each video. If a video is shown a lot but rarely clicked, change the title or thumbnail; you can do this any time.</p><h2>What not to do</h2><ul><li><p>Don't buy views, subscribers or "promotion packages". YouTube's fake engagement policy prohibits them, fake views are often removed, and they confuse YouTube about who your real audience is.</p></li><li><p>Don't join "sub for sub" or view-exchange groups. Viewers who aren't interested leave quickly, which can hurt your videos.</p></li><li><p>Don't use misleading titles or thumbnails. Clicks that end in disappointment lead to short watch times, and YouTube shows those videos less.</p></li><li><p>Don't spam comment sections on bigger channels with your link.</p></li></ul><h2>A 30-day plan for your first 1,000 views</h2><table><thead><tr><th>Week</th><th>Focus</th><th>Tasks</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Topics</td><td>List 20 specific questions from YouTube search suggestions; pick the 4 you can answer best</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>First videos</td><td>Publish 2 long videos with tight hooks; cut 2 Shorts from each</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Distribution</td><td>Share each video in 1 or 2 communities where it answers a real question; embed on your site or newsletter</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Improve</td><td>Publish 2 more videos; change thumbnails on any video with low click-through rate; reply to every comment</td></tr></tbody></table><p>That's 4 long videos and 8 Shorts in a month. Results vary a lot by topic, so treat 1,000 views as a first milestone, not a deadline.</p><h2>FAQ</h2><h3>How long does it take to get 1,000 views on YouTube?</h3><p>For a new channel, anywhere from a few days to a few months. Specific search-based videos and regular Shorts usually get there fastest.</p><h3>Do Shorts views count toward 1,000 views?</h3><p>Yes, Shorts views count as views on your channel. For the YouTube Partner Program, though, Shorts views and long-form watch hours are counted separately.</p><h3>Is it safe to buy YouTube views?</h3><p>No. It breaks YouTube's policies, fake views are often removed, and it can put your channel at risk.</p><h3>Does sharing my video on social media help?</h3><p>Yes, especially at the start, when your channel has no audience yet. Share in places where the video is genuinely useful.</p><h3>How do I know what's stopping my views?</h3><p>Check YouTube Studio. Low impressions mean a topic or discovery problem; low click-through rate means a title or thumbnail problem; short watch time means a problem with the video itself. If you're stuck, a channel audit can pinpoint which.</p>$g$,
  content_updated_at = now()
where slug = 'how-to-get-1000-views-on-youtube'
  and body_json::text like '%tps://ytagencies.com/youtube-channel-aud%';

